"[{\"id\":\"6a37ace6-7bb8-83ee-9c3f-2317a2005dc3\",\"title\":\"Threat-Modeling for RL-LLM\",\"create\":1782033639.943944,\"update\":1782033646.754622,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a threat-modeling framework for reproducibility failures in RL-for-LLM papers. List threat categories, mitigations, and how they map to the 7 checklist items.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-769\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\\\usepackage{listings} % for JSON schema listings\\n 31 | \\\\lstset{basicstyle=\\\\small\\\\ttfamily,frame=single,columns=fullflexible,breaklines=true}\\n 32 | \\n 33 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 34 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 35 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 36 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 37 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 38 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 41 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 42 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 43 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\grpoReg}{\\\\textsc{GRPO-Reg}}\\n 44 | \\n 45 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 46 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 47 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 50 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 51 | \\n 52 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 53 | \\n 54 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 55 | \\\\maketitle\\n 56 | \\n 57 | % =============================================================================\\n 58 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 59 | % =============================================================================\\n 60 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 61 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 62 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 63 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 64 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 65 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 66 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 67 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 68 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 69 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 70 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 71 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 72 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 73 | \\n 74 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 75 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 76 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 77 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 78 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 79 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 80 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 81 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 82 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 83 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 84 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 85 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 86 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 87 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 88 | \\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 91 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 92 | % =============================================================================\\n 93 | \\n 94 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 95 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 96 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 97 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 98 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 99 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n100 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n101 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n102 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n103 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n104 | \\n105 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n106 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n107 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n108 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n109 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n110 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n111 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n112 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n113 | \\n114 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n115 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n116 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n117 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n118 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n119 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n120 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n121 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n122 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n123 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n124 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n125 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n126 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n127 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n128 | nothing about the variants.\\n129 | \\n130 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n131 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n132 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n133 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n134 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n135 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n136 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n137 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n138 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n139 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n140 | \\n141 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n142 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n143 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n144 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n145 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n146 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n147 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n148 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n149 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n150 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n151 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n152 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n153 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n154 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n155 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n156 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n157 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n158 | \\n159 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n160 | This paper makes three:\\n161 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n162 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n163 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n164 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n165 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n166 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n167 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n168 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n169 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n170 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n171 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n172 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n173 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n174 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n175 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n176 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n177 | \\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n180 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n181 | % =============================================================================\\n182 | \\n183 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n184 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n185 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n186 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n187 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n188 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n189 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n190 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n191 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n192 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n193 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n194 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n195 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n196 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n197 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n198 | \\n199 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n200 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n201 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n202 | \\\\begin{center}\\n203 | \\\\small\\n204 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n205 | \\\\toprule\\n206 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n207 | \\\\midrule\\n208 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n211 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n212 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n213 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n214 | \\\\midrule\\n215 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n216 | \\\\bottomrule\\n217 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n218 | \\\\end{center}\\n219 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n220 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n221 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n222 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n223 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n224 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n225 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n226 | \\n227 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n228 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n229 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n230 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n231 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n232 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n233 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n234 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n235 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n236 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n237 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n238 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n239 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n240 | \\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n243 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n244 | % =============================================================================\\n245 | \\n246 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n247 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n248 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n249 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n250 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n251 | \\n252 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n253 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n254 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n255 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n256 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n257 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n258 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n259 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n260 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n261 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n262 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n263 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n264 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n265 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n266 | \\n267 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n268 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n269 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n270 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n271 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n272 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n273 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n274 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n275 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n276 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n277 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n278 | \\n279 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n280 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n281 | own \\\\texttt{generate}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n282 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n283 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n284 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n285 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n286 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n287 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n288 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n289 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n290 | \\n291 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n292 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n293 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n294 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n295 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n296 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n297 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n298 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n299 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n300 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n301 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n302 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n303 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n304 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n305 | \\n306 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n307 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n308 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n309 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n310 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n311 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n312 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n313 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n314 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n315 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n316 | \\n317 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n318 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n319 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n320 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n321 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n322 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n323 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n324 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n325 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n326 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n327 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n328 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n329 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n330 | \\n331 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n332 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n333 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n334 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n335 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n336 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n337 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n338 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n339 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n340 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n341 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n342 | \\n343 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n344 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n345 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n346 | \\n347 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n348 | \\\\centering\\n349 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n350 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n351 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n352 | \\\\small\\n353 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n354 | \\\\toprule\\n355 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n356 | \\\\midrule\\n357 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n362 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n363 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n364 | \\\\bottomrule\\n365 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n366 | \\\\end{table}\\n367 | \\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n370 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n371 | % =============================================================================\\n372 | \\n373 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n374 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n375 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n376 | \\n377 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n378 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n379 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n380 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n381 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n382 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n383 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n384 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n385 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n386 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n387 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n388 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n389 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n390 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n391 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n392 | \\n393 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n394 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n395 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n396 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n397 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n398 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n399 | \\n400 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n401 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n402 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n403 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n404 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n405 | \\n406 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n407 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n408 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n409 | \\n410 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n411 | \\\\centering\\n412 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n413 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n414 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n415 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n416 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n417 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n418 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n419 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n420 | \\\\small\\n421 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n422 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n423 | \\\\toprule\\n424 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n425 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n426 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n427 | \\\\midrule\\n428 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n429 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n431 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n432 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n433 | \\\\bottomrule\\n434 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n435 | \\\\end{table}\\n436 | \\n437 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n438 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n439 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n440 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n441 | \\n442 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n443 | \\\\centering\\n444 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n445 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n446 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n447 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n448 | \\\\small\\n449 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n450 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n451 | \\\\toprule\\n452 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n453 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n454 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n455 | \\\\midrule\\n456 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n457 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n459 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n460 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n461 | \\\\bottomrule\\n462 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n463 | \\\\end{table}\\n464 | \\n465 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n466 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n467 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n468 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n469 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n470 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n471 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n472 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n473 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n474 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n475 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n476 | \\n477 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n478 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n479 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n480 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n481 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n482 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n483 | the contribution.\\n484 | \\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n487 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n488 | % =============================================================================\\n489 | \\n490 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n491 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n492 | trainers log it by default}.\\n493 | \\n494 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n495 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer. The\\n496 | marginal work is to write them down once, in a stable schema, and to compute\\n497 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} from the grouped reward tensor that GRPO already materializes.\\n498 | \\n499 | \\\\subparagraph{A concrete TRL plugin: \\\\texttt{trl-min-report-rl}.}\\n500 | We sketch a small TRL extension that makes adoption automatic. For every\\n501 | \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer.train()} call it emits a run-start JSON block containing the\\n502 | seven \\\\minreport{} fields, then logs per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} from the actual\\n503 | grouped rewards used by GRPO. The package shape is:\\n504 | \\\\begin{verbatim}\\n505 | trl_min_report_rl/\\n506 |   __init__.py\\n507 |   config.py              # MinReportRLConfig dataclass + CLI flags\\n508 |   callback.py            # run-start manifest + finalization\\n509 |   grpo_mixin.py          # reward-tensor hook\\n510 |   emitter.py             # JSONL + stdout + logger adapters\\n511 |   schema/\\n512 |     grpo_run_start.v0.1.json\\n513 |     grpo_step.v0.1.json\\n514 | \\\\end{verbatim}\\n515 | The public API is a single extra argument to \\\\texttt{GRPOConfig}:\\n516 | \\\\begin{verbatim}\\n517 | args = GRPOConfig(..., report_min_report_rl=True)\\n518 | trainer = MinReportGRPOTrainer(\\n519 |     model=model, args=args, reward_funcs=[reward_fn],\\n520 |     train_dataset=train_ds, eval_dataset=heldout_ds,\\n521 |     processing_class=tokenizer, min_report_rl=MinReportRLConfig(...))\\n522 | \\\\end{verbatim}\\n523 | A no-subclass adapter, \\\\texttt{enable\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl(trainer, config)}, is\\n524 | provided for compatibility with existing code.\\n525 | \\n526 | The critical hook is \\\\texttt{on\\\\_grpo\\\\_rewards\\\\_computed}, called after reward\\n527 | functions return and after distributed gather, but before advantage\\n528 | normalization. It receives \\\\texttt{rewards\\\\_per\\\\_func}, \\\\texttt{reward\\\\_weights},\\n529 | and \\\\texttt{num\\\\_generations}, and computes\\n530 | \\\\[\\n531 | \\\\text{ZVF}_t = \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_{x=1}^{N}\\n532 |   \\\\mathbf{1}\\\\big[\\\\operatorname{Var}(r_{x,1},\\\\dots,r_{x,G})=0\\\\big],\\n533 | \\\\qquad\\n534 | \\\\text{GU}_t = 1 - \\\\text{ZVF}_t,\\n535 | \\\\]\\n536 | where unusable (all-NaN) groups are counted as zero-variance. The hook logs both\\n537 | human-readable scalars (\\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl/zvf},\\n538 | \\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl/gu}, \\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl/usable\\\\_groups}) and a\\n539 | per-step JSON event.\\n540 | \\n541 | \\\\subparagraph{Upstream surface.}\\n542 | The cleanest upstream TRL change adds three elements to \\\\texttt{GRPOConfig}:\\n543 | \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl}, \\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl\\\\_strict}, and\\n544 | \\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl\\\\_output\\\\_dir}; plus two trainer methods\\n545 | (\\\\texttt{\\\\_emit\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl\\\\_run\\\\_start} and\\n546 | \\\\texttt{\\\\_emit\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl\\\\_step}) and the reward-path hook call. That\\n547 | is the ``few lines of telemetry'' version of the standard.\\n548 | \\n549 | \\\\subparagraph{Run-start manifest.}\\n550 | The first line of \\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl.jsonl} is a JSON object whose\\n551 | \\\\texttt{min\\\\_reportable\\\\_stack} field contains the seven items. A fragment is\\n552 | shown in Listing~\\\\ref{lst:runstart}; the full schema is in the\\n553 | \\\\grpoReg{} catalog paper \\\\cite{grpoRegistry2026}.\\n554 | \\n555 | \\\\begin{lstlisting}[language=json, caption={Fragment of the\\n556 |   \\\\texttt{min\\\\_report\\\\_rl} run-start manifest.}, label={lst:runstart}]\\n557 | {\\n558 |   \\\"event\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.run_start\\\",\\n559 |   \\\"min_reportable_stack\\\": {\\n560 |     \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\\"status\\\":\\\"known\\\", \\\"loss_type\\\":\\\"dapo\\\", ...},\\n561 |     \\\"2_reference_policy_kl\\\": {\\\"status\\\":\\\"known\\\", \\\"kl\\\": {\\\"enabled\\\":false}},\\n562 |     \\\"3_sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\\"rollout_engine\\\":\\\"vllm\\\", ...},\\n563 |     \\\"4_zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\\"enabled\\\":true, \\\"basis\\\":\\\"weighted_reward\\\", ...},\\n564 |     \\\"5_group_size_schedule\\\": {\\\"train_num_generations\\\":8, ...},\\n565 |     \\\"6_heldout_split\\\": {\\\"heldout_dataset_id\\\":\\\"gsm8k:test:500\\\", ...},\\n566 |     \\\"7_decontamination_probes\\\": {\\\"ngram_overlap\\\":{\\\"passed\\\":true}, ...}\\n567 |   }\\n568 | }\\n569 | \\\\end{lstlisting}\\n570 | \\n571 | \\\\subparagraph{Other trainers.}\\n572 | verl's actor/rollout separation makes sampler, precision, and group-size\\n573 | schedule first-class; OpenRLHF's reference-model and KL options map directly\\n574 | onto item~2. Both can emit the same manifest block and per-step telemetry using\\n575 | the same shared emitter. The schema is trainer-agnostic; only the source of each\\n576 | field changes.\\n577 | \\n578 | \\\\paragraph{Enforcement and audit tooling.}\\n579 | The same manifests feed three tools. \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}\\n580 | (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:tooling}) compares two manifests and flags whether their nuisance\\n581 | differences are large enough to flip a comparison. The \\\\texttt{MIN-REPORT-RL\\n582 | Auditor} scores an existing paper or repo against the seven-item checklist,\\n583 | assigns a 0--100 reproducibility-auditable badge, and produces a reviewer-facing\\n584 | summary \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The \\\\texttt{MRRL-AuditRunner} turns the controlled\\n585 | single-stack audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} into a pre-registered executable\\n586 | pipeline: it locks the shared stack, expands each variant as a declared hook,\\n587 | and computes survival verdicts from paired seed-level held-out deltas\\n588 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Together these tools turn the abstract standard into a\\n589 | mechanical check that venues and reviewers can run in CI.\\n590 | \\n591 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n592 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n593 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n594 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n595 | appendix.\\n596 | \\n597 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n598 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n599 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n600 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n601 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n602 | \\n603 | % =============================================================================\\n604 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n605 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n606 | % =============================================================================\\n607 | \\n608 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n609 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n610 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n611 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n612 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n613 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n614 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n615 | \\n616 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n617 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n618 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n619 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n620 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n621 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n622 | justify it the same way.}\\n623 | \\n624 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n625 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n626 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n627 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n628 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n629 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n630 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n631 | \\n632 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n633 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n634 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n635 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n636 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n637 | algorithm.\\n638 | \\n639 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n640 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n641 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n642 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n643 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n644 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n645 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n646 | reported.\\n647 | \\n648 | % =============================================================================\\n649 | \\\\section{Companion Tooling: \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}}\\n650 | \\\\label{sec:tooling}\\n651 | % =============================================================================\\n652 | \\n653 | A standard is easier to adopt if reviewers can check it mechanically. We sketch\\n654 | a companion CLI, \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}, that consumes two \\\\minreport{}\\n655 | run manifests and reports whether their differences are large enough to flip a\\n656 | comparison. The tool is not a replacement for the audit of\\n657 | \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}; it is an enforcement layer that tells a reviewer, in\\n658 | seconds, whether a head-to-head is stack-comparable.\\n659 | \\n660 | \\\\paragraph{Manifest contract.} Each run is a YAML/JSON block containing the\\n661 | seven \\\\minreport{} fields, plus run identity (trainer, version, seed, hardware),\\n662 | model and tokenizer hashes, LoRA configuration, optimizer settings, and results.\\n663 | The schema is the JSON block proposed in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}.\\n664 | \\n665 | \\\\paragraph{Diff taxonomy.} Every field difference receives three labels:\\n666 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n667 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Lever.} One of eight lever groups: L0~run identity/provenance,\\n668 |     L1~loss form, L2~reference policy and KL handling, L3~sampler/backend/\\n669 |     precision, L4~usable-signal telemetry (\\\\zvf/\\\\gu{}), L5~group-size\\n670 |     schedule, L6~held-out evaluation and checkpoint selection, L7~decontamination\\n671 |     and parser robustness.\\n672 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Diff kind.} EQUAL, COSMETIC, PROVENANCE, PARAMETRIC, SCHEDULE,\\n673 |     SEMANTIC\\\\_OBJECTIVE, DISTRIBUTIONAL, TELEMETRY, MISSING, OPAQUE, DERIVED, or\\n674 |     INVALID\\\\_TARGET.\\n675 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Role.} TREATMENT\\\\_DELTA if the user declares it as the\\n676 |     algorithmic delta being tested; NUISANCE\\\\_DELTA if it is an uncontrolled\\n677 |     stack difference; COVERAGE\\\\_GAP if the field is missing; INVALIDATOR if the\\n678 |     comparison target is not common.\\n679 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n680 | \\n681 | \\\\paragraph{Flip-risk classes.} The tool compares each nuisance difference to\\n682 | the reported comparison margin and classifies the overall risk as one of:\\n683 | \\\\begin{center}\\n684 | \\\\small\\n685 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\\n686 | \\\\toprule\\n687 | \\\\textbf{Class} & \\\\textbf{Meaning}\\\\\\\\\\n688 | \\\\midrule\\n689 | R0 \\\\texttt{same} & No meaningful difference.\\\\\\\\\\n690 | R1 \\\\texttt{cosmetic} & Version string or comment only.\\\\\\\\\\n691 | R2 \\\\texttt{small} & Estimated effect $<25\\\\%$ of the comparison margin.\\\\\\\\\\n692 | R3 \\\\texttt{material} & Effect $25$--$100\\\\%$ of the margin; can shrink a claim.\\\\\\\\\\n693 | R4 \\\\texttt{flip\\\\_capable} & Effect bound $\\\\\\text{\\\\textgreater=}$ margin; can flip the ranking.\\\\\\\\\\n694 | R5 \\\\texttt{invalidating} & The comparison target is not common.\\\\\\\\\\n695 | RU \\\\texttt{unknown} & Missing or opaque evidence; comparison unverifiable.\\\\\\\\\\n696 | \\\\bottomrule\\n697 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n698 | \\\\end{center}\\n699 | \\n700 | \\\\paragraph{Verdict and CI integration.} The tool emits a deterministic verdict\\n701 | (\\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATCHED}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATERIAL}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_CONFOUNDED},\\n702 | \\\\texttt{UNVERIFIABLE}, or \\\\texttt{INVALID\\\\_COMPARISON}) and an exit code, so a\\n703 | paper's reproducibility workflow can fail a comparison that is\\n704 | flip-capable. Example:\\n705 | \\\\begin{verbatim}\\n706 | grpo-stackdiff compare dapo.yaml grpo.yaml \\\\\\n707 |   --metric heldout.exact_match --claim-delta +0.024 \\\\\\n708 |   --fail-on flip-capable\\n709 | \\\\end{verbatim}\\n710 | \\n711 | \\\\paragraph{Role in the \\\\minreport{} ecosystem.} Authors emit the JSON block;\\n712 | venues ask for it; reviewers run \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}. The cost to an author\\n713 | is still a few lines of telemetry; the cost to a reviewer is one command. The\\n714 | tool turns the abstract stack-conditioning thesis of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack} into a\\n715 | concrete, reproducible check.\\n716 | \\n717 | % =============================================================================\\n718 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n719 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n720 | % =============================================================================\\n721 | \\n722 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n723 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n724 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n725 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n726 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n727 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n728 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n729 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n730 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n731 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n732 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n733 | can actually trust.\\n734 | \\n735 | % =============================================================================\\n736 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n737 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n738 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n739 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n740 | %\\n741 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n742 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n743 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n744 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n745 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n746 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n747 | % =============================================================================\\n748 | \\\\section*{References}\\n749 | \\\\small\\n750 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n751 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n752 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n753 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n754 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n755 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n756 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n757 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n758 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n759 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n760 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n761 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n762 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n763 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n764 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n765 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n766 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n767 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n768 | \\n769 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37aa2b-92ec-83ee-af6d-28135a16b99d\",\"title\":\"Scorecard Attribution Method\",\"create\":1782032941.764731,\"update\":1782033375.337304,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a scorecard that attributes an observed head-to-head delta between two GRPO variants to stack differences vs. algorithmic differences. Define the attribution method, required measurements, and confidence language.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-692\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | The companion \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff} tool described in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:tooling}\\n512 | then consumes these manifests to flag confounded comparisons automatically.\\n513 | \\n514 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n515 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n516 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n517 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n518 | appendix.\\n519 | \\n520 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n521 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n522 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n523 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n524 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n525 | \\n526 | % =============================================================================\\n527 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n528 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n529 | % =============================================================================\\n530 | \\n531 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n532 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n533 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n534 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n535 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n536 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n537 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n538 | \\n539 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n540 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n541 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n542 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n543 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n544 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n545 | justify it the same way.}\\n546 | \\n547 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n548 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n549 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n550 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n551 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n552 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n553 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n554 | \\n555 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n556 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n557 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n558 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n559 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n560 | algorithm.\\n561 | \\n562 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n563 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n564 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n565 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n566 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n567 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n568 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n569 | reported.\\n570 | \\n571 | % =============================================================================\\n572 | \\\\section{Companion Tooling: \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}}\\n573 | \\\\label{sec:tooling}\\n574 | % =============================================================================\\n575 | \\n576 | A standard is easier to adopt if reviewers can check it mechanically. We sketch\\n577 | a companion CLI, \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}, that consumes two \\\\minreport{}\\n578 | run manifests and reports whether their differences are large enough to flip a\\n579 | comparison. The tool is not a replacement for the audit of\\n580 | \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}; it is an enforcement layer that tells a reviewer, in\\n581 | seconds, whether a head-to-head is stack-comparable.\\n582 | \\n583 | \\\\paragraph{Manifest contract.} Each run is a YAML/JSON block containing the\\n584 | seven \\\\minreport{} fields, plus run identity (trainer, version, seed, hardware),\\n585 | model and tokenizer hashes, LoRA configuration, optimizer settings, and results.\\n586 | The schema is the JSON block proposed in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}.\\n587 | \\n588 | \\\\paragraph{Diff taxonomy.} Every field difference receives three labels:\\n589 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n590 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Lever.} One of eight lever groups: L0~run identity/provenance,\\n591 |     L1~loss form, L2~reference policy and KL handling, L3~sampler/backend/\\n592 |     precision, L4~usable-signal telemetry (\\\\zvf/\\\\gu{}), L5~group-size\\n593 |     schedule, L6~held-out evaluation and checkpoint selection, L7~decontamination\\n594 |     and parser robustness.\\n595 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Diff kind.} EQUAL, COSMETIC, PROVENANCE, PARAMETRIC, SCHEDULE,\\n596 |     SEMANTIC\\\\_OBJECTIVE, DISTRIBUTIONAL, TELEMETRY, MISSING, OPAQUE, DERIVED, or\\n597 |     INVALID\\\\_TARGET.\\n598 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Role.} TREATMENT\\\\_DELTA if the user declares it as the\\n599 |     algorithmic delta being tested; NUISANCE\\\\_DELTA if it is an uncontrolled\\n600 |     stack difference; COVERAGE\\\\_GAP if the field is missing; INVALIDATOR if the\\n601 |     comparison target is not common.\\n602 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n603 | \\n604 | \\\\paragraph{Flip-risk classes.} The tool compares each nuisance difference to\\n605 | the reported comparison margin and classifies the overall risk as one of:\\n606 | \\\\begin{center}\\n607 | \\\\small\\n608 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\\n609 | \\\\toprule\\n610 | \\\\textbf{Class} & \\\\textbf{Meaning}\\\\\\\\\\n611 | \\\\midrule\\n612 | R0 \\\\texttt{same} & No meaningful difference.\\\\\\\\\\n613 | R1 \\\\texttt{cosmetic} & Version string or comment only.\\\\\\\\\\n614 | R2 \\\\texttt{small} & Estimated effect $<25\\\\%$ of the comparison margin.\\\\\\\\\\n615 | R3 \\\\texttt{material} & Effect $25$--$100\\\\%$ of the margin; can shrink a claim.\\\\\\\\\\n616 | R4 \\\\texttt{flip\\\\_capable} & Effect bound $\\\\\\text{\\\\textgreater=}$ margin; can flip the ranking.\\\\\\\\\\n617 | R5 \\\\texttt{invalidating} & The comparison target is not common.\\\\\\\\\\n618 | RU \\\\texttt{unknown} & Missing or opaque evidence; comparison unverifiable.\\\\\\\\\\n619 | \\\\bottomrule\\n620 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n621 | \\\\end{center}\\n622 | \\n623 | \\\\paragraph{Verdict and CI integration.} The tool emits a deterministic verdict\\n624 | (\\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATCHED}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATERIAL}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_CONFOUNDED},\\n625 | \\\\texttt{UNVERIFIABLE}, or \\\\texttt{INVALID\\\\_COMPARISON}) and an exit code, so a\\n626 | paper's reproducibility workflow can fail a comparison that is\\n627 | flip-capable. Example:\\n628 | \\\\begin{verbatim}\\n629 | grpo-stackdiff compare dapo.yaml grpo.yaml \\\\\\n630 |   --metric heldout.exact_match --claim-delta +0.024 \\\\\\n631 |   --fail-on flip-capable\\n632 | \\\\end{verbatim}\\n633 | \\n634 | \\\\paragraph{Role in the \\\\minreport{} ecosystem.} Authors emit the JSON block;\\n635 | venues ask for it; reviewers run \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}. The cost to an author\\n636 | is still a few lines of telemetry; the cost to a reviewer is one command. The\\n637 | tool turns the abstract stack-conditioning thesis of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack} into a\\n638 | concrete, reproducible check.\\n639 | \\n640 | % =============================================================================\\n641 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n642 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n643 | % =============================================================================\\n644 | \\n645 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n646 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n647 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n648 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n649 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n650 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n651 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n652 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n653 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n654 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n655 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n656 | can actually trust.\\n657 | \\n658 | % =============================================================================\\n659 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n660 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n661 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n662 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n663 | %\\n664 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n665 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n666 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n667 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n668 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n669 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n670 | % =============================================================================\\n671 | \\\\section*{References}\\n672 | \\\\small\\n673 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n674 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n675 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n676 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n677 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n678 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n679 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n680 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n681 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n682 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n683 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n684 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n685 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n686 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n687 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n688 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n689 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n690 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n691 | \\n692 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"Here’s a drop-in scorecard I’d add as a new appendix or as the formal backend for `grpo-stackdiff`.\\n\\nThe core idea is: do **not** ask “did variant A beat variant B?” Ask “how much of the observed A-on-stack-\\\\(S_A\\\\) vs B-on-stack-\\\\(S_B\\\\) delta survives when the stack is neutralized?” That matches the paper’s thesis that GRPO-family rankings are full-stack comparisons, not algorithm-label comparisons; the paper’s motivating example is exactly a matched visible GRPO config whose backend swap yielded 84.4% vs. 5.0% last-10 reward, with the omitted stack fields doing the causal work. fileciteturn0file0 fileciteturn1file14\\n\\n## GRPO Delta Attribution Scorecard, GDAS\\n\\n**Purpose.** Given an observed head-to-head delta between two GRPO variants, GDAS outputs:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\n=\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{algorithm}}\\n+\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{stack}}\\n\\\\quad\\n\\\\text{plus an interaction/unknown warning when the evidence cannot separate them.}\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe scorecard has three products:\\n\\n| Output | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Algorithmic attribution** | The part of the delta that survives when both variants are run in the same stack. |\\n| **Stack attribution** | The part explained by backend, sampler, KL/reference handling, loss-mask differences, LoRA/optimizer, reward parser, evaluation harness, or other nuisance stack differences. |\\n| **Confidence language** | A constrained verbal claim: algorithm-dominant, stack-dominant, mixed, reversed, unverifiable, or invalid. |\\n\\nUse **percentage-point deltas**, not ratios, for reward/accuracy metrics. A 5 pp held-out gain and a 17× training-reward ratio are different evidence types.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Estimand\\n\\nLet:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nY(v,s)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nbe the mean performance of variant \\\\(v\\\\) in stack \\\\(s\\\\), measured on the pre-registered primary metric, preferably held-out exact match or another held-out task metric.\\n\\nLet the published or observed comparison be:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\n=\\nY(A,S_A)-Y(B,S_B).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is the diagonal comparison: variant A in its own stack against variant B in its own stack.\\n\\nGDAS defines the **algorithmic effect** as the effect of changing the declared GRPO variant while holding the stack fixed:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{alg}}(s)\\n=\\nY(A,s)-Y(B,s).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt defines the **stack effect** as the effect of changing the stack while holding the algorithm fixed:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{stack}}(v)\\n=\\nY(v,S_A)-Y(v,S_B).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe paper already gestures at this structure through its controlled audit proposal: report the published delta on each variant’s own stack, then report the controlled delta in one shared stack, with a survival verdict. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Evidence tiers\\n\\nThe scorecard’s first field is the **identification tier**. This determines the maximum strength of the attribution language.\\n\\n| Tier | Evidence available | What GDAS may say |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **T0: Manifest-only** | Only run manifests and published numbers. | “Stack-comparable,” “stack-material,” “stack-confounded,” or “unverifiable.” No numeric algorithmic attribution. |\\n| **T1: Single-stack survival** | Both variants re-run in one pre-registered shared stack \\\\(S^\\\\*\\\\). | “The claim survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses in a common stack.” Residual is **stack-associated**, not pure stack. |\\n| **T2: Crossed bridge** | Four cells: \\\\(A,S_A\\\\), \\\\(A,S_B\\\\), \\\\(B,S_A\\\\), \\\\(B,S_B\\\\). | Quantitative decomposition into algorithm main effect and stack main effect. |\\n| **T3: Crossed bridge + lever ablations** | T2 plus one-at-a-time or Shapley-style swaps of sampler, KL, loss mask, parser, etc. | Attribution to specific stack levers, not just “stack.” |\\n| **T4: Multi-stack replication** | T3 repeated across models, datasets, and backends. | Strongest general claim: “algorithmic effect is robust across tested stacks.” |\\n\\nA single-seed comparison is always **descriptive only**. The paper’s statistical protocol says the independent unit is the seed, not the per-step reward trace, and recommends seed-level uncertainty, bootstrap 95% CIs, and multiple-test control over pre-registered survival tests. fileciteturn1file10\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Attribution method\\n\\n### 3.1 Gold-standard crossed bridge\\n\\nRun the four-cell bridge:\\n\\n|  | Stack \\\\(S_A\\\\) | Stack \\\\(S_B\\\\) |\\n|---|---:|---:|\\n| Variant A | \\\\(Y(A,S_A)\\\\) | \\\\(Y(A,S_B)\\\\) |\\n| Variant B | \\\\(Y(B,S_A)\\\\) | \\\\(Y(B,S_B)\\\\) |\\n\\nThen compute:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{alg}}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{1}{2}\\n\\\\left[\\nY(A,S_A)-Y(B,S_A)\\n+\\nY(A,S_B)-Y(B,S_B)\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is the average same-stack algorithmic advantage.\\n\\nCompute:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{stack}}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{1}{2}\\n\\\\left[\\nY(A,S_A)-Y(A,S_B)\\n+\\nY(B,S_A)-Y(B,S_B)\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis is the average advantage of stack \\\\(S_A\\\\) over stack \\\\(S_B\\\\), averaged across the two variants.\\n\\nThese add exactly to the observed diagonal comparison:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\n=\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{alg}}\\n+\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{stack}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nAlso compute the algorithm-by-stack instability:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat{I}_{A\\\\times S}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{1}{2}\\n\\\\left[\\nY(A,S_A)-Y(B,S_A)\\n-\\n\\\\bigl(Y(A,S_B)-Y(B,S_B)\\\\bigr)\\n\\\\right].\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThis term is not added to the diagonal decomposition; it is a **fragility warning**. If it is large, the algorithmic effect is stack-dependent, even if the average algorithmic main effect is nonzero.\\n\\nReport all three with bootstrap 95% CIs across seeds.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3.2 Single-stack survival estimate\\n\\nWhen the full crossed bridge is too expensive, run both variants in one shared pre-registered stack \\\\(S^\\\\*\\\\):\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{survive}}\\n=\\nY(A,S^\\\\*)-Y(B,S^\\\\*).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{residual}}\\n=\\n\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\n-\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{survive}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nInterpretation:\\n\\n| Quantity | Interpretation |\\n|---|---|\\n| \\\\(\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{survive}}\\\\) | Same-stack algorithmic survival estimate. |\\n| \\\\(\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{residual}}\\\\) | Stack-associated residual: stack + algorithm-by-stack interaction + publication-stack effects. |\\n\\nDo **not** call the residual “pure stack” unless the crossed bridge or lever ablations were run.\\n\\nSurvival fraction:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nf_{\\\\text{survive}}\\n=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{survive}}}{\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nUse signed fractions, but report absolute magnitudes when signs conflict.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3.3 Manifest-only stack-risk attribution\\n\\nWhen no bridge run exists, GDAS gives no causal attribution. It only reports whether the observed delta is **stack-comparable**.\\n\\nUse the paper’s lever taxonomy: run identity/provenance, loss form, reference/KL, sampler/backend/precision, usable-signal telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out evaluation/checkpoint selection, and decontamination/parser robustness. The paper’s `grpo-stackdiff` section already classifies differences as treatment deltas, nuisance deltas, coverage gaps, or invalidators, and grades risk from same/cosmetic through material, flip-capable, invalidating, or unknown. fileciteturn1file1\\n\\nFor each nuisance difference \\\\(j\\\\), assign an effect bound \\\\(b_j\\\\):\\n\\n| Risk class | Effect-bound rule |\\n|---|---|\\n| R0 same | \\\\(b_j=0\\\\). |\\n| R1 cosmetic | \\\\(b_j=0\\\\). |\\n| R2 small | \\\\(b_j < 0.25|\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}|\\\\). |\\n| R3 material | \\\\(0.25|\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}| \\\\le b_j < |\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}|\\\\). |\\n| R4 flip-capable | \\\\(b_j \\\\ge |\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}|\\\\). |\\n| R5 invalidating | Target is not common; stop. |\\n| RU unknown | Field missing/opaque; attribution unavailable. |\\n\\nConservative stack explainability bound:\\n\\n\\\\[\\nB_{\\\\text{stack}}\\n=\\n\\\\sum_j b_j.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIf \\\\(B_{\\\\text{stack}}\\\\ge |\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}|\\\\), the stack differences are sufficient to explain or flip the observed claim, so the comparison cannot support an algorithmic conclusion without bridge runs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Required measurements\\n\\nGDAS requires the paper’s Min-Report-RL manifest, plus raw seed-level results. The seven-item checklist is the natural base: loss form; reference/KL; sampler/backend/precision; per-step ZVF/GU; group-size schedule; held-out split; and decontamination/parser probes. The paper explicitly justifies each as a flip lever. fileciteturn1file7\\n\\n| Lever | Required measurement | Attribution use |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **L0 provenance** | Trainer/library and version, hardware, seed set, base checkpoint hash, tokenizer hash, chat template, LoRA targets/rank, optimizer settings, checkpoint-selection rule. | Invalidates or constrains the comparison target. Different base model/tokenizer/evaluator usually means no clean algorithmic claim. |\\n| **L1 loss form** | Ratio definition, old-policy handling, clipping bounds, completion-only vs whole-sequence mask, advantage normalization. | Semantic objective changes are high-risk stack or treatment differences. Count as algorithmic only if explicitly declared as the variant delta. |\\n| **L2 reference/KL** | Frozen reference present/absent, KL in loss vs reward, coefficient/schedule, forward/reverse, token/sequence KL. | Separates actual objective differences from “same GRPO” labels. |\\n| **L3 sampler/backend/precision** | vLLM/SGLang/API/trainer generate, decoding params, logit precision, max tokens, tokenizer/template parity. | Measures rollout-distribution differences; high-risk because sampler changes usable groups. |\\n| **L4 usable-signal telemetry** | Per-step ZVF, GU \\\\(=1-\\\\text{ZVF}\\\\), reward variance substitute if ZVF is not appropriate, collapse rate, time-to-collapse. | Mechanistic attribution: a “better algorithm” may merely have more usable gradient signal. The paper’s telemetry table is designed for exactly this distinction. fileciteturn1file10 |\\n| **L5 group-size schedule** | \\\\(G\\\\) per step, adaptive rule, compute budget, total sampled completions/tokens. | Separates algorithmic adaptive-\\\\(G\\\\) claims from extra compute or easier mixed-group formation. |\\n| **L6 held-out evaluation** | Disjoint held-out set, evaluator/harness, sample size, confidence interval, primary metric, checkpoint-selection rule. | Prevents training reward or reward-harness selection from masquerading as capability. The paper states training reward is not capability evidence. fileciteturn1file13 |\\n| **L7 decontamination/parser probes** | Train/test overlap probe, parser adversarial tests, format-only and length-only controls. | Detects reward hacking, overlap, or parser artifacts. |\\n\\nAdditional raw data required:\\n\\n| Data | Why |\\n|---|---|\\n| Per-seed held-out scores | Unit for inference. |\\n| Per-prompt paired held-out scores | Enables paired bootstrap or randomization tests. |\\n| Per-step online reward | Dynamics diagnostic only. |\\n| Per-step ZVF/GU or reward variance | Mechanistic stack-vs-algorithm evidence. |\\n| Per-step KL, entropy, completion length, parse success | Helps explain whether gains come from exploration, formatting, or length. |\\n| Rollout counts and token counts | Needed when adaptive sampling or dynamic group sizing changes compute. |\\n| Full manifests for both original and controlled runs | Required for attribution and `grpo-stackdiff` comparison. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Scorecard fields\\n\\nEach head-to-head comparison gets one GDAS card.\\n\\n| Field | Fill-in rule |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Claim** | “Variant A beats variant B by \\\\(\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\\\) on metric \\\\(M\\\\).” |\\n| **Primary metric** | Held-out metric, not online reward unless explicitly scoped as training dynamics. |\\n| **Observed delta** | \\\\(Y(A,S_A)-Y(B,S_B)\\\\), with 95% CI. |\\n| **Evidence tier** | T0–T4. |\\n| **Algorithm estimate** | \\\\(\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{alg}}\\\\) from crossed bridge, or \\\\(\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{survive}}\\\\) from shared stack. |\\n| **Stack estimate** | \\\\(\\\\widehat{\\\\Delta}_{\\\\text{stack}}\\\\) from crossed bridge; otherwise residual is “stack-associated.” |\\n| **Interaction warning** | \\\\(\\\\widehat{I}_{A\\\\times S}\\\\), with CI. |\\n| **Stack-risk bound** | \\\\(B_{\\\\text{stack}}/|\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}|\\\\). |\\n| **Telemetry alignment** | “Matched,” “winner has more usable signal,” “collapse/saturation explains delta,” or “unmeasured.” |\\n| **Validity gates** | Held-out disjoint? Decontamination passed? Parser robust? |\\n| **Statistical gates** | Seed count, bootstrap CI, MDE/SESOI, FDR correction if multiple variants. |\\n| **Verdict** | Algorithm-dominant, stack-dominant, mixed, disappears, reverses, unverifiable, or invalid. |\\n| **Allowed wording** | The strongest sentence the evidence permits. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Verdict rules\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\delta_{\\\\min}\\\\) be the pre-registered smallest effect size of interest, for example 1 pp held-out exact match or the audit’s MDE.\\n\\n| Verdict | Rule | Allowed wording |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Algorithm-dominant** | Same-stack or crossed \\\\(\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{alg}}\\\\) has same sign as \\\\(\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\\\), is at least 75% of \\\\(\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{obs}}\\\\), CI excludes 0 and exceeds \\\\(\\\\delta_{\\\\min}\\\\); stack component is below 25% or CI includes 0; interaction low. | “Most of the observed delta is attributable to the declared algorithmic difference under the tested stacks.” |\\n| **Algorithm-led mixed** | Algorithmic component is material and same sign, but stack component is also material. | “The gain survives, but the published magnitude is partly stack-conditioned.” |\\n| **Stack-led mixed** | Stack component is material and larger than algorithmic component; algorithm still nonzero. | “The comparison contains a surviving algorithmic effect, but most of the observed published delta is stack-associated.” |\\n| **Stack-dominated** | Algorithmic component is <25% of observed delta or not distinguishable from 0; stack component or stack-risk bound can explain the margin. | “The observed head-to-head is better explained as a stack difference than as an algorithmic improvement.” |\\n| **Disappears** | Shared-stack delta is within \\\\(\\\\pm\\\\delta_{\\\\min}\\\\) or CI includes 0 and point estimate is small. | “The published gain does not survive stack control.” |\\n| **Reverses** | Shared-stack or crossed algorithmic estimate has the opposite sign and exceeds \\\\(\\\\delta_{\\\\min}\\\\). | “The ranking reverses under stack control.” |\\n| **Unverifiable** | Missing/opaque L1–L4 or L6 fields, closed backend with unknown loss/KL, no usable-signal telemetry, no seed-level data, or manifest-only with R4/RU differences. | “The comparison is between A-on-\\\\(S_A\\\\) and B-on-\\\\(S_B\\\\); no algorithmic attribution is warranted.” |\\n| **Invalid** | Different target model/task/evaluator, contaminated held-out set, non-comparable metric, or invalid checkpoint selection. | “This is not a valid head-to-head for the stated claim.” |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Confidence language\\n\\nUse confidence as a cap, not decoration.\\n\\n| Confidence | Requirements | Phrase template |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **High confidence** | T2 or T3; complete manifests; held-out disjoint; decontamination/parser probes pass; seed-level bootstrap CIs; interaction low or explicitly reported. | “With high confidence, we attribute \\\\(x\\\\) pp \\\\([CI]\\\\) of the \\\\(d\\\\) pp observed delta to the algorithm and \\\\(s\\\\) pp \\\\([CI]\\\\) to stack differences under the tested stacks.” |\\n| **Moderate confidence** | T1 shared-stack survival; complete manifests; held-out valid; enough seeds; no crossed bridge. | “The claim partially/fully survives in a shared stack. The remaining delta is stack-associated, but stack and interaction are not separately identified.” |\\n| **Low confidence** | T0 manifest-only, incomplete bridge, wide CIs, weak seed count, or telemetry gaps. | “The observed delta is compatible with an algorithmic effect, but stack differences are material; the result should be scoped as an implementation comparison.” |\\n| **Descriptive only** | Single seed, training reward only, or no seed-level uncertainty. | “This run is descriptive and should not be used for inferential attribution.” |\\n| **Unverifiable** | Missing/opaque high-leverage fields or closed stack with unknown objective. | “No attribution is possible from the reported evidence.” |\\n\\nAvoid wording like “A beats B” unless the verdict is algorithm-dominant or algorithm-led mixed. Prefer “A-on-\\\\(S_A\\\\) beats B-on-\\\\(S_B\\\\)” for uncontrolled comparisons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Example report template\\n\\n```text\\nGDAS Attribution Card\\n\\nClaim:\\n  Variant A beats Variant B on held-out exact match.\\n\\nObserved comparison:\\n  A on S_A: ___\\n  B on S_B: ___\\n  Δ_obs: ___ pp [95% CI ___, ___]\\n\\nEvidence tier:\\n  T0 / T1 / T2 / T3 / T4\\n\\nControlled estimates:\\n  Δ_alg or Δ_survive: ___ pp [95% CI ___, ___]\\n  Δ_stack: ___ pp [95% CI ___, ___]\\n  Stack-associated residual: ___ pp\\n  A×S interaction: ___ pp [95% CI ___, ___]\\n\\nManifest risk:\\n  L0 provenance: R__\\n  L1 loss form: R__\\n  L2 reference/KL: R__\\n  L3 sampler/backend/precision: R__\\n  L4 usable-signal telemetry: R__\\n  L5 group-size schedule: R__\\n  L6 held-out/checkpoint/evaluator: R__\\n  L7 decontamination/parser: R__\\n  Stack-risk bound B_stack / |Δ_obs|: ___\\n\\nTelemetry:\\n  Mean ZVF/GU trajectories matched? yes/no\\n  Collapse rate difference: ___\\n  Winner has more usable signal because of stack? yes/no/unknown\\n\\nVerdict:\\n  Algorithm-dominant / algorithm-led mixed / stack-led mixed /\\n  stack-dominated / disappears / reverses / unverifiable / invalid\\n\\nAllowed claim:\\n  “______________________________________________”\\n```\\n\\nThe important discipline is that GDAS separates **survival** from **attribution**. A shared-stack run can show that a gain survives, shrinks, disappears, or reverses. A crossed bridge can decompose the observed diagonal delta into algorithm and stack main effects. Lever ablations are needed to say *which* stack difference caused the stack component.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37a9b3-a2b0-83e8-8e59-f5addc5ddbcd\",\"title\":\"Claim Extraction Bot\",\"create\":1782032821.348373,\"update\":1782033170.317404,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a bot that scans GRPO-family papers, extracts every claim tied to a specific stack lever, and checks whether that lever is actually reported in the cited paper. Explain the claim-extraction model and the report format.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-692\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | The companion \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff} tool described in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:tooling}\\n512 | then consumes these manifests to flag confounded comparisons automatically.\\n513 | \\n514 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n515 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n516 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n517 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n518 | appendix.\\n519 | \\n520 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n521 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n522 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n523 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n524 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n525 | \\n526 | % =============================================================================\\n527 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n528 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n529 | % =============================================================================\\n530 | \\n531 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n532 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n533 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n534 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n535 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n536 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n537 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n538 | \\n539 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n540 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n541 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n542 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n543 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n544 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n545 | justify it the same way.}\\n546 | \\n547 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n548 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n549 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n550 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n551 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n552 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n553 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n554 | \\n555 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n556 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n557 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n558 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n559 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n560 | algorithm.\\n561 | \\n562 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n563 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n564 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n565 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n566 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n567 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n568 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n569 | reported.\\n570 | \\n571 | % =============================================================================\\n572 | \\\\section{Companion Tooling: \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}}\\n573 | \\\\label{sec:tooling}\\n574 | % =============================================================================\\n575 | \\n576 | A standard is easier to adopt if reviewers can check it mechanically. We sketch\\n577 | a companion CLI, \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}, that consumes two \\\\minreport{}\\n578 | run manifests and reports whether their differences are large enough to flip a\\n579 | comparison. The tool is not a replacement for the audit of\\n580 | \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}; it is an enforcement layer that tells a reviewer, in\\n581 | seconds, whether a head-to-head is stack-comparable.\\n582 | \\n583 | \\\\paragraph{Manifest contract.} Each run is a YAML/JSON block containing the\\n584 | seven \\\\minreport{} fields, plus run identity (trainer, version, seed, hardware),\\n585 | model and tokenizer hashes, LoRA configuration, optimizer settings, and results.\\n586 | The schema is the JSON block proposed in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}.\\n587 | \\n588 | \\\\paragraph{Diff taxonomy.} Every field difference receives three labels:\\n589 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n590 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Lever.} One of eight lever groups: L0~run identity/provenance,\\n591 |     L1~loss form, L2~reference policy and KL handling, L3~sampler/backend/\\n592 |     precision, L4~usable-signal telemetry (\\\\zvf/\\\\gu{}), L5~group-size\\n593 |     schedule, L6~held-out evaluation and checkpoint selection, L7~decontamination\\n594 |     and parser robustness.\\n595 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Diff kind.} EQUAL, COSMETIC, PROVENANCE, PARAMETRIC, SCHEDULE,\\n596 |     SEMANTIC\\\\_OBJECTIVE, DISTRIBUTIONAL, TELEMETRY, MISSING, OPAQUE, DERIVED, or\\n597 |     INVALID\\\\_TARGET.\\n598 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Role.} TREATMENT\\\\_DELTA if the user declares it as the\\n599 |     algorithmic delta being tested; NUISANCE\\\\_DELTA if it is an uncontrolled\\n600 |     stack difference; COVERAGE\\\\_GAP if the field is missing; INVALIDATOR if the\\n601 |     comparison target is not common.\\n602 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n603 | \\n604 | \\\\paragraph{Flip-risk classes.} The tool compares each nuisance difference to\\n605 | the reported comparison margin and classifies the overall risk as one of:\\n606 | \\\\begin{center}\\n607 | \\\\small\\n608 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\\n609 | \\\\toprule\\n610 | \\\\textbf{Class} & \\\\textbf{Meaning}\\\\\\\\\\n611 | \\\\midrule\\n612 | R0 \\\\texttt{same} & No meaningful difference.\\\\\\\\\\n613 | R1 \\\\texttt{cosmetic} & Version string or comment only.\\\\\\\\\\n614 | R2 \\\\texttt{small} & Estimated effect $<25\\\\%$ of the comparison margin.\\\\\\\\\\n615 | R3 \\\\texttt{material} & Effect $25$--$100\\\\%$ of the margin; can shrink a claim.\\\\\\\\\\n616 | R4 \\\\texttt{flip\\\\_capable} & Effect bound $\\\\\\text{\\\\textgreater=}$ margin; can flip the ranking.\\\\\\\\\\n617 | R5 \\\\texttt{invalidating} & The comparison target is not common.\\\\\\\\\\n618 | RU \\\\texttt{unknown} & Missing or opaque evidence; comparison unverifiable.\\\\\\\\\\n619 | \\\\bottomrule\\n620 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n621 | \\\\end{center}\\n622 | \\n623 | \\\\paragraph{Verdict and CI integration.} The tool emits a deterministic verdict\\n624 | (\\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATCHED}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATERIAL}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_CONFOUNDED},\\n625 | \\\\texttt{UNVERIFIABLE}, or \\\\texttt{INVALID\\\\_COMPARISON}) and an exit code, so a\\n626 | paper's reproducibility workflow can fail a comparison that is\\n627 | flip-capable. Example:\\n628 | \\\\begin{verbatim}\\n629 | grpo-stackdiff compare dapo.yaml grpo.yaml \\\\\\n630 |   --metric heldout.exact_match --claim-delta +0.024 \\\\\\n631 |   --fail-on flip-capable\\n632 | \\\\end{verbatim}\\n633 | \\n634 | \\\\paragraph{Role in the \\\\minreport{} ecosystem.} Authors emit the JSON block;\\n635 | venues ask for it; reviewers run \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}. The cost to an author\\n636 | is still a few lines of telemetry; the cost to a reviewer is one command. The\\n637 | tool turns the abstract stack-conditioning thesis of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack} into a\\n638 | concrete, reproducible check.\\n639 | \\n640 | % =============================================================================\\n641 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n642 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n643 | % =============================================================================\\n644 | \\n645 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n646 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n647 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n648 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n649 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n650 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n651 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n652 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n653 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n654 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n655 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n656 | can actually trust.\\n657 | \\n658 | % =============================================================================\\n659 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n660 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n661 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n662 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n663 | %\\n664 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n665 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n666 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n667 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n668 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n669 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n670 | % =============================================================================\\n671 | \\\\section*{References}\\n672 | \\\\small\\n673 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n674 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n675 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n676 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n677 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n678 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n679 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n680 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n681 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n682 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n683 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n684 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n685 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n686 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n687 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n688 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n689 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n690 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n691 | \\n692 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Bot: **LeverTrace**\\n\\n**LeverTrace** is a citation-audit bot for GRPO-family papers. It reads a paper, finds every atomic claim that depends on a stack lever, follows the citation attached to that claim, and checks whether the cited paper actually reports the lever detail needed to support the claim.\\n\\nThe motivation comes directly from the attached Min-Report-RL paper: it argues that “GRPO” is not a complete experimental description, because runs can differ in loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, group-size schedule, reward parser, held-out evaluation, and telemetry while still wearing the same algorithm label. The paper’s core point is that algorithm rankings are really rankings over full stacks, not labels alone. fileciteturn1file1 fileciteturn1file11\\n\\nLeverTrace is therefore an upstream companion to the paper’s proposed `grpo-stackdiff`: `grpo-stackdiff` compares manifests; LeverTrace builds the claim/evidence ledger that tells reviewers whether the cited papers contain enough information to build those manifests. The paper already defines a useful downstream taxonomy: lever, diff kind, role, flip-risk class, and verdict. LeverTrace reuses that vocabulary but applies it to claims and citations rather than only to two completed run manifests. fileciteturn1file0\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. What LeverTrace scans for\\n\\nLeverTrace extracts only **lever-bound claims**: claims whose truth, attribution, or interpretability depends on one of the Min-Report-RL stack fields.\\n\\nThe lever ontology is:\\n\\n| Lever | What LeverTrace looks for | Required reporting evidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **L0: Provenance** | trainer, version, seed, hardware, model/tokenizer hashes, optimizer, LoRA config | explicit run identity or linked artifact |\\n| **L1: Loss form** | PPO ratio, clipping, clip bounds, asymmetric clip-higher, token mask, advantage normalization, length normalization | formula, objective description, mask definition, normalization rule |\\n| **L2: Reference policy / KL** | frozen reference, absent reference, KL in loss vs reward, KL coefficient, schedule, forward/reverse KL, token/sequence KL | explicit KL/reference description |\\n| **L3: Sampler / backend / precision** | vLLM/SGLang/API/generate, temperature, top-p, max tokens, bf16/fp16/fp32, tokenizer/chat template sharing | sampler config and precision/tokenizer details |\\n| **L4: Usable-signal telemetry** | ZVF, GU, reward variance, gradient-signal proxy, collapse rule | per-step trajectory or substitute metric |\\n| **L5: Group-size schedule** | fixed G, adaptive G, dynamic sampling, rollout allocation, AERO-style rules | per-step G or adaptive rule |\\n| **L6: Held-out evaluation** | disjoint held-out split, evaluator, sample size, confidence interval, checkpoint-selection rule | held-out protocol separate from training reward |\\n| **L7: Decontamination / parser robustness** | train/test overlap, n-gram or embedding checks, adversarial format-only parser tests, reward parser behavior | probe results and parser tests |\\n\\nThis matches the attached paper’s seven mandatory stack fields, where each field is included because it can flip a comparison: loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, ZVF/GU telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination/parser probes. fileciteturn1file8 fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Claim-extraction model\\n\\nLeverTrace uses a **recall-first, schema-constrained claim model**. The model does not simply summarize papers. It converts text into atomic audit records.\\n\\n### Claim tuple\\n\\nEvery extracted claim is represented as:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"C-0042\\\",\\n  \\\"claim_text\\\": \\\"DAPO improves GRPO by using clip-higher and dynamic sampling.\\\",\\n  \\\"source_location\\\": {\\n    \\\"paper\\\": \\\"paper_under_review.pdf\\\",\\n    \\\"page\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"section\\\": \\\"Method\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"claim_type\\\": [\\n    \\\"method_attribution\\\",\\n    \\\"comparison_mechanism\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"subject\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\",\\n  \\\"baseline_or_target\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"metric_or_outcome\\\": null,\\n  \\\"lever_tags\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L1.loss_form\\\",\\n      \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"clip_bounds\\\",\\n      \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"ratio_formula\\\", \\\"clip_low\\\", \\\"clip_high\\\", \\\"mask\\\", \\\"advantage_norm\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L5.group_size_schedule\\\",\\n      \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n      \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"group_size_rule\\\", \\\"sampling_rule\\\", \\\"compute_accounting\\\"]\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"citations\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"key\\\": \\\"dapo2025\\\",\\n      \\\"anchor_text\\\": \\\"[cite: dapo2025]\\\",\\n      \\\"citation_scope\\\": \\\"same_sentence\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"polarity\\\": \\\"positive\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.91\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Claim types\\n\\nLeverTrace separates claims into several types because they require different verification standards:\\n\\n| Claim type | Example | What must be checked |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Method-attribution claim** | “GSPO uses a sequence-level IS ratio.” | Does the cited GSPO paper report that ratio and its formula? |\\n| **Implementation claim** | “We use vLLM with bf16 rollouts.” | Does the paper report engine, precision, decoding parameters, tokenizer/template? |\\n| **Comparison claim** | “Method A beats GRPO by 2.4 points.” | Are both stacks reported well enough to compare? |\\n| **Mechanism claim** | “The gain comes from dynamic sampling.” | Is the lever reported and isolated from nuisance stack deltas? |\\n| **Telemetry claim** | “The method avoids collapse.” | Are ZVF/GU or substitute signal metrics reported per step? |\\n| **Capability claim** | “The method improves GSM8K accuracy.” | Is there a held-out split, harness, sample size, CI, and checkpoint-selection rule? |\\n| **Integrity claim** | “The evaluation is uncontaminated.” | Is there a train/test overlap probe and parser robustness test? |\\n\\nThe paper itself gives the reason for treating capability claims specially: online training reward is not capability evidence, and a variant can show a training-reward gain that vanishes on held-out evaluation. fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Extraction pipeline\\n\\nLeverTrace runs in five stages.\\n\\n### Stage A — Document normalization\\n\\nThe bot ingests PDFs, LaTeX, appendices, supplementary files, and linked code/configs. It preserves:\\n\\n```text\\nsection boundaries\\nparagraphs\\nequations\\ntables\\nfigure captions\\nbibliography entries\\ninline citation anchors\\nappendix/hyperparameter tables\\n```\\n\\nTables matter because stack levers are often reported in small experimental-setting tables rather than prose.\\n\\n### Stage B — Candidate claim detection\\n\\nA high-recall detector marks candidate spans using three signals:\\n\\n1. **Citation neighborhoods**: sentences or paragraphs near citations such as “DAPO,” “GSPO,” “Dr.GRPO,” “AERO,” or “GRPO baseline.”\\n2. **Lever lexicon triggers**: words like `clip`, `ratio`, `KL`, `reference`, `vLLM`, `top-p`, `temperature`, `G=`, `group size`, `held-out`, `contamination`, `ZVF`, `reward parser`.\\n3. **Result/attribution syntax**: phrases like “improves over,” “outperforms,” “we attribute,” “uses,” “changes,” “removes,” “normalizes,” “reports,” “does not report.”\\n\\nThe detector is intentionally over-inclusive. False positives are cheaper than missed claims.\\n\\n### Stage C — Atomic claim splitting\\n\\nA sentence like:\\n\\n> “DAPO improves GRPO through clip-higher, dynamic sampling, and better held-out accuracy.”\\n\\nbecomes three separate records:\\n\\n```text\\nC1: DAPO uses clip-higher.                 → L1\\nC2: DAPO uses dynamic sampling.            → L5\\nC3: DAPO improves held-out accuracy.       → L6\\n```\\n\\nThis matters because one citation may support the loss-form claim but not the held-out-evaluation claim.\\n\\n### Stage D — Lever tagging and slot generation\\n\\nA multi-label classifier maps each atomic claim to one or more levers. Then a deterministic slot generator expands the lever into required evidence.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L2.reference_policy_kl\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The baseline is KL-regularized GRPO.\\\",\\n  \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\n    \\\"frozen_reference_policy\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_location_loss_or_reward\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_coefficient\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_schedule\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_estimator\\\",\\n    \\\"per_token_or_per_sequence\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis strictness follows the Min-Report-RL standard: “KL-regularized” is not enough unless the paper reports where KL enters the objective, how it is estimated, and how it is scheduled. fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n### Stage E — Citation resolution\\n\\nThe citation resolver assigns each claim to a target paper or artifact:\\n\\n```text\\nsame-sentence citation     → high confidence\\nsame-paragraph citation    → medium confidence\\nsection-level citation     → low confidence\\nno citation                → MISSING_CITATION\\nTODO placeholder citation  → TARGET_UNRESOLVED\\n```\\n\\nFor the attached paper itself, many references are intentionally TODO stubs, so LeverTrace would not pretend those citations are verified. It would mark claims tied to `dapo2025`, `gspo2025`, `drgrpo2025`, and similar placeholder keys as `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real bibliography entries are supplied. The paper explicitly says the bibliography is a manual TODO stub that should not ship as-is. fileciteturn1file12\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Evidence-checking model\\n\\nOnce a claim is extracted, LeverTrace asks a narrower question:\\n\\n> “Does the cited paper actually report the lever detail needed to support this claim?”\\n\\nIt does **not** ask:\\n\\n> “Is the algorithm good?”  \\n> “Is the result real?”  \\n> “Would the gain survive reimplementation?”\\n\\nThat survival question belongs to the controlled audit proposed in the paper. LeverTrace only audits claim-to-report coverage.\\n\\n### Evidence tuple\\n\\nFor each claim, the evidence checker produces:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"E-0042-a\\\",\\n  \\\"target_paper\\\": \\\"dapo2025.pdf\\\",\\n  \\\"target_location\\\": {\\n    \\\"page\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"section\\\": \\\"Algorithm\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"evidence_text\\\": \\\"The objective clips the upper ratio bound at ...\\\",\\n  \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formula\\\",\\n  \\\"matched_slots\\\": [\\\"ratio_formula\\\", \\\"clip_high\\\"],\\n  \\\"missing_slots\\\": [\\\"token_mask\\\", \\\"advantage_norm\\\"],\\n  \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.84\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Verification verdicts\\n\\n| Verdict | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| **SUPPORTED_REPORTED** | The cited paper explicitly reports all required lever slots. |\\n| **SUPPORTED_PARTIAL** | The cited paper reports the lever but omits mandatory details. |\\n| **UNREPORTED** | The cited paper does not report the lever. |\\n| **CONTRADICTED** | The cited paper reports a different value or mechanism than the claim states. |\\n| **TARGET_UNRESOLVED** | The citation cannot be resolved, e.g. TODO key, missing bibliography, dead link. |\\n| **OPAQUE** | The cited paper points to a closed backend or artifact whose lever cannot be inspected. |\\n| **AMBIGUOUS_SCOPE** | The claim has multiple citations or a paragraph-level citation and the support cannot be uniquely assigned. |\\n| **NOT_A_LEVER_CLAIM** | The candidate was extracted but does not depend on a stack lever. |\\n\\nFor closed or managed backends, the bot does not infer hidden details. It marks the field `OPAQUE`, consistent with the paper’s point that closed runners should be scoped as a platform implementation rather than treated as fully specified “GRPO.” fileciteturn1file2\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Risk scoring\\n\\nLeverTrace reuses the attached paper’s `grpo-stackdiff` risk vocabulary, but applies it at the claim level.\\n\\n| Risk | Claim-level meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| **R0 same** | The cited paper reports the lever exactly as claimed. |\\n| **R1 cosmetic** | Only naming/version/cosmetic detail differs. |\\n| **R2 small** | Missing detail is unlikely to affect the claim’s interpretation. |\\n| **R3 material** | Missing or partial lever report could shrink the claim. |\\n| **R4 flip_capable** | Missing/contradicted lever is capable of flipping the comparison. |\\n| **R5 invalidating** | The comparison target is not common or the citation target is wrong. |\\n| **RU unknown** | Evidence is missing, unresolved, or opaque. |\\n\\nThe paper’s own downstream taxonomy includes these risk classes and verdicts for stack comparisons; LeverTrace uses them to prioritize reviewer attention. fileciteturn1file0\\n\\nA missing **L1 loss-form** detail in a claimed 2–5 point GRPO-family improvement is usually high risk, because the paper argues that many GRPO variants are small deltas on the base loop and that unreported stack effects can exceed the claimed method effect. fileciteturn1file6\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Report format\\n\\nLeverTrace emits two reports:\\n\\n1. a **human-readable audit report** for reviewers;\\n2. a **machine-readable JSON report** for CI, reproducibility checklists, and `grpo-stackdiff`.\\n\\n### Human-readable report\\n\\n```markdown\\n# LeverTrace Citation Audit Report\\n\\n## 1. Executive summary\\n\\nSource paper: <paper title>\\nCorpus scanned: <N source papers, M cited targets>\\nClaims extracted: <count>\\nLever-bound claims: <count>\\nFully supported: <count>\\nPartially supported: <count>\\nUnreported: <count>\\nContradicted: <count>\\nUnresolved / opaque: <count>\\n\\nOverall verdict: UNVERIFIABLE | STACK_CLAIMS_PARTIAL | STACK_CLAIMS_REPORTED\\n\\nHighest-risk findings:\\n- C-0042: DAPO clip-higher claim cites dapo2025, but citation target is unresolved. Risk: RU.\\n- C-0051: Baseline KL claim lacks KL coefficient and placement in cited paper. Risk: R4.\\n- C-0068: Held-out improvement claim reports training reward only. Risk: R3/R4.\\n\\n## 2. Coverage by lever\\n\\n| Lever | Claims | Supported | Partial | Unreported | Contradicted | Opaque/Unresolved |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| L1 Loss form | 12 | 5 | 4 | 1 | 0 | 2 |\\n| L2 Reference/KL | 8 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 |\\n| L3 Sampler/backend/precision | 9 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\\n| L4 ZVF/GU telemetry | 3 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 0 |\\n| L5 Group-size schedule | 6 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\\n| L6 Held-out evaluation | 10 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 |\\n| L7 Decontamination/parser | 4 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 0 |\\n\\n## 3. Claim ledger\\n\\n| ID | Source claim | Lever | Citation target | Required evidence | Evidence found | Verdict | Risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-0042 | “DAPO uses clip-higher + dynamic sampling.” | L1, L5 | dapo2025 | clip bounds; dynamic sampling rule | citation unresolved | TARGET_UNRESOLVED | RU |\\n| C-0051 | “The baseline is KL-regularized GRPO.” | L2 | baseline paper | frozen ref; KL placement; beta schedule | KL mentioned, beta omitted | SUPPORTED_PARTIAL | R4 |\\n| C-0068 | “The method improves held-out GSM8K.” | L6 | same paper | disjoint split; harness; n; CI | accuracy table only | SUPPORTED_PARTIAL | R3 |\\n| C-0074 | “No contamination affects the benchmark.” | L7 | appendix | overlap probe; parser adversarial test | no probe found | UNREPORTED | R4 |\\n\\n## 4. Per-cited-paper report cards\\n\\n### dapo2025\\nStatus: TARGET_UNRESOLVED\\nClaims depending on this target: C-0042, C-0043, C-0044\\nAction: replace TODO citation with real paper and rerun.\\n\\n### gspo2025\\nStatus: PARTIAL\\nReported levers: L1 sequence-level ratio\\nMissing levers: sampler precision, held-out CI\\n\\n## 5. Reviewer action list\\n\\n1. Require real bibliography entries for unresolved targets.\\n2. Ask authors to report missing L1/L2/L3 fields before accepting any algorithmic-gain claim.\\n3. Treat L6 capability claims as training-dynamics claims unless held-out disjointness, harness, n, CI, and checkpoint rule are reported.\\n4. Queue R4/R5 findings for human review.\\n```\\n\\n### Machine-readable JSON\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"levertrace_version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\",\\n  \\\"source_paper\\\": {\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Example GRPO-family paper\\\",\\n    \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"abc123\\\",\\n    \\\"parsed_from\\\": [\\\"pdf\\\", \\\"latex\\\", \\\"appendix\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"claims_total\\\": 87,\\n    \\\"lever_bound_claims\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"supported_reported\\\": 18,\\n    \\\"supported_partial\\\": 11,\\n    \\\"unreported\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"contradicted\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"target_unresolved\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"opaque\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"overall_verdict\\\": \\\"STACK_CLAIMS_PARTIAL\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"C-0042\\\",\\n      \\\"claim_text\\\": \\\"DAPO uses clip-higher and dynamic sampling.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_location\\\": {\\n        \\\"page\\\": 6,\\n        \\\"section\\\": \\\"Related Work\\\",\\n        \\\"char_start\\\": 18420,\\n        \\\"char_end\\\": 18473\\n      },\\n      \\\"claim_type\\\": [\\\"method_attribution\\\"],\\n      \\\"subject\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\",\\n      \\\"lever_tags\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L1.loss_form\\\",\\n          \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"clip_higher\\\",\\n          \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"ratio_formula\\\", \\\"clip_low\\\", \\\"clip_high\\\", \\\"token_mask\\\", \\\"advantage_norm\\\"]\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L5.group_size_schedule\\\",\\n          \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n          \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"sampling_rule\\\", \\\"per_step_group_size\\\", \\\"compute_accounting\\\"]\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"citations\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"key\\\": \\\"dapo2025\\\",\\n          \\\"resolved\\\": false,\\n          \\\"reason\\\": \\\"bibliography_entry_is_todo_stub\\\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"evidence_checks\\\": [],\\n      \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"TARGET_UNRESOLVED\\\",\\n      \\\"risk\\\": \\\"RU\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": {\\n        \\\"claim_extraction\\\": 0.94,\\n        \\\"lever_mapping\\\": 0.89,\\n        \\\"citation_resolution\\\": 0.22,\\n        \\\"evidence_verification\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Resolve citation target and rerun evidence check.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. How LeverTrace would treat the attached paper\\n\\nFor the attached paper, LeverTrace would extract claims such as:\\n\\n| Claim in attached paper | Lever | Citation key | Likely LeverTrace result |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| “DAPO uses clip-higher + dynamic sampling.” | L1, L5 | `dapo2025` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real DAPO citation is supplied |\\n| “GSPO changes the importance-sampling granularity.” | L1 | `gspo2025` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real GSPO citation is supplied |\\n| “Dr.GRPO changes length/normalization in the loss.” | L1 | `drgrpo2025` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real Dr.GRPO citation is supplied |\\n| “AERO is adaptive-G.” | L5 | `aero2024` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real AERO citation is supplied |\\n| “ZVF/GU reveals whether the run had usable learning signal.” | L4 | `zvfaudit2026` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` because the v1 audit citation is still a TODO |\\n| “The proposed audit holds sampler, KL, LoRA, optimizer, parser, group size, held-out slice, harness, and seeds fixed.” | L1–L7 | same paper | `SUPPORTED_REPORTED` as a proposed protocol, not as completed evidence |\\n\\nThat is not a criticism of the draft; the draft explicitly labels these references as placeholders. It is exactly the kind of issue LeverTrace is designed to surface before submission.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. One-sentence product definition\\n\\n**LeverTrace is a GRPO-family citation auditor that turns prose claims into lever-tagged, citation-linked evidence checks, then reports whether each cited paper actually reports the stack detail needed to make the claim auditable.**\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37a843-e158-83e8-9139-05719599a47c\",\"title\":\"Automated Compliance Checker\",\"create\":1782032453.461813,\"update\":1782032790.108458,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent an automated compliance checker that scans a GRPO paper or repo and scores it against the 7-item MIN-REPORT-RL checklist. Provide the scoring rubric, heuristics for each item, and example outputs.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/CHECKLIST.md\\nLines: 1-168\\n````md\\n  1 | # MIN-REPORT-RL — Author Checklist\\n  2 | \\n  3 | A copy-pasteable minimum-reportable-stack for **any GRPO-family RL post-training paper**\\n  4 | (GRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO, …).\\n  5 | \\n  6 | **Why this exists.** Algorithm labels are under-specified treatments. In a controlled audit,\\n  7 | a *nominally identical* GRPO config (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset, seed,\\n  8 | step budget) produced **84.4%** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] last-10 training reward on one backend and **5.0%** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] on\\n  9 | another — a ~17× gap [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] with **no visible hyperparameter difference**. The label was constant;\\n 10 | the stack was not. Each item below is on the list because it is a **documented lever that can\\n 11 | flip a head-to-head comparison**. If two papers both report these seven fields, a reader can\\n 12 | tell whether their comparison is confounded.\\n 13 | \\n 14 | Report all seven. They are a *minimum*, not a maximum.\\n 15 | \\n 16 | **Provenance / scope of the worked numbers in this checklist.** The concrete\\n 17 | numbers used as motivating examples below (61.6–89.6% prompt-token\\n 18 | loss magnitude; the 17× matched-config gap; the 82.0→83.3% held-out\\n 19 | control at p=0.26; the 95.0–98.1% / 95.0% Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n 20 | are inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this position\\n 21 | piece. **[TODO:trace to v1 audit citation]** A reader who wants to\\n 22 | verify the numbers should consult that paper; this checklist presents\\n 23 | them as illustrative of the *kind* of stack-driven flip, not as\\n 24 | re-derivable from a fresh run. Once the v1 audit paper is in\\n 25 | citation scope, the references go in the `\\\\bibliography{...}` and\\n 26 | the [TODO:trace] markers are replaced with real `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n 27 | \\n 28 | ---\\n 29 | \\n 30 | ## The 7 items\\n 31 | \\n 32 | ### 1. Loss form\\n 33 | - **Report:** PPO importance ratio used? (yes/no). Clipped? bounds (incl. asymmetric\\n 34 |   \\\"clip-higher\\\")? Token mask: completion-only or whole-sequence? Advantage normalization:\\n 35 |   per-group / per-batch / running estimate?\\n 36 | - **Why it can flip:** the token mask reassigns gradient. In one diagnostic [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation], **61.6–89.6%**\\n 37 |   of full-sequence loss magnitude came from *prompt* tokens, not completion tokens — a\\n 38 |   whole-sequence and a completion-only mask are different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO\\n 39 |   *is* a normalization change; GSPO *is* an IS-granularity change. If the baseline loss form\\n 40 |   is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n 41 | - **Good:** \\\"Token-masked completion-only; PPO ratio with symmetric clip ε=0.2; per-group\\n 42 |   advantage normalization (subtract group mean, divide by group std).\\\"\\n 43 | - **Bad:** \\\"We use the standard GRPO loss.\\\"\\n 44 | \\n 45 | ### 2. Reference policy + KL handling\\n 46 | - **Report:** frozen reference policy retained? (yes/no). KL term in the **loss** or folded\\n 47 |   into the **reward**? KL coefficient + schedule. Forward/reverse, per-token/per-sequence.\\n 48 | - **Why it can flip:** KL placement changes the objective and steady-state exploration. A\\n 49 |   runner with *no* frozen reference (one optimizer step per rollout) is not doing KL-regularized\\n 50 |   GRPO even at matched LR. Dual-anchor methods shift steady-state ZVF and must be recalibrated.\\n 51 | - **Good:** \\\"Frozen reference = SFT checkpoint; KL in the loss, β=0.04 constant; reverse KL,\\n 52 |   per-token.\\\"\\n 53 | - **Bad:** \\\"KL regularization as usual.\\\" / (silent on whether a reference exists)\\n 54 | \\n 55 | ### 3. Sampler / backend / precision\\n 56 | - **Report:** rollout engine (vLLM / SGLang / managed API / trainer `.generate`). Decoding\\n 57 |   params (temperature, top-p, max_tokens). Logit precision in sampler vs. trainer\\n 58 |   (bf16/fp16/fp32). Same tokenizer + chat template in sampler and trainer? (yes/no).\\n 59 | - **Why it can flip:** the sampler *defines* the rollout distribution the group-relative update\\n 60 |   consumes. A managed runner vs. an open vLLM path gave the **17×** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] matched-config gap. Sampler\\n 61 |   precision shifts the probability of mixed-reward groups, hence available gradient.\\n 62 | - **Good:** \\\"vLLM 0.x rollouts, bf16; trainer logits bf16; temp 0.8, top-p 1.0, max_tokens 512;\\n 63 |   identical tokenizer/chat template across both.\\\"\\n 64 | - **Bad:** \\\"Generated with default settings.\\\"\\n 65 | \\n 66 | ### 4. Per-step ZVF and GU trajectory\\n 67 | - **Report:** per-step **ZVF** (fraction of prompts whose G completions all get identical\\n 68 |   reward → zero gradient) and **GU = 1 − ZVF**, logged every step (release the trajectory).\\n 69 | - **Why it can flip:** ZVF/GU reveals whether the run had *any* usable learning signal. A\\n 70 |   method can \\\"win\\\" purely because its stack produced lower ZVF (more usable groups), not because\\n 71 |   its algorithm is better. Without the trajectory, a collapsed run and a saturated run look\\n 72 |   identical from final reward. (Mixed-group probability:\\n 73 |   `P(usable) ≈ (1/N) Σ_x [1 − (1−p_x)^G − p_x^G]`.) Cheap collapse triage: first-5-step rule\\n 74 |   ZVF ≥ 80% with reward ≤ 5%.\\n 75 | - **Good:** \\\"ZVF/GU logged per step (released CSV); mean ZVF@25 = 0.43; no run trips the\\n 76 |   collapse rule.\\\"\\n 77 | - **Bad:** Only final reward reported; no per-step signal telemetry.\\n 78 | - **Substitutes allowed:** under dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration ZVF\\n 79 |   degenerates → report per-step reward variance or gradient-norm variance instead. Item 4\\n 80 |   mandates *a usable-signal trajectory*, of which ZVF/GU is the default.\\n 81 | \\n 82 | ### 5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive)\\n 83 | - **Report:** group size G at every step; the rule that changes it if adaptive (e.g. AERO-style\\n 84 |   double/halve on rolling ZVF).\\n 85 | - **Why it can flip:** G sets the mixed-group probability and was one of the strongest single\\n 86 |   knobs measured (G = 2→4→8→16 moved last-10 reward across a wide band). Adaptive-G vs. fixed-G\\n 87 |   partly credits the variant for spending more compute on hard prompts — separate that from the\\n 88 |   algorithmic claim.\\n 89 | - **Good:** \\\"Fixed G=8 throughout.\\\" / \\\"Adaptive G∈{4,…,16}, baseline 8, double when rolling\\n 90 |   ZVF>0.8, halve when <0.3, window 10.\\\"\\n 91 | - **Bad:** \\\"Group size 8.\\\" (when it was actually adaptive)\\n 92 | \\n 93 | ### 6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment\\n 94 | - **Report:** a held-out slice **disjoint** from training prompts, scored by a harness, with N\\n 95 |   and a CI, reported **separately** from online training reward.\\n 96 | - **Why it can flip:** training reward is dynamics, not capability. A clean paired control\\n 97 |   improved only **82.0% → 83.3% (p=0.26)** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] despite near-saturated training reward; selecting\\n 98 |   checkpoints by training reward produced a spurious 87–95% \\\"capability\\\" band. Four 70B seeds\\n 99 |   ranged 95.0–98.1% on training last-10 [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] yet all landed on 95.0% held-out (sampling noise, not\\n100 |   generalization).\\n101 | - **Good:** \\\"Held-out = 500 GSM8K test problems, seed 0, disjoint from every training batch;\\n102 |   base 82.0% → post 83.3% [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation], Wilson 95% CI, paired per-prompt p=0.54 [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation].\\\"</update>\\n103 | - **Bad:** Reporting training-set reward as the capability number; \\\"accuracy 94%\\\" with no split\\n104 |   stated.\\n105 | \\n106 | ### 7. Decontamination probe results\\n107 | - **Report:** train/test contamination check (n-gram or embedding overlap between training\\n108 |   prompts and the held-out/benchmark slice) AND parser behavior on adversarial format-only\\n109 |   inputs.\\n110 | - **Why it can flip:** verifiable rewards still admit reward hacking — parser artifacts, format\\n111 |   shortcuts, length effects, train-prompt overfitting. Overlap → \\\"gain\\\" may be memorization;\\n112 |   parser rewards a format token → \\\"gain\\\" may be a shortcut.\\n113 | - **Good:** \\\"Max 8-gram overlap between train and held-out = 0.0%; parser rejects 100% of\\n114 |   format-only (no-answer) adversarial inputs.\\\"\\n115 | - **Bad:** No contamination check; parser behavior unstated.\\n116 | \\n117 | ---\\n118 | \\n119 | ## Fillable appendix template (drop into your paper)\\n120 | \\n121 | ```\\n122 | ================ MIN-REPORT-RL BLOCK ================\\n123 | Method label:            <e.g. GRPO / DAPO / GSPO / ...>\\n124 | Base checkpoint:         <model id + revision/hash>\\n125 | Tokenizer + chat tmpl:   <id; same in sampler and trainer? yes/no>\\n126 | \\n127 | [1] LOSS FORM\\n128 |     PPO ratio used:      <yes/no>\\n129 |     Clip:                <none / symmetric eps=__ / asymmetric lo=__ hi=__>\\n130 |     Token mask:          <completion-only / whole-sequence>\\n131 |     Advantage norm.:     <per-group / per-batch / running; formula>\\n132 | \\n133 | [2] REFERENCE + KL\\n134 |     Frozen reference:    <yes (=__ checkpoint) / no>\\n135 |     KL location:         <in loss / in reward / absent>\\n136 |     KL coeff + schedule: <beta=__, schedule=__>\\n137 |     KL estimator:        <forward/reverse, per-token/per-seq>\\n138 | \\n139 | [3] SAMPLER / BACKEND / PRECISION\\n140 |     Rollout engine:      <vLLM/SGLang/managed API/trainer.generate; version>\\n141 |     Decoding:            <temp=__, top_p=__, max_tokens=__>\\n142 |     Precision:           <sampler=__, trainer=__>\\n143 | \\n144 | [4] ZVF / GU TRAJECTORY\\n145 |     Per-step logged:     <yes/no; artifact link>\\n146 |     Mean ZVF@25:         <__>      Mean GU@25: <__>\\n147 |     Collapse rule trip:  <__ / S seeds>   (rule: ZVF>=80% & reward<=5% in first 5 steps)\\n148 |     (or substitute metric under dense/scaffolded rewards: __________)\\n149 | \\n150 | [5] GROUP-SIZE SCHEDULE\\n151 |     Type:                <fixed G=__ / adaptive (rule=__, bounds=__, window=__)>\\n152 | \\n153 | [6] HELD-OUT SPLIT\\n154 |     Slice:               <dataset/split, N=__, seed=__, disjoint from train? yes/no>\\n155 |     Base vs post:        <__% -> __%, CI=__, test=__ (p=__)>\\n156 |     Reported separately from training reward: <yes/no>\\n157 | \\n158 | [7] DECONTAMINATION\\n159 |     Train/test overlap:  <metric=__, value=__>\\n160 |     Parser adversarial:  <format-only reject rate=__%>\\n161 | ====================================================\\n162 | ```\\n163 | \\n164 | ---\\n165 | \\n166 | ## One-line self-test\\n167 | If a reader cannot reproduce your ranking of method A vs. method B **from the block above\\n168 | alone**, the block is incomplete — find the unreported lever and add it.\\n````\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"Below is an invented tool spec for **MIN-REPORT-RL Auditor**, an automated compliance checker for GRPO-family papers and repos. It is designed around the paper’s core claim: a GRPO label is not a complete experimental description, because loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, ZVF/GU telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination can each change the result. fileciteturn0file0\\n\\n# MIN-REPORT-RL Auditor\\n\\n**Purpose:** scan a GRPO-family paper, appendix, repo, logs, and release artifacts; extract evidence for the 7-item MIN-REPORT-RL checklist; assign a reproducibility-compliance score; and produce a reviewer-ready explanation of what is missing.\\n\\n**Target methods:** GRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO, and close variants.\\n\\n**Inputs:**\\n\\n```bash\\nminreport-audit \\\\\\n  --paper paper.pdf \\\\\\n  --repo ./code_release \\\\\\n  --logs ./wandb_export_or_tensorboard \\\\\\n  --mode strict \\\\\\n  --out minreport_audit.json\\n```\\n\\n**Outputs:**\\n\\n1. `minreport_audit.json` — machine-readable score and evidence.\\n2. `MIN_REPORT_RL_REVIEW.md` — reviewer-facing summary.\\n3. `evidence/` — quoted paper spans, config snippets, code locations, log checks.\\n4. Optional badge: `MIN-REPORT-RL: Complete / Usable / Partial / Not Auditable`.\\n\\nThe checker should not merely ask whether an item is “mentioned.” It should ask whether the paper or repo reports enough concrete information for another reader to tell whether two GRPO stacks are comparable. That follows the paper’s definition of MIN-REPORT-RL as the smallest set of fields needed to detect confounding between GRPO-family comparisons. fileciteturn1file4\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. Scoring rubric\\n\\n## Overall score\\n\\nEach of the 7 checklist items receives **0–10 points**.\\n\\n```text\\nRaw score = sum(item scores), max = 70\\nNormalized score = round(100 * raw / 70)\\n```\\n\\n## Verdict bands\\n\\n| Normalized score | Badge | Meaning |\\n|---:|---|---|\\n| 90–100 | **Complete** | All seven fields are concrete, auditable, and consistent across paper/repo/logs. |\\n| 75–89 | **Usable with caveats** | A reviewer can mostly reconstruct the stack, but some subfields or artifacts are weak. |\\n| 50–74 | **Partial** | The paper gives useful details, but multiple flip levers remain under-specified. |\\n| 25–49 | **Not reproducibility-auditable** | Mentions GRPO/RL details, but not enough to distinguish algorithm from stack. |\\n| 0–24 | **Non-compliant** | Mostly label-only reporting. |\\n\\n## Per-item scoring scale\\n\\nFor every checklist item, the auditor assigns a score using this logic:\\n\\n| Score | Meaning |\\n|---:|---|\\n| 10 | Complete: all required subfields present, explicit, and supported by paper/repo/log evidence. |\\n| 8 | Mostly complete: one minor subfield missing, but the stack is still interpretable. |\\n| 5 | Partial: key facts are present, but an important ambiguity remains. |\\n| 2 | Mention-only: item is named but not specified enough to reproduce or audit. |\\n| 0 | Absent, contradictory, or actively misleading. |\\n\\n## Evidence confidence\\n\\nThe score and confidence are separate. A paper can score 8/10 with low confidence if the evidence comes from prose only and the repo contradicts it.\\n\\nEvidence classes:\\n\\n| Evidence class | Confidence effect |\\n|---|---|\\n| Canonical `MIN_REPORT_RL_BLOCK` plus matching repo/log artifacts | Highest |\\n| Explicit paper/appendix text with exact values | High |\\n| Config/code evidence with commit hash | High |\\n| Log-derived evidence | High for telemetry items |\\n| Inferred framework defaults | Low; never enough for full credit |\\n| “Standard GRPO,” “default settings,” “as usual” | Mention-only or zero |\\n\\n## Hard caps\\n\\nThese prevent superficial compliance.\\n\\n1. If the paper has no repo or artifact links, item 4, ZVF/GU trajectory, is capped at **5/10** unless the full trajectory is embedded in the appendix.\\n2. If held-out evaluation is not demonstrably disjoint from training prompts, item 6 is capped at **4/10**.\\n3. If contamination is only discussed conceptually but no probe result is reported, item 7 is capped at **3/10**.\\n4. If a closed/managed backend hides loss or KL internals, items 1–2 cannot exceed **4/10** unless the authors explicitly scope the claim as “platform implementation,” not generic GRPO. The paper itself makes this scoping point for closed runners. fileciteturn1file10\\n5. If paper and repo disagree, use the lower score and emit a contradiction warning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. System design\\n\\n## Pipeline\\n\\n**Stage 1 — Ingest**\\n\\nThe auditor parses:\\n\\n```text\\npaper.pdf / paper.tex / appendix.md\\nREADME.md\\nconfigs/*.yaml, *.json, *.toml\\ntraining scripts\\nevaluation scripts\\nreward parser code\\ndecontamination scripts\\nlogs: wandb, tensorboard, CSV, JSONL\\nmodel cards / release notes\\n```\\n\\n**Stage 2 — Detect GRPO-family scope**\\n\\nIt looks for method labels and variants:\\n\\n```text\\nGRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO,\\nAERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO,\\ngroup relative, group-relative, num_generations,\\ngroup_size, per-group advantage, zero-variance fraction\\n```\\n\\n**Stage 3 — Evidence extraction**\\n\\nThe auditor combines:\\n\\n- regex dictionaries for common field names;\\n- AST/static-code analysis for Python training loops;\\n- config-key mapping for TRL, verl, OpenRLHF, custom trainers;\\n- log-schema detection;\\n- semantic extraction from prose and tables;\\n- consistency checks between paper, configs, and code.\\n\\n**Stage 4 — Normalization**\\n\\nAll extracted evidence is normalized into a canonical schema:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"method_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"base_checkpoint\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n  \\\"tokenizer_chat_template\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n  \\\"loss_form\\\": {},\\n  \\\"reference_kl\\\": {},\\n  \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {},\\n  \\\"zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {},\\n  \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {},\\n  \\\"heldout_split\\\": {},\\n  \\\"decontamination\\\": {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Stage 5 — Score and explain**\\n\\nFor every item, the auditor stores:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"score\\\": 8,\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.83,\\n  \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"source\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n      \\\"location\\\": \\\"appendix B.2\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Token-masked completion-only...\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source\\\": \\\"repo\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"configs/grpo.yaml\\\",\\n      \\\"keys\\\": [\\\"loss.mask=completion_only\\\", \\\"clip_eps=0.2\\\"]\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"advantage normalization formula\\\"],\\n  \\\"warnings\\\": []\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Heuristics for each checklist item\\n\\nThe paper’s seven items are treated as mandatory because each is a flip lever: loss form, KL/reference, sampler/backend/precision, ZVF/GU, group-size schedule, held-out split, and decontamination. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n## Item 1 — Loss form\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\nScore out of 10:\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| PPO-style importance ratio used or explicitly absent | 2 |\\n| Clipping status and bounds, including asymmetric clip-higher | 2 |\\n| Token mask: completion-only vs whole-sequence | 2 |\\n| Advantage normalization: per-group, per-batch, running, or formula | 2 |\\n| Baseline and variant loss forms both specified | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nimportance ratio, old logprobs, pi_theta / pi_old,\\nclip, epsilon, cliprange, clip-higher, asymmetric clip,\\ncompletion-only, token mask, loss mask, response mask,\\nwhole-sequence, prompt tokens,\\nadvantage normalization, group mean, group std,\\nper-group, per-batch, running estimate,\\nDr.GRPO, GSPO, DAPO\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for config keys and code patterns:\\n\\n```text\\nclip_range, cliprange, clip_eps, epsilon, clip_high, clip_low\\nuse_ppo_ratio, importance_sampling, old_log_probs\\ncompletion_mask, response_mask, prompt_mask, loss_mask\\nadvantages = (rewards - rewards.mean(...)) / rewards.std(...)\\nnum_generations, group_rewards, normalize_advantages\\ntorch.exp(logp - old_logp)\\ntorch.clamp(ratio, 1-eps, 1+eps)\\n```\\n\\n**Static-code checks**\\n\\n- Detect whether `old_log_probs` are used.\\n- Detect whether the loss includes prompt tokens by tracing masks.\\n- Detect ratio clipping via `torch.clamp`, `minimum`, `maximum`, or custom clipping.\\n- Detect sequence-level vs token-level importance ratio for GSPO-like variants.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “We use the standard GRPO loss” earns at most **2/10**.\\n- “We follow TRL defaults” earns at most **3/10** unless the repo pins the TRL version and confirms the actual loss path.\\n- A formula in the method section earns partial credit only if the baseline and implementation are consistent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 2 — Reference policy and KL handling\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Frozen reference policy retained or explicitly absent | 2 |\\n| Reference checkpoint/revision if retained | 1 |\\n| KL location: loss, reward, or absent | 2 |\\n| KL coefficient and schedule | 2 |\\n| KL estimator: forward/reverse, per-token/per-sequence | 2 |\\n| Paper/repo consistency | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nreference model, frozen reference, ref policy, old policy,\\nKL, KL penalty, KL regularization, beta, kl_coef,\\nin loss, in reward, reward shaping, non-score reward,\\nforward KL, reverse KL, per-token KL, sequence KL,\\nSFT anchor, dual KL, reference-free\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for:\\n\\n```text\\nref_model, reference_model, create_reference_model\\nkl_coef, beta, init_kl_coef, target_kl\\nkl_penalty, kl_loss, non_score_reward\\nloss += beta * kl\\nreward -= beta * kl\\nref_model = None\\ndisable_reference, use_ref_model: false\\n```\\n\\n**Placement classifier**\\n\\nThe auditor distinguishes:\\n\\n```python\\nif kl appears inside reward computation:\\n    kl_location = \\\"reward\\\"\\nelif kl is added to objective/loss:\\n    kl_location = \\\"loss\\\"\\nelif beta == 0 or ref_model is None:\\n    kl_location = \\\"absent\\\"\\nelse:\\n    kl_location = \\\"ambiguous\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “KL regularization as usual” earns at most **2/10**.\\n- Reporting only `beta=0.04` without saying where KL is applied earns at most **5/10**.\\n- A closed backend with hidden reference/KL internals cannot receive full credit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 3 — Sampler, backend, and precision\\n\\nThe paper highlights sampler/backend/precision because the sampler defines the rollout distribution, and backend differences can move GRPO trajectories under the same visible hyperparameters. fileciteturn1file12\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Rollout engine and version | 2 |\\n| Decoding parameters: temperature, top-p/top-k, max tokens | 2 |\\n| Sampler precision and trainer precision | 2 |\\n| Tokenizer ID/revision and chat template | 2 |\\n| Same tokenizer/template in sampler and trainer, yes/no | 1 |\\n| Managed/backend-specific details or API version | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nvLLM, SGLang, generate, rollout engine,\\nOpenAI API, managed API, inference backend,\\ntemperature, top_p, top-k, max_tokens, max_new_tokens,\\nbf16, fp16, fp32, logits precision,\\ntokenizer, chat template, apply_chat_template,\\nrevision, commit hash\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nInspect:\\n\\n```text\\ngeneration_config.json\\ntokenizer_config.json\\nchat_template.jinja\\nconfigs/rollout.yaml\\nvllm_engine_args\\nsglang_args\\ntransformers.GenerationConfig\\ntemperature, top_p, top_k, do_sample\\ndtype, torch_dtype, bf16, fp16, fp32\\n```\\n\\n**Consistency checks**\\n\\n- Does the paper say `temp=0.8` but config says `temperature: 1.0`?\\n- Does the sampler use vLLM while the trainer uses Hugging Face `generate`?\\n- Is the tokenizer loaded separately in sampler and trainer?\\n- Does the repo pin model revision or only a floating model name?\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “Generated with default settings” earns at most **2/10**.\\n- Naming vLLM without decoding parameters earns at most **4/10**.\\n- Naming precision only for training, not rollout sampling, earns at most **6/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 4 — Per-step ZVF and GU trajectory\\n\\nThe checker treats this as an artifact-heavy item. The paper defines ZVF as the fraction of prompts whose group completions all get identical reward and GU as `1 − ZVF`; it emphasizes that this trajectory reveals whether the run had usable group-relative signal. fileciteturn1file12\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| ZVF/GU definition or accepted substitute | 2 |\\n| Per-step trajectory artifact released | 3 |\\n| Summary stats, e.g. ZVF@25, GU@25 | 1.5 |\\n| Collapse rule or equivalent triage | 1 |\\n| Coverage for every arm and seed | 1.5 |\\n| Schema is machine-readable | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nZVF, zero-variance fraction,\\nGU, gradient utilization,\\nusable signal, reward variance,\\nper-step reward variance, gradient-norm variance,\\ncollapse, first-five-step rule,\\nZVF >= 80%, reward <= 5%\\n```\\n\\n**Repo/log heuristics**\\n\\nLook for logs with columns:\\n\\n```text\\nstep\\nprompt_id\\ngroup_id\\ncompletion_id\\nreward\\nzvf\\ngu\\nreward_variance\\ngradient_norm_variance\\n```\\n\\nAcceptable artifact names:\\n\\n```text\\nzvf_gu.csv\\ntelemetry.jsonl\\ntrain_metrics.csv\\nwandb-history.jsonl\\ntensorboard event files\\n```\\n\\n**Derived-metric computation**\\n\\nIf raw group rewards are available, the auditor computes ZVF itself:\\n\\n```python\\ndef compute_zvf(group_rewards_by_prompt):\\n    zero_var = 0\\n    total = 0\\n    for prompt_id, rewards in group_rewards_by_prompt.items():\\n        total += 1\\n        if max(rewards) == min(rewards):\\n            zero_var += 1\\n    zvf = zero_var / total\\n    gu = 1.0 - zvf\\n    return zvf, gu\\n```\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- Reporting only final reward earns **0/10** for item 4.\\n- Reporting reward variance without prompt/group IDs earns at most **4/10**.\\n- A single plotted curve with no data artifact earns at most **6/10**.\\n- ZVF on one seed only, when multiple seeds are claimed, caps at **7/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 5 — Group-size schedule\\n\\nThe paper treats group size `G` as a major lever because it affects the probability of mixed-reward, gradient-usable groups. fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Fixed or adaptive schedule stated | 2 |\\n| `G` value at every step, or machine-readable schedule | 3 |\\n| Adaptive rule, bounds, and window if adaptive | 2 |\\n| Compute accounting: rollouts/tokens/prompts | 1.5 |\\n| Baseline and variant comparability | 1.5 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\ngroup size, num generations, G=,\\nrollouts per prompt, samples per prompt,\\nadaptive group size, dynamic sampling,\\nAERO, double, halve, rolling ZVF,\\nbounds, window, schedule\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for:\\n\\n```text\\nnum_generations\\nnum_return_sequences\\ngroup_size\\nrollouts_per_prompt\\nn_samples_per_prompt\\nadaptive_g\\nmin_group_size\\nmax_group_size\\nzvf_window\\n```\\n\\n**Schedule reconstruction**\\n\\n- If raw logs include prompt IDs and completions, infer `G` per prompt per step.\\n- If adaptive, verify that schedule changes match the stated rule.\\n- If a variant uses dynamic sampling, check whether compute/token budget is normalized against the baseline.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “Group size 8” earns full credit only if no adaptive code/log behavior contradicts it.\\n- If the method adaptively filters or resamples groups but reports only nominal `G`, cap at **5/10**.\\n- If DAPO-style dynamic sampling is present but no compute accounting is given, cap at **7/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 6 — Held-out split distinct from reward environment\\n\\nThe paper distinguishes online training reward from capability evidence and requires a held-out slice disjoint from training prompts, scored by a harness, with sample size and confidence interval. fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Dataset/split name, sample size, seed | 2 |\\n| Explicit train/eval disjointness | 2 |\\n| Evaluation harness and scoring parser | 2 |\\n| Training reward reported separately from held-out accuracy | 1.5 |\\n| Confidence interval or statistical test | 1.5 |\\n| Checkpoint selection rule | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nheld-out, test set, validation set, disjoint,\\nGSM8K test, MATH test, benchmark split,\\nevaluation harness, lm-eval, grader, parser,\\nconfidence interval, CI, bootstrap, Wilson,\\npaired test, p-value, seed,\\ncheckpoint selection, best checkpoint, last checkpoint\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nInspect:\\n\\n```text\\neval.py\\nevaluate.py\\nlm_eval configs\\ndataset split loading\\ntrain.jsonl / test.jsonl\\nheldout_ids.txt\\ncheckpoint selection scripts\\n```\\n\\n**Disjointness verifier**\\n\\nIf datasets are included:\\n\\n- Hash normalized prompts.\\n- Compare train and eval IDs.\\n- Run n-gram overlap as a weaker fallback.\\n- Flag if eval prompts appear in reward-training files.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “Accuracy 94%” with no split earns at most **2/10**.\\n- Reporting training-set reward as “accuracy” earns **0–2/10**.\\n- Held-out score without N/CI caps at **7/10**.\\n- Selecting the best checkpoint by training reward without a stated rule triggers a warning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 7 — Decontamination probe results\\n\\nThe paper requires both train/test contamination checks and parser behavior on adversarial format-only inputs, because reward hacking can come from overlap, parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, or train-prompt overfitting. fileciteturn1file5\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Train/test overlap method: n-gram, MinHash, embedding, etc. | 2 |\\n| Quantitative overlap result | 2 |\\n| Threshold or decision rule | 1 |\\n| Parser adversarial format-only tests | 2 |\\n| Parser reject/accept rates | 1.5 |\\n| Scripts or fixtures released | 1 |\\n| Remediation for contaminated cases | 0.5 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\ndecontamination, contamination, overlap,\\nn-gram, 8-gram, MinHash, embedding similarity,\\ntrain/test leakage, duplicate prompts,\\nparser adversarial, format-only, no-answer,\\nreward hacking, shortcut, parser rejects,\\nanswer extractor, format token\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for:\\n\\n```text\\ndecontam.py\\noverlap.py\\nngram_overlap\\nminhash\\nfaiss\\nsentence-transformers\\nparser_tests.py\\ntest_reward_parser.py\\nformat_only_cases.jsonl\\nadversarial_parser_inputs.jsonl\\n```\\n\\n**Parser probe checks**\\n\\nThe auditor generates or loads cases like:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"input\\\": \\\"####\\\",\\n  \\\"model_output\\\": \\\"Therefore the answer is \\\\\\\\boxed{}\\\",\\n  \\\"expected_reward\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"reason\\\": \\\"format-only, no answer\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen it runs the reward parser if available and flags cases where format-only outputs receive positive reward.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “We use the official test set” is not a decontamination probe.\\n- “No contamination was found” without method/result earns at most **3/10**.\\n- Overlap check without parser adversarial tests caps at **6/10**.\\n- Parser tests without train/test overlap caps at **6/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. Canonical output schema\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"tool\\\": \\\"MIN-REPORT-RL Auditor\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"target\\\": {\\n    \\\"paper\\\": \\\"paper.pdf\\\",\\n    \\\"repo\\\": \\\"https://github.com/example/grpo-release\\\",\\n    \\\"commit\\\": \\\"7fd31c2\\\",\\n    \\\"method_labels_detected\\\": [\\\"GRPO\\\", \\\"DAPO\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"raw\\\": 58.5,\\n    \\\"max\\\": 70,\\n    \\\"normalized\\\": 84,\\n    \\\"badge\\\": \\\"Usable with caveats\\\",\\n    \\\"confidence\\\": 0.81\\n  },\\n  \\\"items\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": 0.86,\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"mostly_complete\\\",\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"baseline advantage normalization formula\\\"],\\n      \\\"warnings\\\": []\\n    },\\n    \\\"2_reference_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": 0.91,\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"mostly_complete\\\",\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [],\\n      \\\"warnings\\\": [\\\"paper says reverse KL; code variable name says approx_kl, verified as reverse estimator\\\"]\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"blocking_issues\\\": [\\n    \\\"No released adversarial parser fixtures; item 7 capped at 8.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"reviewer_summary\\\": \\\"The stack is mostly auditable. The main missing pieces are parser adversarial fixtures and the exact baseline advantage-normalization formula.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Example output A — strong paper plus repo\\n\\n## CLI summary\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: dapo_math_release\\nMode: strict\\n\\nOverall: 93/100\\nRaw: 65.0 / 70\\nBadge: COMPLETE\\nConfidence: 0.89\\n\\n[1] Loss form                         9.0/10  PASS\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    9.0/10  PASS\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     10.0/10 PASS\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      9.0/10  PASS\\n[5] Group-size schedule               9.0/10  PASS\\n[6] Held-out split                    10.0/10 PASS\\n[7] Decontamination probes            9.0/10  PASS\\n\\nMain caveat:\\n- Item 4: ZVF/GU trajectory is released for all seeds, but only mean ZVF@25 is summarized in the paper; GU@25 is present in the CSV.\\n```\\n\\n## Reviewer-facing summary\\n\\n```md\\n# MIN-REPORT-RL Review\\n\\nVerdict: COMPLETE, 93/100.\\n\\nThe paper is reproducibility-auditable. It reports the GRPO-family stack with enough detail\\nto distinguish the algorithmic delta from the stack: loss ratio/clipping/masking/advantage\\nnormalization, frozen reference and KL placement, vLLM rollout configuration, sampler/trainer\\nprecision, ZVF/GU telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out disjoint evaluation, and decontamination.\\n\\nRemaining minor issue:\\n- Add GU@25 to the paper table instead of requiring readers to open telemetry.csv.\\n```\\n\\n## JSON excerpt\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalized\\\": 93,\\n    \\\"badge\\\": \\\"Complete\\\",\\n    \\\"confidence\\\": 0.89\\n  },\\n  \\\"items\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"source\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n          \\\"location\\\": \\\"Appendix A.1\\\",\\n          \\\"text\\\": \\\"PPO ratio with asymmetric clip_low=0.2, clip_high=0.28; completion-only mask; per-group advantage normalization.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"source\\\": \\\"repo\\\",\\n          \\\"path\\\": \\\"configs/dapo_math.yaml\\\",\\n          \\\"keys\\\": {\\n            \\\"loss.use_ratio\\\": true,\\n            \\\"loss.clip_low\\\": 0.2,\\n            \\\"loss.clip_high\\\": 0.28,\\n            \\\"loss.mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n            \\\"loss.adv_norm\\\": \\\"per_group\\\"\\n          }\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"missing\\\": []\\n    },\\n    \\\"4_zvf_gu\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"source\\\": \\\"logs\\\",\\n          \\\"path\\\": \\\"artifacts/telemetry/zvf_gu_seed*.csv\\\",\\n          \\\"columns\\\": [\\\"step\\\", \\\"seed\\\", \\\"zvf\\\", \\\"gu\\\", \\\"mean_reward\\\"]\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"derived\\\": {\\n        \\\"mean_zvf_at_25\\\": 0.43,\\n        \\\"mean_gu_at_25\\\": 0.57,\\n        \\\"collapse_rule_trips\\\": \\\"0/8 seeds\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"GU@25 not printed in paper table\\\"]\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. Example output B — typical “standard GRPO” paper, no repo\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: grpo_reasoning_paper.pdf\\nMode: paper-only\\n\\nOverall: 31/100\\nRaw: 21.5 / 70\\nBadge: NOT REPRODUCIBILITY-AUDITABLE\\nConfidence: 0.42\\n\\n[1] Loss form                         2.0/10  FAIL\\n    Evidence: \\\"We use the standard GRPO objective.\\\"\\n    Missing: ratio yes/no, clip bounds, token mask, advantage norm.\\n\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    2.5/10  FAIL\\n    Evidence: \\\"KL regularization is applied.\\\"\\n    Missing: frozen reference, KL location, beta schedule, estimator.\\n\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     4.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"We sample 8 responses at temperature 0.7.\\\"\\n    Missing: rollout engine, top_p, max_tokens, precision, tokenizer/chat-template identity.\\n\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      0.0/10  FAIL\\n    Missing: no ZVF, GU, reward variance, or group-signal trajectory.\\n\\n[5] Group-size schedule               4.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"G=8.\\\"\\n    Missing: per-step schedule; no confirmation fixed vs adaptive.\\n\\n[6] Held-out split                    5.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"GSM8K test accuracy is reported.\\\"\\n    Missing: disjointness statement, CI, checkpoint selection rule.\\n\\n[7] Decontamination probes            4.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"We remove duplicate train/test examples.\\\"\\n    Missing: overlap metric, quantitative result, parser adversarial tests.\\n\\nBlocking reviewer note:\\nThis paper should not be treated as an auditable GRPO comparison. The algorithm label is\\nreported, but most stack fields that MIN-REPORT-RL treats as flip levers are absent.\\n```\\n\\n## Suggested author fixes\\n\\n```md\\nRequired before reproducibility review:\\n1. Add a MIN-REPORT-RL block to the appendix.\\n2. State whether the loss uses an old-policy ratio, clipping, completion-only masking, and per-group normalization.\\n3. State whether KL is in the loss, reward, or absent.\\n4. Release per-step group-reward telemetry or ZVF/GU CSV.\\n5. Report held-out N, CI, disjointness, and checkpoint selection.\\n6. Add train/test overlap and reward-parser adversarial probes.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Example output C — repo found, paper underspecified\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: mad-grpo-code-release\\nMode: paper+repo\\n\\nOverall: 76/100\\nRaw: 53.0 / 70\\nBadge: USABLE WITH CAVEATS\\nConfidence: 0.78\\n\\nThe paper itself is incomplete, but the repo makes the stack partially reconstructable.\\n\\nItem scores:\\n[1] Loss form                         8/10\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    7/10\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     8/10\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      5/10\\n[5] Group-size schedule               9/10\\n[6] Held-out split                    8/10\\n[7] Decontamination probes            8/10\\n\\nContradictions:\\n- Paper says \\\"completion-only loss\\\"; repo default is `loss_mask: full_sequence` in `configs/base.yaml`.\\n- Experiment override `configs/mad_grpo_math.yaml` changes this to `completion_only`, so the run is probably compliant, but the paper should cite the exact config.\\n\\nCaps applied:\\n- Item 4 capped at 5 because only aggregate ZVF@25 is reported; no per-step trajectory artifact is released.\\n```\\n\\n## JSON excerpt\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalized\\\": 76,\\n    \\\"badge\\\": \\\"Usable with caveats\\\",\\n    \\\"confidence\\\": 0.78\\n  },\\n  \\\"contradictions\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"field\\\": \\\"loss_form.token_mask\\\",\\n      \\\"paper\\\": \\\"completion-only\\\",\\n      \\\"repo_default\\\": \\\"full_sequence\\\",\\n      \\\"experiment_override\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n      \\\"resolution\\\": \\\"experiment override wins, but paper should cite config path\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"caps\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"item\\\": \\\"4_zvf_gu\\\",\\n      \\\"cap\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"No released per-step trajectory.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Example output D — closed managed backend\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: managed_api_grpo_study\\nMode: strict\\n\\nOverall: 49/100\\nRaw: 34.5 / 70\\nBadge: NOT REPRODUCIBILITY-AUDITABLE\\nConfidence: 0.55\\n\\nImportant scoping warning:\\nThe work uses a closed managed RL backend. The authors disclose this, but the loss form and\\nreference/KL implementation are not inspectable. The result should be scoped as:\\n\\\"ManagedBackend-X GRPO implementation\\\"\\nnot:\\n\\\"GRPO generally.\\\"\\n\\nScores:\\n[1] Loss form                         4/10  capped: closed backend\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    4/10  capped: closed backend\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     7/10\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      0/10\\n[5] Group-size schedule               7/10\\n[6] Held-out split                    8/10\\n[7] Decontamination probes            4.5/10\\n\\nReviewer note:\\nThis may still be a useful platform result, but it is not enough evidence for an algorithmic\\nGRPO-family claim unless the hidden stack fields are disclosed or the claim is explicitly scoped.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9. Anti-gaming rules\\n\\nThe checker should be conservative because the paper’s whole point is that visible hyperparameters are not enough.\\n\\n## Ambiguous prose penalties\\n\\n| Phrase | Maximum credit |\\n|---|---:|\\n| “standard GRPO loss” | 2/10 on item 1 |\\n| “KL as usual” | 2/10 on item 2 |\\n| “default generation settings” | 2/10 on item 3 |\\n| “we report reward curves” | 3/10 on item 4 unless ZVF/GU or substitute is present |\\n| “G=8” with adaptive code present | 5/10 on item 5 |\\n| “evaluated on GSM8K” | 4/10 on item 6 unless split/disjointness/N/CI are present |\\n| “no contamination” | 3/10 on item 7 unless method and result are reported |\\n\\n## Contradiction handling\\n\\nWhen paper and repo disagree:\\n\\n```text\\nitem_score = min(paper_score, repo_score_after_resolution)\\nwarning = \\\"paper/repo contradiction\\\"\\n```\\n\\nIf a launch config overrides a default, the checker accepts the launch config only if it can identify the exact experiment command, config merge order, or run artifact.\\n\\n## Default handling\\n\\nFramework defaults count only when all are true:\\n\\n1. framework and version are pinned;\\n2. the exact trainer class is identified;\\n3. the repo does not override the default;\\n4. the default is documented or visible in code.\\n\\nOtherwise defaults are treated as uncertain.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10. Recommended canonical artifact\\n\\nThe easiest way for papers to pass the checker is to ship this file:\\n\\n```text\\nartifacts/min_report_rl.json\\n```\\n\\nMinimal schema:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"method_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"base_checkpoint\\\": {\\n    \\\"id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\\",\\n    \\\"revision\\\": \\\"...\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"tokenizer_chat_template\\\": {\\n    \\\"tokenizer_id\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n    \\\"chat_template_hash\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n    \\\"same_sampler_trainer\\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\\"loss_form\\\": {\\n    \\\"ppo_ratio_used\\\": true,\\n    \\\"clip\\\": {\\\"type\\\": \\\"symmetric\\\", \\\"epsilon\\\": 0.2},\\n    \\\"token_mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n    \\\"advantage_norm\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": \\\"per_group\\\",\\n      \\\"formula\\\": \\\"(r - mean_group) / std_group\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"reference_kl\\\": {\\n    \\\"frozen_reference\\\": true,\\n    \\\"reference_checkpoint\\\": \\\"SFT checkpoint hash\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_location\\\": \\\"loss\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_coeff\\\": 0.04,\\n    \\\"kl_schedule\\\": \\\"constant\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_estimator\\\": \\\"reverse_per_token\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n    \\\"rollout_engine\\\": \\\"vLLM\\\",\\n    \\\"rollout_engine_version\\\": \\\"0.x\\\",\\n    \\\"decoding\\\": {\\\"temperature\\\": 0.8, \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0, \\\"max_tokens\\\": 512},\\n    \\\"precision\\\": {\\\"sampler_logits\\\": \\\"bf16\\\", \\\"trainer_logits\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"}\\n  },\\n  \\\"zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n    \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"telemetry/zvf_gu.csv\\\",\\n    \\\"per_step_logged\\\": true,\\n    \\\"mean_zvf_at_25\\\": 0.43,\\n    \\\"mean_gu_at_25\\\": 0.57,\\n    \\\"collapse_rule_trips\\\": \\\"0/8\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"fixed\\\",\\n    \\\"G\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"telemetry/group_size_by_step.csv\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"heldout_split\\\": {\\n    \\\"dataset\\\": \\\"GSM8K\\\",\\n    \\\"split\\\": \\\"test\\\",\\n    \\\"N\\\": 500,\\n    \\\"seed\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"disjoint_from_train\\\": true,\\n    \\\"harness\\\": \\\"eval/evaluate_gsm8k.py\\\",\\n    \\\"base_score\\\": 0.82,\\n    \\\"post_score\\\": 0.833,\\n    \\\"ci\\\": \\\"Wilson 95%\\\",\\n    \\\"reported_separately_from_training_reward\\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\\"decontamination\\\": {\\n    \\\"overlap_metric\\\": \\\"8-gram overlap\\\",\\n    \\\"overlap_value\\\": 0.0,\\n    \\\"parser_adversarial_artifact\\\": \\\"tests/format_only_cases.jsonl\\\",\\n    \\\"format_only_reject_rate\\\": 1.0\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis matches the paper’s adoption idea: trainers should emit the seven-field block and per-step telemetry by default, because the compliance cost should be close to a JSON block plus logged trajectories. fileciteturn1file1\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37a4ab-33e4-83ee-9e3e-a89e36a0cbde\",\"title\":\"Plugin for GRPO Reporting\",\"create\":1782031532.756041,\"update\":1782032029.644241,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached MIN-REPORT-RL position paper. Invent a TRL plugin that automatically emits the 7-item minimum-reportable-stack block as JSON at the start of every GRPO run and logs per-step ZVF/GU. Specify the hooks, config flags, and output schema.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Proposed TRL plugin: `trl-min-report-rl`\\n\\n`trl-min-report-rl` is a small TRL observer/callback package for `GRPOTrainer`. It does **not** change the loss, sampler, optimizer, reward function, or training loop semantics. Its only job is to make the paper’s Min-Report-RL block automatic: emit the seven-item stack manifest as JSON before the first rollout, then log per-step `ZVF` and `GU` from the grouped rewards that GRPO already needs to compute advantages. The attached paper’s Section 3 defines the seven mandatory fields, and Section 5 explicitly proposes a TRL flag like `report_min_report_rl=True` plus a shared JSON emitter for TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF. fileciteturn0file0\\n\\nCurrent TRL already has most of the necessary surfaces: `GRPOTrainer` accepts `callbacks` and `rollout_func`, reward functions are summed across reward functions, and `GRPOConfig` exposes key stack levers such as `num_generations`, vLLM settings, `beta`, `epsilon`, `importance_sampling_level`, `scale_rewards`, and `loss_type`. citeturn256404view0turn256404view1turn256404view2 TRL also already logs a closely related reward-diversity metric, `frac_reward_zero_std`, but the plugin should still emit canonical `min_report_rl/zvf` and `min_report_rl/gu` because the standard needs a pinned definition, JSONL trace, tolerance, grouping key, reward stage, and manifest schema. citeturn256404view3\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. User-facing API\\n\\n### Native upstream form\\n\\n```python\\nfrom trl import GRPOConfig, GRPOTrainer\\n\\ntraining_args = GRPOConfig(\\n    output_dir=\\\"runs/qwen-grpo\\\",\\n    learning_rate=1e-6,\\n    num_generations=8,\\n    use_vllm=True,\\n    vllm_mode=\\\"server\\\",\\n\\n    # New plugin flags\\n    report_min_report_rl=True,\\n    min_report_rl_strict=\\\"warn\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_output_dir=\\\"runs/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_zvf_atol=1e-12,\\n    min_report_rl_emit_to=[\\\"file\\\", \\\"trainer_log\\\", \\\"tracker\\\"],\\n\\n    # User-supplied fields TRL cannot infer reliably\\n    min_report_rl_reward_env_id=\\\"gsm8k/train[:500]\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_heldout_id=\\\"gsm8k/test[:500]\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_eval_harness=\\\"lm-eval-harness:gsm8k@<commit>\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_decontam_report=\\\"artifacts/decontam_gsm8k.json\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_parser_probe_report=\\\"artifacts/reward_parser_probe.json\\\",\\n)\\n\\ntrainer = GRPOTrainer(\\n    model=\\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n    args=training_args,\\n    reward_funcs=[reward_fn],\\n    train_dataset=train_dataset,\\n    eval_dataset=heldout_dataset,\\n)\\n\\ntrainer.train()\\n```\\n\\n### External package form before upstream merge\\n\\n```python\\nfrom trl import GRPOTrainer, GRPOConfig\\nfrom trl_min_report_rl import MinReportRLConfig, attach_min_report_rl\\n\\nargs = GRPOConfig(...)\\ntrainer = GRPOTrainer(..., args=args)\\n\\nattach_min_report_rl(\\n    trainer,\\n    MinReportRLConfig(\\n        enabled=True,\\n        strict=\\\"warn\\\",\\n        output_dir=f\\\"{args.output_dir}/min_report_rl\\\",\\n        reward_env_id=\\\"gsm8k/train[:500]\\\",\\n        heldout_id=\\\"gsm8k/test[:500]\\\",\\n        eval_harness=\\\"lm-eval-harness:gsm8k@<commit>\\\",\\n        decontam_report=\\\"artifacts/decontam.json\\\",\\n        parser_probe_report=\\\"artifacts/parser_probe.json\\\",\\n    ),\\n)\\n\\ntrainer.train()\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Hooks\\n\\nThe plugin should be implemented as a **hybrid**: ordinary `TrainerCallback` hooks for lifecycle events, plus one small GRPO-specific reward hook because normal callbacks do not see the grouped reward tensor at the right time.\\n\\n| Hook | Existing or new | When it fires | What it does |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| `GRPOTrainer.__init__` config hook | new TRL patch | After `GRPOConfig` is resolved | If `report_min_report_rl=True`, attach `MinReportRLCallback` and `GRPORewardTelemetryObserver`. |\\n| `on_init_end` | existing callback | End of trainer init | Snapshot static trainer/model/tokenizer/PEFT/config fields, but do not write yet. |\\n| `on_train_begin` | existing callback | Before first training step | Emit `min_report_rl.manifest.v1.json`; log manifest URI and schema version. |\\n| `on_grpo_rollout_begin(payload)` | new GRPO observer hook | Before completions are generated | Allocate `rollout_id`; record sampler/backend/decoding params and actual group-size plan. |\\n| `on_grpo_rewards_computed(payload)` | **new required GRPO observer hook** | Immediately after raw reward aggregation, before reward scaling / advantage normalization | Compute ZVF/GU from per-prompt grouped rewards; write JSONL step record; push scalar logs. |\\n| `on_grpo_advantages_computed(payload)` | optional new hook | After advantage normalization | Sanity-check that zero-variance groups produce zero usable group-relative signal. |\\n| `on_grpo_group_size_update(payload)` | optional new hook | Whenever adaptive `G` changes | Append the schedule event and reason. |\\n| `on_log` | existing callback | TRL logging event | Merge `min_report_rl/zvf`, `min_report_rl/gu`, and counts into tracker logs. |\\n| `on_save` | existing callback | Checkpoint save | Copy manifest and telemetry tail into checkpoint artifact directory. |\\n| `on_train_end` | existing callback | End of training | Emit `min_report_rl.summary.v1.json`, including telemetry checksum and collapse triage. |\\n\\nThe critical hook is `on_grpo_rewards_computed`. It must run **after** all reward functions have been weighted and aggregated into the scalar reward used by GRPO, but **before** `scale_rewards`, advantage normalization, clipping, or loss computation. TRL’s current docs state that rewards can come from one or more reward functions and are summed, so the plugin should compute ZVF on the same aggregated scalar reward that will drive the group-relative update. citeturn256404view0\\n\\n### Required reward hook payload\\n\\n```python\\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\\nclass GRPORewardTelemetryPayload:\\n    run_id: str\\n    phase: Literal[\\\"train\\\", \\\"eval\\\"]\\n    global_step: int\\n    rollout_id: int\\n    optimizer_step: int | None\\n    process_rank: int\\n    world_size: int\\n\\n    # Grouping\\n    prompt_group_ids: list[str]          # length = num_completions\\n    prompt_hashes: list[str]             # same length; no raw prompts by default\\n    completion_indices: list[int]        # 0..G_i-1 within prompt\\n    group_sizes: dict[str, int]          # prompt_group_id -> actual G_i\\n\\n    # Rewards\\n    raw_aggregated_rewards: list[float | None]\\n    reward_components: dict[str, list[float | None]]\\n    reward_weights: dict[str, float]\\n    reward_stage: Literal[\\\"raw_aggregated_before_scaling\\\"]\\n\\n    # Optional diagnostics\\n    completion_lengths: list[int] | None\\n    truncated: list[bool] | None\\n    sampler_logprob_dtype: str | None\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Config flags\\n\\nUse one top-level enable flag and a nested config object. In CLI form, every nested field becomes `--min_report_rl_*`.\\n\\n| Flag | Default | Meaning |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| `report_min_report_rl` | `True` for `GRPOTrainer` | Emit the manifest and per-step telemetry. |\\n| `min_report_rl_output_dir` | `\\\"{output_dir}/min_report_rl\\\"` | Directory for JSON manifest, JSONL telemetry, and summary. |\\n| `min_report_rl_schema_version` | `\\\"min-report-rl.trl.v1\\\"` | Manifest schema version. |\\n| `min_report_rl_strict` | `\\\"warn\\\"` | `\\\"off\\\"`, `\\\"warn\\\"`, or `\\\"error\\\"` for missing/unknown mandatory fields. |\\n| `min_report_rl_emit_to` | `[\\\"file\\\", \\\"trainer_log\\\"]` | Destinations: `file`, `stdout`, `trainer_log`, `tracker`, `hub_artifact`. |\\n| `min_report_rl_metric_prefix` | `\\\"min_report_rl\\\"` | Prefix for scalar logs. |\\n| `min_report_rl_zvf_atol` | `1e-12` | Absolute reward equality tolerance. |\\n| `min_report_rl_zvf_rtol` | `0.0` | Relative reward equality tolerance. |\\n| `min_report_rl_hash_prompts` | `True` | Hash prompts instead of logging raw prompt text. |\\n| `min_report_rl_prompt_hash_salt_env` | `\\\"MIN_REPORT_RL_SALT\\\"` | Optional salt env var for prompt hashes. |\\n| `min_report_rl_log_reward_components` | `True` | Also log component-level ZVF/GU, not only aggregated reward ZVF/GU. |\\n| `min_report_rl_collapse_window_steps` | `5` | Window for first-five-step collapse triage. |\\n| `min_report_rl_collapse_zvf_threshold` | `0.80` | Collapse warning threshold. |\\n| `min_report_rl_collapse_reward_threshold` | `0.05` | Collapse warning threshold for low reward. |\\n| `min_report_rl_reward_env_id` | `null` | Training/reward environment identifier. |\\n| `min_report_rl_heldout_id` | `null` | Held-out dataset/split identifier. |\\n| `min_report_rl_heldout_fingerprint` | inferred if possible | Dataset fingerprint or prompt-hash manifest. |\\n| `min_report_rl_eval_harness` | `null` | Harness name/version/commit for held-out scoring. |\\n| `min_report_rl_ci_method` | `null` | Confidence-interval method used for held-out evaluation. |\\n| `min_report_rl_decontam_report` | `null` | Path/URI to train/test overlap report. |\\n| `min_report_rl_parser_probe_report` | `null` | Path/URI to adversarial parser probe report. |\\n| `min_report_rl_fail_on_closed_stack_unknowns` | `False` | If true, fail when a backend cannot expose mandatory fields. |\\n\\nFields that TRL can infer should be filled automatically: `loss_type`, `epsilon`, `epsilon_high`, `importance_sampling_level`, `scale_rewards`, `beta`, reference-model state, `num_generations`, vLLM settings, decoding parameters, tokenizer/chat-template hashes, precision flags, PEFT/LoRA config if present, optimizer class, package versions, and seed. Fields that TRL cannot prove, especially held-out disjointness and decontamination probes, must be supplied or marked `unknown`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. ZVF/GU computation\\n\\nFor each training step, the plugin groups rewards by prompt. For prompt group `x` with completions `r_{x,1..G}`, the group is zero-variance if:\\n\\n```text\\nmax(finite_rewards_x) - min(finite_rewards_x)\\n    <= min_report_rl_zvf_atol\\n       + min_report_rl_zvf_rtol * max(1, max(abs(finite_rewards_x)))\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```text\\nZVF = zero_variance_group_count / eligible_group_count\\nGU  = 1.0 - ZVF\\n```\\n\\nImportant details:\\n\\n* The denominator is **prompt groups**, not completions.\\n* The reward stage is `raw_aggregated_before_scaling`, so ZVF reflects the scalar signal entering GRPO before normalization.\\n* Groups with fewer than two finite rewards are excluded and counted in `ineligible_group_count`.\\n* For variable/adaptive `G`, each prompt uses its actual `G_i`; the JSONL record logs min/mean/max group size.\\n* For multiple reward functions, the canonical ZVF uses the final weighted scalar reward; optional component-level metrics are logged as `min_report_rl/zvf_by_reward/<reward_name>`.\\n* In distributed training, all ranks gather reward telemetry first; rank zero computes the global ZVF/GU, and all ranks log the same metric keys to avoid tracker divergence.\\n\\nScalar names emitted to TRL/tracker logs:\\n\\n```text\\nmin_report_rl/zvf\\nmin_report_rl/gu\\nmin_report_rl/zero_variance_groups\\nmin_report_rl/mixed_groups\\nmin_report_rl/eligible_groups\\nmin_report_rl/ineligible_groups\\nmin_report_rl/group_size_mean\\nmin_report_rl/group_size_min\\nmin_report_rl/group_size_max\\nmin_report_rl/collapse_candidate\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Run-start manifest schema\\n\\nThe start-of-run artifact is a single JSON file:\\n\\n```text\\n{output_dir}/min_report_rl/min_report_rl.manifest.v1.json\\n```\\n\\nSchema outline:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"$schema\\\": \\\"https://zvf.dev/schemas/min-report-rl/trl-manifest-v1.json\\\",\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"min-report-rl.trl.v1\\\",\\n  \\\"emitted_at_utc\\\": \\\"2026-06-21T00:00:00Z\\\",\\n  \\\"run\\\": {\\n    \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid\\\",\\n    \\\"trainer\\\": \\\"trl.GRPOTrainer\\\",\\n    \\\"algorithm_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n    \\\"trl_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"transformers_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"accelerate_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"torch_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"python_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"git\\\": {\\n      \\\"repo\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n      \\\"commit\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n      \\\"dirty\\\": \\\"boolean|null\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"seed\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"distributed\\\": {\\n      \\\"world_size\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"mixed_precision\\\": \\\"bf16|fp16|fp32|unknown\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"minimum_reportable_stack\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"loss_type\\\": \\\"grpo|dapo|dr_grpo|bnpo|cispo|sapo|luspo|vespo|custom\\\",\\n      \\\"importance_ratio\\\": {\\n        \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n        \\\"level\\\": \\\"token|sequence|none|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"old_policy_source\\\": \\\"rollout_logprobs|policy_snapshot|unknown\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clip\\\": {\\n        \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n        \\\"epsilon_low\\\": 0.2,\\n        \\\"epsilon_high\\\": 0.2,\\n        \\\"asymmetric\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"token_mask\\\": {\\n        \\\"scope\\\": \\\"completion_only|whole_sequence|custom|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"mask_truncated_completions\\\": false,\\n        \\\"top_entropy_quantile\\\": 1.0\\n      },\\n      \\\"advantage_normalization\\\": {\\n        \\\"scale_rewards\\\": \\\"group|batch|none|custom\\\",\\n        \\\"multi_objective_aggregation\\\": \\\"sum_then_normalize|normalize_then_sum|custom\\\",\\n        \\\"reward_weights\\\": {\\n          \\\"reward_fn_name\\\": 1.0\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"2_reference_policy_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"reference_policy\\\": {\\n        \\\"retained\\\": true,\\n        \\\"source\\\": \\\"separate_ref_model|adapter_disabled|none|closed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"sync_ref_model\\\": false,\\n        \\\"ref_model_sync_steps\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"kl\\\": {\\n        \\\"coefficient_beta\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"loss|reward|none|closed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"schedule\\\": \\\"constant|linear|custom|none|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"per_token_forward|per_sequence|reverse|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"logged_metric\\\": \\\"kl\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"3_sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"rollout_engine\\\": {\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"vllm|transformers.generate|custom_rollout_func|managed_api|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"mode\\\": \\\"server|colocate|local|closed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"version\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"model_impl\\\": \\\"vllm|transformers|unknown\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"decoding\\\": {\\n        \\\"temperature\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"top_k\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"min_p\\\": null,\\n        \\\"max_completion_length\\\": 256,\\n        \\\"repetition_penalty\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"generation_kwargs\\\": {}\\n      },\\n      \\\"precision\\\": {\\n        \\\"trainer_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16|fp16|fp32|mixed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16|fp16|fp32|closed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"logprob_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16|fp16|fp32|closed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"lm_head_cast_to_fp32\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"tokenizer_and_template\\\": {\\n        \\\"tokenizer_name_or_path\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n        \\\"tokenizer_revision\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"tokenizer_hash\\\": \\\"sha256|null\\\",\\n        \\\"chat_template_hash\\\": \\\"sha256|null\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_trainer_template_shared\\\": true\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"4_zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n      \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n      \\\"definition\\\": \\\"ZVF = fraction of prompt groups whose raw aggregated rewards have zero variance; GU = 1 - ZVF\\\",\\n      \\\"reward_stage\\\": \\\"raw_aggregated_before_scaling\\\",\\n      \\\"tolerance\\\": {\\n        \\\"atol\\\": 1e-12,\\n        \\\"rtol\\\": 0.0\\n      },\\n      \\\"logged_every\\\": \\\"rollout_step\\\",\\n      \\\"telemetry_jsonl\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.steps.v1.jsonl\\\",\\n      \\\"metric_prefix\\\": \\\"min_report_rl\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"5_group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n      \\\"initial_num_generations\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"schedule_type\\\": \\\"fixed|adaptive|dynamic_sampling|custom|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"per_step_logged\\\": true,\\n      \\\"adaptive_rule\\\": null,\\n      \\\"compute_accounting\\\": {\\n        \\\"log_completion_count\\\": true,\\n        \\\"log_token_count\\\": true\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"6_heldout_split\\\": {\\n      \\\"reward_environment\\\": {\\n        \\\"dataset_id\\\": \\\"gsm8k/train[:500]\\\",\\n        \\\"split\\\": \\\"train\\\",\\n        \\\"fingerprint\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"prompt_hash_manifest\\\": \\\"path|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"heldout\\\": {\\n        \\\"dataset_id\\\": \\\"gsm8k/test[:500]\\\",\\n        \\\"split\\\": \\\"test\\\",\\n        \\\"fingerprint\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"n\\\": 500,\\n        \\\"disjoint_from_reward_environment\\\": \\\"true|false|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"eval_harness\\\": \\\"lm-eval-harness:gsm8k@<commit>\\\",\\n        \\\"confidence_interval\\\": {\\n          \\\"method\\\": \\\"bootstrap|wilson|normal|unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"level\\\": 0.95\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"7_decontamination_probes\\\": {\\n      \\\"train_test_overlap\\\": {\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"passed|failed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\\"ngram\\\", \\\"embedding\\\"],\\n        \\\"report_uri\\\": \\\"artifacts/decontam_gsm8k.json\\\",\\n        \\\"max_ngram_overlap\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"max_embedding_similarity\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"reward_parser\\\": {\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n        \\\"version\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"source_digest\\\": \\\"sha256|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"parser_adversarial_probe\\\": {\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"passed|failed|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"report_uri\\\": \\\"artifacts/reward_parser_probe.json\\\",\\n        \\\"format_only_reward_rate\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"length_only_reward_rate\\\": 0.0\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"implementation_context\\\": {\\n    \\\"model\\\": {\\n      \\\"name_or_path\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"revision\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n      \\\"config_hash\\\": \\\"sha256|null\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"peft_lora\\\": {\\n      \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n      \\\"r\\\": 16,\\n      \\\"alpha\\\": 32,\\n      \\\"target_modules\\\": [\\\"q_proj\\\", \\\"v_proj\\\"],\\n      \\\"dropout\\\": 0.0\\n    },\\n    \\\"optimizer\\\": {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"adamw_torch_fused\\\",\\n      \\\"learning_rate\\\": 1e-6,\\n      \\\"weight_decay\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scheduler\\\": \\\"linear\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"unknown_fields\\\": [],\\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [],\\n  \\\"artifacts\\\": {\\n    \\\"manifest_path\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.manifest.v1.json\\\",\\n    \\\"telemetry_path\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.steps.v1.jsonl\\\",\\n    \\\"summary_path\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.summary.v1.json\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe `minimum_reportable_stack` object is deliberately seven-keyed. Additional useful details, such as model, LoRA, optimizer, hardware, and package versions, live under `implementation_context` so the canonical seven-item block stays stable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Per-step ZVF/GU JSONL schema\\n\\nThe plugin writes one JSON object per rollout/scoring event to:\\n\\n```text\\n{output_dir}/min_report_rl/min_report_rl.steps.v1.jsonl\\n```\\n\\nEach line:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"min-report-rl.trl-step.v1\\\",\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid\\\",\\n  \\\"emitted_at_utc\\\": \\\"2026-06-21T00:00:05Z\\\",\\n  \\\"phase\\\": \\\"train\\\",\\n  \\\"global_step\\\": 12,\\n  \\\"rollout_id\\\": 12,\\n  \\\"optimizer_step\\\": 12,\\n  \\\"reused_rollout\\\": false,\\n  \\\"world_size\\\": 8,\\n  \\\"grouping\\\": {\\n    \\\"num_prompt_groups\\\": 64,\\n    \\\"eligible_groups\\\": 64,\\n    \\\"ineligible_groups\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"group_size_min\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"group_size_mean\\\": 8.0,\\n    \\\"group_size_max\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"variable_group_size\\\": false\\n  },\\n  \\\"zvf\\\": {\\n    \\\"value\\\": 0.3125,\\n    \\\"zero_variance_groups\\\": 20,\\n    \\\"tolerance\\\": {\\n      \\\"atol\\\": 1e-12,\\n      \\\"rtol\\\": 0.0\\n    },\\n    \\\"reward_stage\\\": \\\"raw_aggregated_before_scaling\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"gu\\\": {\\n    \\\"value\\\": 0.6875,\\n    \\\"mixed_groups\\\": 44\\n  },\\n  \\\"reward\\\": {\\n    \\\"mean\\\": 0.421875,\\n    \\\"std\\\": 0.493857,\\n    \\\"min\\\": 0.0,\\n    \\\"max\\\": 1.0,\\n    \\\"none_count\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"nan_count\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"component_means\\\": {\\n      \\\"accuracy_reward\\\": 0.421875\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"trl_metrics\\\": {\\n    \\\"frac_reward_zero_std\\\": 0.3125,\\n    \\\"reward\\\": 0.421875,\\n    \\\"reward_std\\\": 0.493857,\\n    \\\"kl\\\": null\\n  },\\n  \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n    \\\"schedule_type\\\": \\\"fixed\\\",\\n    \\\"actual_num_generations\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"adaptive_rule\\\": null,\\n    \\\"changed_this_step\\\": false\\n  },\\n  \\\"collapse_triage\\\": {\\n    \\\"window\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"zvf_threshold\\\": 0.8,\\n    \\\"reward_threshold\\\": 0.05,\\n    \\\"first_five_rule_evaluable\\\": true,\\n    \\\"collapse_candidate\\\": false\\n  },\\n  \\\"privacy\\\": {\\n    \\\"raw_prompts_logged\\\": false,\\n    \\\"prompt_hashes_logged\\\": true,\\n    \\\"prompt_hash_sample\\\": [\\n      \\\"sha256:...\\\"\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf `num_iterations > 1` reuses the same rollout for multiple optimizer steps, the plugin should either log the same `rollout_id` with `reused_rollout=true`, or log once and include:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"applies_to_optimizer_steps\\\": [12, 13, 14]\\n```\\n\\nThat avoids pretending a reused reward batch is fresh signal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Failure and warning behavior\\n\\n`min_report_rl_strict=\\\"warn\\\"` should be the default for usability. The plugin should still emit the JSON block with explicit `unknown` values and a `warnings` array. `min_report_rl_strict=\\\"error\\\"` should fail before training if any of the seven mandatory items are absent, except for values that are genuinely inapplicable and marked as such.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"field\\\": \\\"6_heldout_split.heldout.disjoint_from_reward_environment\\\",\\n  \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n  \\\"message\\\": \\\"Held-out disjointness was not proven. Provide min_report_rl_decontam_report or set strict='off'.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"field\\\": \\\"3_sampler_backend_precision.precision.sampler_dtype\\\",\\n  \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n  \\\"message\\\": \\\"Managed backend did not expose sampler logit precision; marked as closed/unknown.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Minimal upstream patch\\n\\nThe smallest TRL patch is:\\n\\n```python\\n# inside GRPOTrainer, after rewards are aggregated and before reward scaling\\nif self.min_report_rl_observer is not None:\\n    self.min_report_rl_observer.on_grpo_rewards_computed(\\n        GRPORewardTelemetryPayload(\\n            run_id=self.min_report_rl_run_id,\\n            phase=\\\"train\\\",\\n            global_step=self.state.global_step,\\n            rollout_id=self._min_report_rl_rollout_id,\\n            prompt_group_ids=prompt_group_ids,\\n            prompt_hashes=prompt_hashes,\\n            completion_indices=completion_indices,\\n            group_sizes=group_sizes,\\n            raw_aggregated_rewards=raw_rewards,\\n            reward_components=reward_components,\\n            reward_weights=reward_weights,\\n            reward_stage=\\\"raw_aggregated_before_scaling\\\",\\n            world_size=self.accelerator.num_processes,\\n            process_rank=self.accelerator.process_index,\\n        )\\n    )\\n```\\n\\nEverything else can live in the plugin package. The paper’s adoption claim is therefore realistic: the mandatory telemetry is “a few lines over the per-prompt group rewards” plus a manifest writer, not a new training method.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37a265-a630-83ee-bb94-9e901c80a237\",\"title\":\"CLI Tool for GRPO Diff\",\"create\":1782030951.24687,\"update\":1782031358.241174,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a CLI tool that takes two GRPO run manifests and reports which stack levers differ and whether each difference is large enough to flip a comparison. Define the diff taxonomy and output format.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## CLI concept: `grpodiff`\\n\\n`grpodiff` is a Min-Report-RL-aware command-line auditor for GRPO-family runs.\\n\\nIts job is not to say “run A is better than run B.” Its job is to answer the paper’s core question: **are these two runs the same treatment except for the intended method delta, or are there stack differences large enough to flip the comparison?** The paper argues that GRPO labels are under-specified treatments and that loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, usable-signal telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination/parser checks are all mandatory because each can flip a comparison. fileciteturn1file2 fileciteturn1file5 fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. Tool name and basic use\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpodiff compare run_a.yaml run_b.yaml \\\\\\n  --primary heldout.accuracy \\\\\\n  --allow-delta loss.clip_high \\\\\\n  --allow-delta group.dynamic_sampling \\\\\\n  --format text,json \\\\\\n  --out report.grpodiff.json\\n```\\n\\nTagline:\\n\\n```text\\ngrpodiff: diff the stack, not the label.\\n```\\n\\nTypical use cases:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Check whether two nominal GRPO runs are actually comparable.\\ngrpodiff compare managed_grpo.yaml trl_grpo.yaml\\n\\n# Compare GRPO vs DAPO while allowing DAPO’s declared method delta.\\ngrpodiff compare grpo.yaml dapo.yaml \\\\\\n  --allow-profile dapo\\n\\n# Fail CI if a paper artifact has unauditable or flip-capable stack differences.\\ngrpodiff compare baseline.yaml variant.yaml --strict --fail-on flip_plausible\\n\\n# Emit a fillable Min-Report-RL manifest schema.\\ngrpodiff schema --profile minreport > minreport.schema.json\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. Required input: GRPO run manifest\\n\\nEach input is a normalized run manifest. YAML and JSON are both accepted.\\n\\n```yaml\\nschema: grpodiff.run_manifest/v0.1\\n\\nrun:\\n  id: qwen3-8b-gsm8k-grpo-seed42\\n  algorithm_label: GRPO\\n  variant_label: baseline\\n  seed: 42\\n  code_sha: \\\"9d91a...\\\"\\n  trainer: trl\\n  trainer_version: \\\"0.19.0\\\"\\n\\nmodel:\\n  base_checkpoint: Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\n  base_checkpoint_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  tokenizer_id: Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\n  tokenizer_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  chat_template_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  prompt_template_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n\\nloss:\\n  uses_importance_ratio: true\\n  ratio_granularity: token        # token | sequence | none\\n  clip:\\n    enabled: true\\n    low: 0.8\\n    high: 1.2\\n  token_mask: completion_only     # completion_only | whole_sequence | custom\\n  advantage_normalization: per_group\\n  length_normalization: mean_completion_tokens\\n\\nkl:\\n  reference_policy: frozen        # frozen | absent | online | unknown\\n  reference_checkpoint_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  placement: loss                 # loss | reward | none | unknown\\n  coefficient: 0.04\\n  coefficient_schedule: constant\\n  estimator: forward_token        # forward_token | reverse_token | sequence | unknown\\n\\nsampler:\\n  engine: vllm\\n  engine_version: \\\"0.8.5\\\"\\n  backend_kind: open              # open | managed | hybrid\\n  temperature: 0.7\\n  top_p: 0.95\\n  top_k: null\\n  max_tokens: 1024\\n  logit_precision: bf16\\n  trainer_precision: bf16\\n  shares_tokenizer_with_trainer: true\\n  shares_chat_template_with_trainer: true\\n\\ngroup:\\n  schedule:\\n    type: fixed                   # fixed | adaptive | unknown\\n    G: 8\\n  prompts_per_step: 64\\n  rollouts_per_prompt: 8\\n  adaptive_rule: null\\n\\nsignal:\\n  telemetry:\\n    zvf_by_step: \\\"artifacts/zvf.npy\\\"\\n    gu_by_step: \\\"artifacts/gu.npy\\\"\\n    reward_variance_by_step: \\\"artifacts/reward_var.npy\\\"\\n  collapse_rule:\\n    zvf_ge: 0.80\\n    reward_le: 0.05\\n    first_n_steps: 5\\n\\nreward:\\n  train_dataset: GSM8K-500\\n  train_split_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  reward_parser_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  reward_parser_name: exact_math_boxed_answer\\n  reward_environment_id: gsm8k_train_reward_v3\\n\\neval:\\n  primary_metric: heldout.accuracy\\n  heldout_dataset: GSM8K-heldout\\n  heldout_split_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  disjoint_from_reward_env: true\\n  harness_sha: \\\"...\\\"\\n  n: 500\\n  mean: 0.833\\n  ci95: [0.801, 0.861]\\n  checkpoint_selection: fixed_step_30\\n\\ndecontamination:\\n  ngram_probe:\\n    max_overlap: 0.02\\n    passed: true\\n  embedding_probe:\\n    max_similarity: 0.81\\n    passed: true\\n  parser_probe:\\n    adversarial_format_only_pass_rate: 0.00\\n    passed: true\\n\\noptimization:\\n  optimizer: adamw\\n  learning_rate: 1.0e-5\\n  lr_schedule: constant\\n  batch_size_effective: 512\\n  grad_clip: 1.0\\n\\nadapters:\\n  method: lora\\n  rank: 16\\n  alpha: 32\\n  target_modules: [q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj]\\n  dropout: 0.0\\n\\nresults:\\n  train_last10_reward:\\n    mean: 0.844\\n    ci95: null\\n  heldout:\\n    metric: accuracy\\n    mean: 0.833\\n    ci95: [0.801, 0.861]\\n```\\n\\nThe manifest deliberately includes the seven Min-Report-RL fields plus the audit-control fields the paper says should be held fixed in a controlled single-stack comparison: base checkpoint/tokenizer, sampler, KL/reference handling, LoRA targets/rank, optimizer, reward parser, group schedule, held-out slice, harness, and seed sweep. fileciteturn1file4\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Diff taxonomy\\n\\n`grpodiff` separates **what differs** from **whether the difference can flip the comparison**.\\n\\n## 3.1 Lever families\\n\\nThe main taxonomy is:\\n\\n| ID | Lever family | Examples | Default risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `MRS.1.loss` | Loss form | ratio on/off, token vs sequence ratio, clip bounds, token mask, advantage norm, length norm | Critical |\\n| `MRS.2.kl` | Reference policy and KL | frozen vs absent reference, KL in loss vs reward, beta schedule, estimator direction | Critical |\\n| `MRS.3.sampler` | Sampler/backend/precision | vLLM vs managed API, decoding params, bf16 vs fp32 logits, tokenizer/template sharing | Critical |\\n| `MRS.4.signal` | Usable-signal telemetry | ZVF/GU trajectory, reward variance, collapse behavior | Critical if missing or divergent |\\n| `MRS.5.group` | Group-size schedule | fixed G, adaptive G, dynamic sampling, rollout count | Major/Critical |\\n| `MRS.6.eval` | Held-out evaluation | held-out split, harness, sample size, CI, checkpoint selection | Critical |\\n| `MRS.7.decontam` | Decontamination/parser probes | overlap checks, adversarial parser probes, format-only reward pass rate | Major/Critical |\\n| `EXT.model` | Model/tokenizer/prompt identity | checkpoint SHA, tokenizer SHA, chat template, prompt template | Critical |\\n| `EXT.reward` | Reward environment/parser | reward parser code, reward dataset, reward version | Critical |\\n| `EXT.optim` | Optimizer/training plumbing | optimizer, LR schedule, global batch, grad accumulation, precision | Moderate/Major |\\n| `EXT.adapters` | LoRA/adapters | rank, alpha, target modules, dropout | Major |\\n| `EXT.stats` | Statistical comparability | seed count, paired seeds, CI availability, bootstrap policy | Major |\\n| `META.report` | Reporting completeness | missing, unknown, opaque, unverifiable fields | Blocker when on a critical lever |\\n\\nThe seven `MRS.*` families are the Min-Report-RL core. The `EXT.*` families are not a replacement for the paper’s minimum standard; they are the “single-stack audit” controls needed when the user wants to know whether two artifacts are actually comparable.\\n\\n## 3.2 Diff kinds\\n\\nEach field-level difference gets a `diff_kind`:\\n\\n```text\\nequal                 Values are identical after canonicalization.\\nequivalent            Values differ syntactically but normalize to the same meaning.\\nnumeric_delta         Numeric value differs.\\nschedule_delta        Per-step schedule differs.\\nsemantic_delta        Objective, sampling, reward, or evaluation semantics differ.\\nidentity_delta        Hash, checkpoint, tokenizer, parser, dataset, or harness differs.\\ntrace_delta           Telemetry traces differ materially.\\nmissing_left          Field missing in left manifest.\\nmissing_right         Field missing in right manifest.\\nopaque_left           Left run says \\\"managed\\\", \\\"default\\\", or \\\"unknown\\\" for a required field.\\nopaque_right          Right run says \\\"managed\\\", \\\"default\\\", or \\\"unknown\\\" for a required field.\\nunsupported           Tool cannot parse the field.\\n```\\n\\n## 3.3 Confound role\\n\\nA diff is also classified by role:\\n\\n```text\\nintended_method_delta        Declared algorithmic delta, e.g. DAPO clip-higher.\\nuncontrolled_stack_delta     Stack difference not declared as the method delta.\\nmeasurement_delta            Evaluation/reward/harness difference.\\ntelemetry_delta              ZVF/GU or signal-availability difference.\\nreporting_gap                Missing/opaque information.\\nbenign_delta                 Difference judged irrelevant to the comparison.\\n```\\n\\nThis matters because DAPO’s clip-higher setting should not be treated the same way as an accidental tokenizer mismatch. The paper’s audit design holds the stack fixed and lets only the named hook differ; `grpodiff` encodes that distinction. fileciteturn1file7\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. “Large enough to flip” logic\\n\\n`grpodiff` computes a comparison margin, then asks whether each diff has enough plausible effect size to erase or reverse that margin.\\n\\n## 4.1 Comparison margin\\n\\nThe primary metric is chosen in this order:\\n\\n1. User-specified `--primary`.\\n2. Shared held-out metric if both runs have the same held-out split and harness.\\n3. Training last-10 reward, marked `training_only`.\\n4. No metric, marked `unauditable`.\\n\\nFor two runs:\\n\\n```text\\ndelta = metric_right - metric_left\\nmargin_pp = abs(delta) * 100\\nwinner = right if delta > 0 else left\\n```\\n\\nIf both runs report confidence intervals:\\n\\n```text\\nci_overlap = intervals_overlap(left.ci95, right.ci95)\\ndecisive_margin_pp = distance between non-overlapping CI edges, else 0\\n```\\n\\nIf the CIs overlap, `grpodiff` reports that the comparison is already statistically fragile before stack analysis.\\n\\n## 4.2 Effect evidence hierarchy\\n\\nFor each diff, `grpodiff` tries to estimate flip potential using this hierarchy:\\n\\n```text\\nLevel 4: User calibration\\n  Empirical ablation data supplied with --calibration.\\n\\nLevel 3: Corpus calibration\\n  Known internal lab corpus for the same lever/task/model class.\\n\\nLevel 2: Manifest-derived signal\\n  Example: ZVF/GU divergence, collapse detection, held-out/training divergence.\\n\\nLevel 1: Policy prior\\n  Conservative default risk band by lever family.\\n\\nLevel 0: Unscorable\\n  Missing, opaque, or unsupported.\\n```\\n\\nThe paper’s concrete numbers are used as **risk anchors**, not universal effect sizes. For example, the paper reports a matched visible GRPO setup with a backend swap producing 84.4% vs 5.0% last-10 training reward, and warns that 2–5 point GRPO-family gains can be smaller than stack effects. `grpodiff` therefore treats backend/sampler/objective/evaluation mismatches as flip-capable for ordinary GRPO paper margins unless calibration proves otherwise. fileciteturn1file14 fileciteturn1file6\\n\\n## 4.3 Default flip bands\\n\\nThe default policy is intentionally conservative:\\n\\n| Severity | Default plausible impact band | Flip verdict rule |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| `blocker` | unknown / unbounded | `unknown_blocking` |\\n| `critical` | 5 pp to unbounded | `flip_capable` if margin ≤ 5 pp; `flip_plausible` otherwise |\\n| `major` | 2–15 pp | `flip_capable` if margin ≤ 2 pp; `flip_plausible` if margin ≤ 15 pp |\\n| `moderate` | 0.5–5 pp | `flip_capable` if margin ≤ 0.5 pp; `flip_plausible` if margin ≤ 5 pp |\\n| `minor` | 0–1 pp | `flip_plausible` only if margin ≤ 1 pp |\\n| `info` | 0 pp | `not_flip_capable` |\\n\\nOutput verdicts:\\n\\n```text\\nflip_capable       Difference alone is large enough under the default or calibrated bound.\\nflip_plausible     Difference could flip the result; rerun or calibrate.\\nborderline         Difference is near the comparison margin.\\nnot_flip_capable   Difference is unlikely to flip this specific margin.\\nunknown_blocking   Field is missing/opaque on a lever that the standard requires.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Human-readable output\\n\\nDefault terminal output:\\n\\n```text\\ngrpodiff 0.1.0\\nleft:  grpo_trl_seed42.yaml      label=GRPO     run_id=trl-qwen3-gsm8k-42\\nright: dapo_paper_seed42.yaml    label=DAPO     run_id=dapo-qwen3-gsm8k-42\\n\\nPRIMARY COMPARISON\\n  metric: heldout.accuracy\\n  left:   82.0%  CI95=[78.4, 85.1]\\n  right:  83.3%  CI95=[79.8, 86.2]\\n  margin: +1.3 pp right over left\\n  statistical_status: fragile_ci_overlap\\n  comparison_status: NOT INTERPRETABLE AS METHOD EFFECT\\n\\nSUMMARY\\n  min-report completeness:\\n    left:  6/7 complete\\n    right: 5/7 complete\\n\\n  diffs:\\n    total: 14\\n    allowed method deltas: 2\\n    uncontrolled stack deltas: 9\\n    measurement deltas: 2\\n    reporting gaps: 1\\n\\n  flip assessment:\\n    flip_capable:     5\\n    flip_plausible:   3\\n    borderline:       1\\n    not_flip_capable: 2\\n    unknown_blocking: 3\\n\\nVERDICT\\n  CONFOUNDED\\n  The observed margin is 1.3 pp. At least five uncontrolled stack differences\\n  are independently large enough to flip a 1.3 pp comparison.\\n```\\n\\nDiff table:\\n\\n```text\\n┌───────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬───────────────┬───────────────┬────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐\\n│ Lever         │ Field                        │ Left          │ Right         │ Flip verdict   │ Why it matters                       │\\n├───────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼───────────────┼───────────────┼────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤\\n│ MRS.1.loss    │ loss.token_mask              │ whole_sequence│ completion_only│ flip_capable  │ Changes which tokens carry gradient. │\\n│ MRS.2.kl      │ kl.placement                 │ loss          │ reward        │ flip_capable   │ Changes effective objective.         │\\n│ MRS.3.sampler │ sampler.engine               │ trainer.generate│ vllm        │ flip_capable   │ Changes rollout distribution.        │\\n│ MRS.3.sampler │ sampler.logit_precision      │ fp32          │ bf16          │ flip_plausible │ Can change mixed-group probability.  │\\n│ MRS.4.signal  │ signal.zvf_mean_step_1_5     │ 0.84          │ 0.31          │ flip_capable   │ Left has collapse-level low signal.  │\\n│ MRS.5.group   │ group.schedule               │ fixed G=8     │ adaptive 4-16 │ flip_capable   │ Changes usable-group probability.    │\\n│ MRS.6.eval    │ eval.heldout_split_sha       │ gsm8k-a...    │ gsm8k-b...    │ unknown_blocking│ Metrics are not same measurement.   │\\n│ MRS.7.decontam│ parser_probe.passed          │ missing       │ true          │ unknown_blocking│ Reward hacking not excluded.        │\\n│ EXT.adapters  │ adapters.target_modules      │ q,v           │ q,k,v,o       │ flip_plausible │ Changes trainable subspace.          │\\n└───────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴───────────────┴───────────────┴────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘\\n```\\n\\nRecommended action block:\\n\\n```text\\nRECOMMENDED RERUN PLAN\\n  1. Match eval.heldout_split_sha and eval.harness_sha before comparing capability.\\n  2. Match loss.token_mask, kl.placement, sampler.engine, and group.schedule.\\n  3. Recompute ZVF/GU trajectories. If first-five-step ZVF >= 80% and reward <= 5%,\\n     mark the run collapsed rather than treating final reward as method evidence.\\n  4. Treat DAPO clip_high and dynamic_sampling as allowed method deltas only if declared\\n     in --allow-profile dapo.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. JSON output format\\n\\nMachine-readable output is the canonical artifact.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"grpodiff.report/v0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"tool\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"grpodiff\\\",\\n    \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\",\\n    \\\"policy\\\": \\\"minreport-default/v0.1\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"inputs\\\": {\\n    \\\"left\\\": {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"grpo_trl_seed42.yaml\\\",\\n      \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"trl-qwen3-gsm8k-42\\\",\\n      \\\"algorithm_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"right\\\": {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"dapo_paper_seed42.yaml\\\",\\n      \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"dapo-qwen3-gsm8k-42\\\",\\n      \\\"algorithm_label\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"comparison\\\": {\\n    \\\"primary_metric\\\": \\\"heldout.accuracy\\\",\\n    \\\"left_value\\\": 0.82,\\n    \\\"right_value\\\": 0.833,\\n    \\\"delta\\\": 0.013,\\n    \\\"margin_pp\\\": 1.3,\\n    \\\"winner\\\": \\\"right\\\",\\n    \\\"ci_status\\\": \\\"overlap\\\",\\n    \\\"metric_comparability\\\": \\\"same_metric_but_unverified_split\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"confounded\\\",\\n    \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Do not attribute the observed margin to the algorithm label.\\\",\\n    \\\"min_report_completeness\\\": {\\n      \\\"left_complete\\\": 6,\\n      \\\"right_complete\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"required\\\": 7\\n    },\\n    \\\"diff_counts\\\": {\\n      \\\"total\\\": 14,\\n      \\\"allowed_method_delta\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"uncontrolled_stack_delta\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"measurement_delta\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"reporting_gap\\\": 1\\n    },\\n    \\\"flip_counts\\\": {\\n      \\\"flip_capable\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"flip_plausible\\\": 3,\\n      \\\"borderline\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"not_flip_capable\\\": 2,\\n      \\\"unknown_blocking\\\": 3\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"diffs\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever_id\\\": \\\"MRS.1.loss.token_mask\\\",\\n      \\\"lever_family\\\": \\\"MRS.1.loss\\\",\\n      \\\"field_path\\\": \\\"loss.token_mask\\\",\\n      \\\"diff_kind\\\": \\\"semantic_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"uncontrolled_stack_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": \\\"whole_sequence\\\",\\n      \\\"right\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n      \\\"flip_test\\\": {\\n        \\\"comparison_margin_pp\\\": 1.3,\\n        \\\"evidence_level\\\": \\\"policy_prior\\\",\\n        \\\"plausible_impact_band_pp\\\": [5.0, null],\\n        \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"flip_capable\\\",\\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Token mask changes which tokens carry gradient; this is a different objective, not a cosmetic hyperparameter.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Rerun one arm with the token mask matched, or mark the comparison confounded.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever_id\\\": \\\"MRS.2.kl.placement\\\",\\n      \\\"lever_family\\\": \\\"MRS.2.kl\\\",\\n      \\\"field_path\\\": \\\"kl.placement\\\",\\n      \\\"diff_kind\\\": \\\"semantic_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"uncontrolled_stack_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": \\\"loss\\\",\\n      \\\"right\\\": \\\"reward\\\",\\n      \\\"flip_test\\\": {\\n        \\\"comparison_margin_pp\\\": 1.3,\\n        \\\"evidence_level\\\": \\\"policy_prior\\\",\\n        \\\"plausible_impact_band_pp\\\": [5.0, null],\\n        \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"flip_capable\\\",\\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"KL placement changes the effective objective and exploration behavior.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Match KL placement and beta schedule before interpreting the result.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever_id\\\": \\\"MRS.4.signal.zvf_first5\\\",\\n      \\\"lever_family\\\": \\\"MRS.4.signal\\\",\\n      \\\"field_path\\\": \\\"signal.telemetry.zvf_by_step\\\",\\n      \\\"diff_kind\\\": \\\"trace_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"telemetry_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": {\\n        \\\"zvf_mean_first5\\\": 0.84,\\n        \\\"reward_mean_first5\\\": 0.04\\n      },\\n      \\\"right\\\": {\\n        \\\"zvf_mean_first5\\\": 0.31,\\n        \\\"reward_mean_first5\\\": 0.22\\n      },\\n      \\\"derived\\\": {\\n        \\\"left_collapse_triage\\\": true,\\n        \\\"right_collapse_triage\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"flip_test\\\": {\\n        \\\"comparison_margin_pp\\\": 1.3,\\n        \\\"evidence_level\\\": \\\"manifest_derived\\\",\\n        \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"flip_capable\\\",\\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"One run has collapse-level zero-variance signal while the other has usable groups.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Do not compare final reward until both runs have usable-signal trajectories.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"allowed_deltas\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"field_path\\\": \\\"loss.clip.high\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"DAPO profile\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"field_path\\\": \\\"group.dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"DAPO profile\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"exit_code\\\": 2\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Overall verdict taxonomy\\n\\nThe top-level report verdict is one of:\\n\\n```text\\nstack_matched\\n  No uncontrolled stack differences, no blocking missing fields, same evaluation.\\n\\ncontrolled_method_comparison\\n  Only declared method deltas differ; comparison is interpretable subject to statistics.\\n\\nstatistically_fragile\\n  Stack is matched, but primary metric CIs overlap or seed count is insufficient.\\n\\nmeasurement_mismatch\\n  Reward/evaluation/harness/split differs, so the reported outcome is not the same quantity.\\n\\nconfounded\\n  One or more uncontrolled stack differences are flip_capable or flip_plausible.\\n\\nunauditable\\n  Critical Min-Report-RL fields are missing or opaque.\\n```\\n\\nExit codes:\\n\\n```text\\n0  stack_matched or controlled_method_comparison\\n1  non-flip-capable differences found\\n2  flip_plausible or flip_capable uncontrolled differences found\\n3  unauditable due to missing/opaque critical fields\\n4  schema or parse error\\n5  internal error\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Built-in profiles\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpodiff profiles\\n```\\n\\nReturns:\\n\\n```text\\nminreport\\n  Compare only the seven Min-Report-RL required fields.\\n\\naudit\\n  Min-Report-RL plus checkpoint, tokenizer, prompt template, LoRA, optimizer,\\n  reward parser, harness, seed/statistics, and code identity.\\n\\ndapo\\n  audit profile, but allows:\\n    loss.clip.high\\n    group.dynamic_sampling\\n\\ngspo\\n  audit profile, but allows:\\n    loss.ratio_granularity = sequence\\n\\ndrgrpo\\n  audit profile, but allows:\\n    loss.length_normalization\\n    loss.advantage_normalization\\n\\nmad-grpo\\n  audit profile, but allows:\\n    loss.diversity_term\\n    reward.multi_agent_aggregation\\n```\\n\\nThese profiles reflect the paper’s framing that GRPO-family variants are often small deltas on a base GRPO loop, while uncontrolled stack effects can be larger than the claimed method effect. fileciteturn1file6\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9. One-line report mode\\n\\nFor paper artifact checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpodiff compare a.yaml b.yaml --one-line\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```text\\nCONFOUNDED: margin=1.3pp; 5 flip_capable uncontrolled stack diffs:\\nloss.token_mask, kl.placement, sampler.engine, signal.zvf_first5, group.schedule.\\nDo not attribute this comparison to algorithm_label.\\n```\\n\\nThis is the CLI version of the paper’s central standard: **report the stack, not just the label**.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379b6e-4870-83e8-ac20-223ef5138950\",\"title\":\"MIN-REPORT-RL Plugin Design\",\"create\":1782029167.690013,\"update\":1782029627.579695,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached MIN-REPORT-RL position paper. Invent a TRL plugin that automatically emits the 7-item minimum-reportable-stack block as JSON at the start of every GRPO run and logs per-step ZVF/GU. Specify the hooks, config flags, and output schema.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Plugin: `trl-min-report-rl`\\n\\n`trl-min-report-rl` is a small TRL extension for `GRPOTrainer` that emits a machine-readable Min-Report-RL manifest before the first rollout and logs usable-signal telemetry, especially ZVF/GU, for every optimizer step. The attached position paper’s required seven fields are loss form, reference/KL handling, sampler/backend/precision, per-step ZVF/GU, group-size schedule, held-out split, and decontamination/parser probes; it also explicitly proposes a TRL flag named `report_min_report_rl=True` that writes the seven-field block and logs the ZVF/GU trajectory. fileciteturn0file0 fileciteturn1file4 fileciteturn1file0\\n\\nCurrent TRL already has the right raw material: `GRPOConfig` exposes fields such as `num_generations`, `temperature`, `top_p`, `use_vllm`, `beta`, `epsilon`, `epsilon_high`, `importance_sampling_level`, `scale_rewards`, `loss_type`, and `mask_truncated_completions`, and TRL’s logging path supports `report_to` for trackers such as Trackio, W&B, and TensorBoard. citeturn724457view5turn390820view1 The one missing piece is an explicit reward-group hook: TRL computes and gathers per-completion rewards before group normalization, which is exactly where ZVF/GU should be computed. citeturn989954view4\\n\\n---\\n\\n## User-facing API\\n\\n```python\\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\\nfrom trl import GRPOConfig\\nfrom trl_min_report_rl import MinReportGRPOTrainer, MinReportRLConfig\\n\\nargs = GRPOConfig(\\n    output_dir=\\\"runs/qwen-grpo\\\",\\n    num_generations=8,\\n    loss_type=\\\"dapo\\\",\\n    beta=0.0,\\n    use_vllm=True,\\n    vllm_mode=\\\"colocate\\\",\\n    temperature=1.0,\\n    top_p=1.0,\\n    report_to=[\\\"wandb\\\"],\\n\\n    # Proposed upstream TRL flags\\n    report_min_report_rl=True,\\n    min_report_rl_mode=\\\"warn\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_log_every_step=True,\\n)\\n\\nmin_report = MinReportRLConfig(\\n    heldout_manifest=\\\"eval/gsm8k_heldout_manifest.json\\\",\\n    decontamination_manifest=\\\"eval/gsm8k_decontam_probe.json\\\",\\n    reward_parser_name=\\\"gsm8k_exact_answer_v3\\\",\\n    reward_parser_probe=\\\"eval/parser_probe_results.json\\\",\\n)\\n\\ntrainer = MinReportGRPOTrainer(\\n    model=\\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n    args=args,\\n    reward_funcs=[reward_fn],\\n    train_dataset=load_dataset(\\\"gsm8k\\\", \\\"main\\\", split=\\\"train[:500]\\\"),\\n    eval_dataset=load_dataset(\\\"gsm8k\\\", \\\"main\\\", split=\\\"test[:500]\\\"),\\n    min_report_rl=min_report,\\n)\\n\\ntrainer.train()\\n```\\n\\nThe plugin writes:\\n\\n```text\\nruns/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl.json\\nruns/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl\\nruns/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl.summary.json\\n```\\n\\nThe first JSONL line is a `run_start` event containing the same seven-item manifest; all subsequent lines are `step_telemetry` events.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Hooks\\n\\n### 1. `on_grpo_init_end(trainer)`\\n\\nRuns after `GRPOTrainer.__init__` has resolved the model, tokenizer/processor, PEFT config, reward functions, vLLM/generation config, reference model, precision settings, and optimizer arguments.\\n\\nPurpose: collect all static stack fields that should not change during training.\\n\\nCaptured fields include model ID, model revision if available, tokenizer ID, tokenizer hash, chat template hash, reward function identities, LoRA target modules/rank, dtype, optimizer, sampler backend, decoding parameters, loss type, clip settings, KL/reference settings, and initial group size.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. `on_train_begin(trainer)`\\n\\nRuns before the first call to `_prepare_inputs` or `_generate_and_score_completions`.\\n\\nPurpose: emit the seven-item manifest before the first rollout. Only rank 0 writes files and experiment-tracker artifacts; all ranks receive the manifest hash through a barrier/broadcast.\\n\\nOutputs:\\n\\n```text\\nmin_report_rl.json\\nmin_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl line 1: {\\\"event\\\": \\\"run_start\\\", ...}\\ntrainer log/config key: min_report_rl/block\\n```\\n\\nIf `resume_from_checkpoint` is set, the plugin emits a new `run_start` event with:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"resume\\\": {\\n  \\\"resumed\\\": true,\\n  \\\"checkpoint\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n  \\\"previous_manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"...\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. `on_grpo_rewards_computed(...)`\\n\\nInserted immediately after global reward gathering and before advantage normalization. This is the critical hook.\\n\\nProposed signature:\\n\\n```python\\ndef on_grpo_rewards_computed(\\n    trainer,\\n    *,\\n    mode: str,                         # \\\"train\\\" or \\\"eval\\\"\\n    global_step: int,\\n    rollout_step: int,\\n    num_generations: int,              # G\\n    prompts: list,\\n    completions: list,\\n    completion_ids: list[list[int]],\\n    rewards_per_func: torch.Tensor,    # shape [num_prompts * G, num_reward_funcs]\\n    reward_func_names: list[str],\\n    reward_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    unscorable_mask: torch.Tensor | None,\\n) -> MinReportStepStats:\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\nFor an external plugin without an upstream TRL hook, `MinReportGRPOTrainer` can subclass `GRPOTrainer` and override `_calculate_rewards` or `_generate_and_score_completions`. Upstream, the cleaner patch is one line near the existing reward-gather point: call `self.min_report_rl.on_grpo_rewards_computed(...)` after `rewards_per_func = gather(rewards_per_func)`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. `on_training_step_end(trainer, stats)`\\n\\nRuns after each optimizer step.\\n\\nPurpose: write one JSONL row per optimizer step. If TRL reuses a rollout across `steps_per_generation` or `num_iterations`, the plugin re-emits the cached ZVF/GU stats with:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"rollout_reused\\\": true\\n```\\n\\nThis matters because TRL can generate once and reuse/buffer completions across multiple updates; the plugin logs per optimizer step while also recording the rollout step that produced the rewards. TRL’s own source describes this buffering/reuse in `_prepare_inputs`. citeturn989954view1\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. `on_log(trainer, logs)`\\n\\nAdds aggregated scalar keys to normal TRL logs:\\n\\n```text\\nmin_report_rl/zvf\\nmin_report_rl/gu\\nmin_report_rl/group_reward_std_mean\\nmin_report_rl/group_reward_std_p50\\nmin_report_rl/mixed_group_count\\nmin_report_rl/zero_signal_group_count\\nmin_report_rl/unscorable_group_count\\nmin_report_rl/group_size\\n```\\n\\nThe JSONL file remains the authoritative per-step trace. Tracker logs may be averaged by TRL logging intervals; TRL’s current `log` method averages metrics stored in `_metrics`. citeturn989954view3\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. `on_evaluate(trainer, metrics)`\\n\\nUpdates held-out evidence in the summary file:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"heldout_split\\\": {\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"evaluated\\\",\\n  \\\"n\\\": 500,\\n  \\\"metric\\\": \\\"exact_match\\\",\\n  \\\"value\\\": 0.833,\\n  \\\"ci95\\\": [0.801, 0.865],\\n  \\\"harness\\\": \\\"lm-eval-gsm8k-v2\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe start-of-run manifest still exists even when no held-out evaluation is configured; in that case the field is explicit, not omitted:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 7. `on_train_end(trainer)`\\n\\nWrites `min_report_rl.summary.json`, including manifest hash, telemetry hash, first-five-step collapse triage, final ZVF/GU summary, held-out evaluation status, decontamination status, and missing-field report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Config flags\\n\\nProposed additions to `GRPOConfig`:\\n\\n```python\\nreport_min_report_rl: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_mode: Literal[\\\"off\\\", \\\"warn\\\", \\\"enforce\\\"] = \\\"warn\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_output_dir: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_emit_to: list[Literal[\\\"file\\\", \\\"trainer_log\\\", \\\"wandb\\\", \\\"trackio\\\", \\\"tensorboard\\\", \\\"hub\\\"]] = [\\\"file\\\", \\\"trainer_log\\\"]\\nmin_report_rl_manifest_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.json\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_telemetry_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_summary_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.summary.json\\\"\\n\\n# ZVF/GU behavior\\nmin_report_rl_log_every_step: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_zvf_atol: float = 0.0\\nmin_report_rl_zvf_rtol: float = 0.0\\nmin_report_rl_nan_policy: Literal[\\\"zero_signal\\\", \\\"drop_group\\\", \\\"separate\\\"] = \\\"zero_signal\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_dense_reward_mode: Literal[\\\"auto\\\", \\\"zvf\\\", \\\"reward_variance\\\"] = \\\"auto\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_group_by: Literal[\\\"prompt_index\\\", \\\"prompt_hash\\\"] = \\\"prompt_index\\\"\\n\\n# Privacy and hashes\\nmin_report_rl_privacy: Literal[\\\"hash_only\\\", \\\"redact\\\", \\\"raw\\\"] = \\\"hash_only\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_hash_prompts: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_hash_datasets: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_hash_reward_funcs: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_hash_chat_template: bool = True\\n\\n# Held-out and decontamination\\nmin_report_rl_require_heldout: bool = False\\nmin_report_rl_heldout_manifest: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_require_decontamination: bool = False\\nmin_report_rl_decontamination_manifest: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_reward_parser_name: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_reward_parser_version: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_parser_probe_results: str | None = None\\n\\n# Resume behavior\\nmin_report_rl_emit_on_resume: Literal[\\\"append\\\", \\\"overwrite_if_same\\\", \\\"always_new\\\"] = \\\"append\\\"\\n```\\n\\nRecommended defaults:\\n\\n```python\\nreport_min_report_rl=True\\nmin_report_rl_mode=\\\"warn\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_log_every_step=True\\nmin_report_rl_privacy=\\\"hash_only\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`warn` mode emits the manifest even when fields are missing. `enforce` mode raises before training if mandatory stack fields are unknown, except held-out/decontamination fields can be allowed as `not_configured` unless their `require_*` flag is true.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ZVF/GU computation\\n\\nThe plugin computes ZVF from scalar rewards after reward parser execution and reward weighting, but before advantage normalization.\\n\\nFor a step with `N` prompts and group size `G`, reshape scalar rewards as:\\n\\n```python\\ngroup_rewards = rewards.view(N, G)\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```python\\nvalid = ~torch.isnan(group_rewards)\\nvalid_count = valid.sum(dim=1)\\n\\ngroup_min = torch.nanmin(group_rewards, dim=1).values\\ngroup_max = torch.nanmax(group_rewards, dim=1).values\\nreward_span = group_max - group_min\\n\\nzero_signal = (\\n    (valid_count < 2) |\\n    (reward_span <= zvf_atol + zvf_rtol * torch.nanmean(torch.abs(group_rewards), dim=1))\\n)\\n\\nzvf = zero_signal.float().mean()\\ngu = 1.0 - zvf\\n```\\n\\nThe default `nan_policy=\\\"zero_signal\\\"` treats unscorable groups as zero-signal, because they cannot supply usable group-relative advantage. The plugin also logs `zvf_strict_identical_valid_only`, which excludes groups with fewer than two valid rewards, so papers can distinguish parser failures from true all-identical reward groups.\\n\\nFor dense process rewards, `dense_reward_mode=\\\"auto\\\"` switches the reported usable-signal metric to group reward variance when almost no groups are exactly identical. This follows the paper’s caveat that ZVF/GU is the default usable-signal trajectory, but dense process rewards may require reward-variance or gradient-variance substitutes. fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## `min_report_rl.json` output schema\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"min-report-rl/0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"kind\\\": \\\"grpo_run_manifest\\\",\\n  \\\"created_at_utc\\\": \\\"2026-06-21T08:05:52Z\\\",\\n  \\\"run\\\": {\\n    \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid-or-tracker-run-id\\\",\\n    \\\"trainer\\\": \\\"trl.GRPOTrainer\\\",\\n    \\\"plugin\\\": \\\"trl-min-report-rl\\\",\\n    \\\"plugin_version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\",\\n    \\\"trl_version\\\": \\\"1.x\\\",\\n    \\\"transformers_version\\\": \\\"x.y.z\\\",\\n    \\\"accelerate_version\\\": \\\"x.y.z\\\",\\n    \\\"python_version\\\": \\\"3.12\\\",\\n    \\\"distributed\\\": {\\n      \\\"world_size\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"rank0_only_writes\\\": true,\\n      \\\"deepspeed\\\": null,\\n      \\\"fsdp\\\": null\\n    },\\n    \\\"seed\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"data_seed\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"output_dir\\\": \\\"runs/qwen-grpo\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"min_report_stack\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"algorithm_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n      \\\"loss_type\\\": \\\"dapo\\\",\\n      \\\"uses_importance_ratio\\\": true,\\n      \\\"importance_sampling_level\\\": \\\"token\\\",\\n      \\\"num_iterations_per_rollout\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"clip\\\": {\\n        \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n        \\\"epsilon_low\\\": 0.2,\\n        \\\"epsilon_high\\\": 0.28,\\n        \\\"delta\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"token_mask\\\": {\\n        \\\"policy_loss_mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n        \\\"mask_truncated_completions\\\": false,\\n        \\\"top_entropy_quantile\\\": 1.0\\n      },\\n      \\\"advantage_normalization\\\": {\\n        \\\"scale_rewards\\\": \\\"group\\\",\\n        \\\"multi_objective_aggregation\\\": \\\"sum_then_normalize\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"source_fields\\\": [\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.loss_type\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.epsilon\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.epsilon_high\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.importance_sampling_level\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.scale_rewards\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"2_reference_policy_and_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"reference_policy\\\": {\\n        \\\"retained\\\": false,\\n        \\\"mode\\\": \\\"none_when_beta_0\\\",\\n        \\\"sync_ref_model\\\": false,\\n        \\\"ref_model_sync_steps\\\": null,\\n        \\\"ref_model_mixup_alpha\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"kl\\\": {\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n        \\\"beta\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"schedule\\\": \\\"constant\\\",\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_kl_approximation\\\",\\n        \\\"granularity\\\": \\\"per_token\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"source_fields\\\": [\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.beta\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.sync_ref_model\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.ref_model_sync_steps\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"3_sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n      \\\"backend\\\": {\\n        \\\"engine\\\": \\\"vllm\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_mode\\\": \\\"colocate\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_model_impl\\\": \\\"vllm\\\",\\n        \\\"custom_rollout_func\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"decoding\\\": {\\n        \\\"num_generations\\\": 8,\\n        \\\"max_completion_length\\\": 256,\\n        \\\"temperature\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"top_k\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"min_p\\\": null,\\n        \\\"repetition_penalty\\\": 1.0\\n      },\\n      \\\"precision\\\": {\\n        \\\"trainer_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"tf32\\\": true,\\n        \\\"cast_lm_head_to_fp32\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"tokenizer_and_template\\\": {\\n        \\\"model_id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n        \\\"tokenizer_id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n        \\\"tokenizer_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n        \\\"chat_template_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_trainer_template_shared\\\": true\\n      },\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"4_zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n      \\\"telemetry_file\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl\\\",\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"zvf\\\": \\\"fraction of prompt groups with zero usable reward variance\\\",\\n        \\\"gu\\\": \\\"1 - zvf\\\",\\n        \\\"group_reward_std\\\": \\\"std of scalar rewards within each prompt group\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"zvf_definition\\\": {\\n        \\\"computed_on\\\": \\\"weighted_scalar_rewards_before_advantage_normalization\\\",\\n        \\\"nan_policy\\\": \\\"zero_signal\\\",\\n        \\\"atol\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"rtol\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"unscorable_groups_count_as_zero_signal\\\": true\\n      },\\n      \\\"collapse_triage\\\": {\\n        \\\"first_five_rule_enabled\\\": true,\\n        \\\"collapse_if_zvf_ge\\\": 0.8,\\n        \\\"and_reward_le\\\": 0.05\\n      }\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"5_group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"initial_group_size\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"schedule_type\\\": \\\"fixed\\\",\\n      \\\"adaptive_rule\\\": null,\\n      \\\"per_step_logged\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_fields\\\": [\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.num_generations\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"6_heldout_split\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\",\\n      \\\"train_eval_disjoint\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"heldout_dataset\\\": null,\\n      \\\"heldout_split\\\": null,\\n      \\\"sample_size\\\": null,\\n      \\\"metric\\\": null,\\n      \\\"confidence_interval\\\": null,\\n      \\\"harness\\\": null,\\n      \\\"manifest\\\": null\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"7_decontamination_probe\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\",\\n      \\\"train_test_overlap_probe\\\": {\\n        \\\"method\\\": null,\\n        \\\"result\\\": null,\\n        \\\"manifest\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"parser_probe\\\": {\\n        \\\"reward_parser_name\\\": \\\"gsm8k_exact_answer_v3\\\",\\n        \\\"reward_parser_version\\\": null,\\n        \\\"format_only_probe_result\\\": null,\\n        \\\"manifest\\\": null\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"capture_quality\\\": {\\n    \\\"complete_items\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"partial_items\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"not_configured_items\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"missing_required_if_enforce\\\": [\\n      \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\",\\n      \\\"heldout_split.manifest\\\",\\n      \\\"decontamination_probe.manifest\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## `min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl` schema\\n\\nFirst line:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"min-report-rl/telemetry/0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"event\\\": \\\"run_start\\\",\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid-or-tracker-run-id\\\",\\n  \\\"global_step\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n  \\\"manifest_path\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.json\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nPer-step line:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"min-report-rl/telemetry/0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"event\\\": \\\"step_telemetry\\\",\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid-or-tracker-run-id\\\",\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"train\\\",\\n  \\\"global_step\\\": 17,\\n  \\\"optimizer_step\\\": 17,\\n  \\\"rollout_step\\\": 15,\\n  \\\"rollout_reused\\\": false,\\n  \\\"group_size\\\": 8,\\n  \\\"num_prompt_groups\\\": 64,\\n  \\\"num_completions\\\": 512,\\n\\n  \\\"zvf\\\": 0.3125,\\n  \\\"gu\\\": 0.6875,\\n  \\\"zvf_strict_identical_valid_only\\\": 0.296875,\\n  \\\"zero_signal_group_count\\\": 20,\\n  \\\"mixed_group_count\\\": 44,\\n  \\\"unscorable_group_count\\\": 1,\\n\\n  \\\"reward\\\": {\\n    \\\"mean\\\": 0.421,\\n    \\\"std\\\": 0.494,\\n    \\\"group_std_mean\\\": 0.318,\\n    \\\"group_std_p50\\\": 0.353,\\n    \\\"group_std_min\\\": 0.0,\\n    \\\"group_std_max\\\": 0.535\\n  },\\n\\n  \\\"reward_functions\\\": {\\n    \\\"accuracy_reward\\\": {\\n      \\\"mean\\\": 0.421,\\n      \\\"std\\\": 0.494,\\n      \\\"nan_count\\\": 0\\n    }\\n  },\\n\\n  \\\"collapse_triage\\\": {\\n    \\\"first_five_window\\\": false,\\n    \\\"collapse_flag\\\": false,\\n    \\\"reason\\\": null\\n  },\\n\\n  \\\"privacy\\\": {\\n    \\\"prompt_logging\\\": \\\"hash_only\\\",\\n    \\\"prompt_hash_salt\\\": \\\"run_salt_sha256:...\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Minimal upstream patch shape\\n\\n```python\\n# trl/trainer/grpo_config.py\\n@dataclass\\nclass GRPOConfig(...):\\n    report_min_report_rl: bool = True\\n    min_report_rl_mode: str = \\\"warn\\\"\\n    min_report_rl_log_every_step: bool = True\\n    min_report_rl_output_dir: str | None = None\\n    min_report_rl_zvf_atol: float = 0.0\\n    min_report_rl_zvf_rtol: float = 0.0\\n    min_report_rl_nan_policy: str = \\\"zero_signal\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```python\\n# trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py\\nclass GRPOTrainer(...):\\n    def __init__(..., min_report_rl: MinReportRLConfig | None = None, ...):\\n        ...\\n        self.min_report_rl = maybe_build_min_report_plugin(args, min_report_rl)\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_grpo_init_end(self)\\n\\n    def train(...):\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_train_begin(self)\\n        return super().train(...)\\n\\n    def _calculate_rewards(...):\\n        rewards_per_func = super_reward_calculation(...)\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_grpo_rewards_computed(\\n            self,\\n            mode=\\\"train\\\" if self.model.training else \\\"eval\\\",\\n            global_step=self.state.global_step,\\n            num_generations=self.num_generations if self.model.training else self.num_generations_eval,\\n            prompts=prompts,\\n            completions=completions,\\n            completion_ids=completion_ids_list,\\n            rewards_per_func=rewards_per_func,\\n            reward_func_names=self.reward_func_names,\\n            reward_weights=self.reward_weights,\\n        )\\n        return rewards_per_func\\n\\n    def training_step(...):\\n        out = super().training_step(...)\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_training_step_end(self)\\n        return out\\n\\n    def log(self, logs, start_time=None):\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_log(self, logs)\\n        return super().log(logs, start_time)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Enforcement behavior\\n\\nIn `warn` mode, the plugin never blocks training. It emits the JSON with explicit `unknown`, `partial`, or `not_configured` statuses.\\n\\nIn `enforce` mode, it blocks before the first rollout if items 1–5 are incomplete, because those are directly available inside TRL. It blocks on items 6–7 only when `min_report_rl_require_heldout=True` or `min_report_rl_require_decontamination=True`.\\n\\nThat gives the paper exactly what it asks for: the cost is a JSON block plus a per-step usable-signal trace, while the implementation stays local to the existing GRPO reward/logging path.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379b30-3280-83ee-a374-ea8cebc756d7\",\"title\":\"Automated Compliance Checker\",\"create\":1782029105.728427,\"update\":1782029485.849985,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent an automated compliance checker that scans a GRPO paper or repo and scores it against the 7-item MIN-REPORT-RL checklist. Provide the scoring rubric, heuristics for each item, and example outputs.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/CHECKLIST.md\\nLines: 1-168\\n````md\\n  1 | # MIN-REPORT-RL — Author Checklist\\n  2 | \\n  3 | A copy-pasteable minimum-reportable-stack for **any GRPO-family RL post-training paper**\\n  4 | (GRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO, …).\\n  5 | \\n  6 | **Why this exists.** Algorithm labels are under-specified treatments. In a controlled audit,\\n  7 | a *nominally identical* GRPO config (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset, seed,\\n  8 | step budget) produced **84.4%** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] last-10 training reward on one backend and **5.0%** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] on\\n  9 | another — a ~17× gap [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] with **no visible hyperparameter difference**. The label was constant;\\n 10 | the stack was not. Each item below is on the list because it is a **documented lever that can\\n 11 | flip a head-to-head comparison**. If two papers both report these seven fields, a reader can\\n 12 | tell whether their comparison is confounded.\\n 13 | \\n 14 | Report all seven. They are a *minimum*, not a maximum.\\n 15 | \\n 16 | **Provenance / scope of the worked numbers in this checklist.** The concrete\\n 17 | numbers used as motivating examples below (61.6–89.6% prompt-token\\n 18 | loss magnitude; the 17× matched-config gap; the 82.0→83.3% held-out\\n 19 | control at p=0.26; the 95.0–98.1% / 95.0% Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n 20 | are inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this position\\n 21 | piece. **[TODO:trace to v1 audit citation]** A reader who wants to\\n 22 | verify the numbers should consult that paper; this checklist presents\\n 23 | them as illustrative of the *kind* of stack-driven flip, not as\\n 24 | re-derivable from a fresh run. Once the v1 audit paper is in\\n 25 | citation scope, the references go in the `\\\\bibliography{...}` and\\n 26 | the [TODO:trace] markers are replaced with real `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n 27 | \\n 28 | ---\\n 29 | \\n 30 | ## The 7 items\\n 31 | \\n 32 | ### 1. Loss form\\n 33 | - **Report:** PPO importance ratio used? (yes/no). Clipped? bounds (incl. asymmetric\\n 34 |   \\\"clip-higher\\\")? Token mask: completion-only or whole-sequence? Advantage normalization:\\n 35 |   per-group / per-batch / running estimate?\\n 36 | - **Why it can flip:** the token mask reassigns gradient. In one diagnostic [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation], **61.6–89.6%**\\n 37 |   of full-sequence loss magnitude came from *prompt* tokens, not completion tokens — a\\n 38 |   whole-sequence and a completion-only mask are different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO\\n 39 |   *is* a normalization change; GSPO *is* an IS-granularity change. If the baseline loss form\\n 40 |   is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n 41 | - **Good:** \\\"Token-masked completion-only; PPO ratio with symmetric clip ε=0.2; per-group\\n 42 |   advantage normalization (subtract group mean, divide by group std).\\\"\\n 43 | - **Bad:** \\\"We use the standard GRPO loss.\\\"\\n 44 | \\n 45 | ### 2. Reference policy + KL handling\\n 46 | - **Report:** frozen reference policy retained? (yes/no). KL term in the **loss** or folded\\n 47 |   into the **reward**? KL coefficient + schedule. Forward/reverse, per-token/per-sequence.\\n 48 | - **Why it can flip:** KL placement changes the objective and steady-state exploration. A\\n 49 |   runner with *no* frozen reference (one optimizer step per rollout) is not doing KL-regularized\\n 50 |   GRPO even at matched LR. Dual-anchor methods shift steady-state ZVF and must be recalibrated.\\n 51 | - **Good:** \\\"Frozen reference = SFT checkpoint; KL in the loss, β=0.04 constant; reverse KL,\\n 52 |   per-token.\\\"\\n 53 | - **Bad:** \\\"KL regularization as usual.\\\" / (silent on whether a reference exists)\\n 54 | \\n 55 | ### 3. Sampler / backend / precision\\n 56 | - **Report:** rollout engine (vLLM / SGLang / managed API / trainer `.generate`). Decoding\\n 57 |   params (temperature, top-p, max_tokens). Logit precision in sampler vs. trainer\\n 58 |   (bf16/fp16/fp32). Same tokenizer + chat template in sampler and trainer? (yes/no).\\n 59 | - **Why it can flip:** the sampler *defines* the rollout distribution the group-relative update\\n 60 |   consumes. A managed runner vs. an open vLLM path gave the **17×** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] matched-config gap. Sampler\\n 61 |   precision shifts the probability of mixed-reward groups, hence available gradient.\\n 62 | - **Good:** \\\"vLLM 0.x rollouts, bf16; trainer logits bf16; temp 0.8, top-p 1.0, max_tokens 512;\\n 63 |   identical tokenizer/chat template across both.\\\"\\n 64 | - **Bad:** \\\"Generated with default settings.\\\"\\n 65 | \\n 66 | ### 4. Per-step ZVF and GU trajectory\\n 67 | - **Report:** per-step **ZVF** (fraction of prompts whose G completions all get identical\\n 68 |   reward → zero gradient) and **GU = 1 − ZVF**, logged every step (release the trajectory).\\n 69 | - **Why it can flip:** ZVF/GU reveals whether the run had *any* usable learning signal. A\\n 70 |   method can \\\"win\\\" purely because its stack produced lower ZVF (more usable groups), not because\\n 71 |   its algorithm is better. Without the trajectory, a collapsed run and a saturated run look\\n 72 |   identical from final reward. (Mixed-group probability:\\n 73 |   `P(usable) ≈ (1/N) Σ_x [1 − (1−p_x)^G − p_x^G]`.) Cheap collapse triage: first-5-step rule\\n 74 |   ZVF ≥ 80% with reward ≤ 5%.\\n 75 | - **Good:** \\\"ZVF/GU logged per step (released CSV); mean ZVF@25 = 0.43; no run trips the\\n 76 |   collapse rule.\\\"\\n 77 | - **Bad:** Only final reward reported; no per-step signal telemetry.\\n 78 | - **Substitutes allowed:** under dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration ZVF\\n 79 |   degenerates → report per-step reward variance or gradient-norm variance instead. Item 4\\n 80 |   mandates *a usable-signal trajectory*, of which ZVF/GU is the default.\\n 81 | \\n 82 | ### 5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive)\\n 83 | - **Report:** group size G at every step; the rule that changes it if adaptive (e.g. AERO-style\\n 84 |   double/halve on rolling ZVF).\\n 85 | - **Why it can flip:** G sets the mixed-group probability and was one of the strongest single\\n 86 |   knobs measured (G = 2→4→8→16 moved last-10 reward across a wide band). Adaptive-G vs. fixed-G\\n 87 |   partly credits the variant for spending more compute on hard prompts — separate that from the\\n 88 |   algorithmic claim.\\n 89 | - **Good:** \\\"Fixed G=8 throughout.\\\" / \\\"Adaptive G∈{4,…,16}, baseline 8, double when rolling\\n 90 |   ZVF>0.8, halve when <0.3, window 10.\\\"\\n 91 | - **Bad:** \\\"Group size 8.\\\" (when it was actually adaptive)\\n 92 | \\n 93 | ### 6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment\\n 94 | - **Report:** a held-out slice **disjoint** from training prompts, scored by a harness, with N\\n 95 |   and a CI, reported **separately** from online training reward.\\n 96 | - **Why it can flip:** training reward is dynamics, not capability. A clean paired control\\n 97 |   improved only **82.0% → 83.3% (p=0.26)** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] despite near-saturated training reward; selecting\\n 98 |   checkpoints by training reward produced a spurious 87–95% \\\"capability\\\" band. Four 70B seeds\\n 99 |   ranged 95.0–98.1% on training last-10 [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] yet all landed on 95.0% held-out (sampling noise, not\\n100 |   generalization).\\n101 | - **Good:** \\\"Held-out = 500 GSM8K test problems, seed 0, disjoint from every training batch;\\n102 |   base 82.0% → post 83.3% [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation], Wilson 95% CI, paired per-prompt p=0.54 [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation].\\\"</update>\\n103 | - **Bad:** Reporting training-set reward as the capability number; \\\"accuracy 94%\\\" with no split\\n104 |   stated.\\n105 | \\n106 | ### 7. Decontamination probe results\\n107 | - **Report:** train/test contamination check (n-gram or embedding overlap between training\\n108 |   prompts and the held-out/benchmark slice) AND parser behavior on adversarial format-only\\n109 |   inputs.\\n110 | - **Why it can flip:** verifiable rewards still admit reward hacking — parser artifacts, format\\n111 |   shortcuts, length effects, train-prompt overfitting. Overlap → \\\"gain\\\" may be memorization;\\n112 |   parser rewards a format token → \\\"gain\\\" may be a shortcut.\\n113 | - **Good:** \\\"Max 8-gram overlap between train and held-out = 0.0%; parser rejects 100% of\\n114 |   format-only (no-answer) adversarial inputs.\\\"\\n115 | - **Bad:** No contamination check; parser behavior unstated.\\n116 | \\n117 | ---\\n118 | \\n119 | ## Fillable appendix template (drop into your paper)\\n120 | \\n121 | ```\\n122 | ================ MIN-REPORT-RL BLOCK ================\\n123 | Method label:            <e.g. GRPO / DAPO / GSPO / ...>\\n124 | Base checkpoint:         <model id + revision/hash>\\n125 | Tokenizer + chat tmpl:   <id; same in sampler and trainer? yes/no>\\n126 | \\n127 | [1] LOSS FORM\\n128 |     PPO ratio used:      <yes/no>\\n129 |     Clip:                <none / symmetric eps=__ / asymmetric lo=__ hi=__>\\n130 |     Token mask:          <completion-only / whole-sequence>\\n131 |     Advantage norm.:     <per-group / per-batch / running; formula>\\n132 | \\n133 | [2] REFERENCE + KL\\n134 |     Frozen reference:    <yes (=__ checkpoint) / no>\\n135 |     KL location:         <in loss / in reward / absent>\\n136 |     KL coeff + schedule: <beta=__, schedule=__>\\n137 |     KL estimator:        <forward/reverse, per-token/per-seq>\\n138 | \\n139 | [3] SAMPLER / BACKEND / PRECISION\\n140 |     Rollout engine:      <vLLM/SGLang/managed API/trainer.generate; version>\\n141 |     Decoding:            <temp=__, top_p=__, max_tokens=__>\\n142 |     Precision:           <sampler=__, trainer=__>\\n143 | \\n144 | [4] ZVF / GU TRAJECTORY\\n145 |     Per-step logged:     <yes/no; artifact link>\\n146 |     Mean ZVF@25:         <__>      Mean GU@25: <__>\\n147 |     Collapse rule trip:  <__ / S seeds>   (rule: ZVF>=80% & reward<=5% in first 5 steps)\\n148 |     (or substitute metric under dense/scaffolded rewards: __________)\\n149 | \\n150 | [5] GROUP-SIZE SCHEDULE\\n151 |     Type:                <fixed G=__ / adaptive (rule=__, bounds=__, window=__)>\\n152 | \\n153 | [6] HELD-OUT SPLIT\\n154 |     Slice:               <dataset/split, N=__, seed=__, disjoint from train? yes/no>\\n155 |     Base vs post:        <__% -> __%, CI=__, test=__ (p=__)>\\n156 |     Reported separately from training reward: <yes/no>\\n157 | \\n158 | [7] DECONTAMINATION\\n159 |     Train/test overlap:  <metric=__, value=__>\\n160 |     Parser adversarial:  <format-only reject rate=__%>\\n161 | ====================================================\\n162 | ```\\n163 | \\n164 | ---\\n165 | \\n166 | ## One-line self-test\\n167 | If a reader cannot reproduce your ranking of method A vs. method B **from the block above\\n168 | alone**, the block is incomplete — find the unreported lever and add it.\\n````\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## StackCheck-RL: an automated MIN-REPORT-RL compliance checker\\n\\nI would build **StackCheck-RL** as an evidence-first linter for GRPO-family papers and repositories. Its job is not to decide whether a method is good; it decides whether the paper or repo reports the full experimental treatment named by the MIN-REPORT-RL checklist. The attached draft frames the problem exactly this way: “GRPO” is not a complete treatment label, because loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend, group-size behavior, telemetry, held-out evaluation, and decontamination can each flip a comparison. fileciteturn0file0 The paper’s Section 3 defines the seven reportable fields, and Table 1 summarizes them as mandatory “flip levers,” not optional hyperparameters. fileciteturn1file3 fileciteturn1file6\\n\\n### What StackCheck-RL scans\\n\\n**Inputs**\\n\\n`stackcheck-rl paper.pdf repo/ --mode paper+repo`\\n\\nIt accepts a manuscript, LaTeX source, Markdown appendix, model card, README, experiment config, training scripts, logs, TensorBoard or W&B exports, reward parser tests, and evaluation artifacts.\\n\\n**Outputs**\\n\\nIt emits:\\n\\n1. A **headline compliance score** from 0–100.\\n2. A **seven-item scorecard** with evidence snippets and file paths.\\n3. A **strict reported score**: what the paper or README explicitly tells the reader.\\n4. A **reconstructed score**: what an auditor can infer from code/config/logs.\\n5. A **contradiction report**: places where paper and repo disagree.\\n6. A **machine-readable `min_report_rl.audit.json`**.\\n7. Optional CI outputs: Markdown badge, GitHub check, SARIF annotations.\\n\\nThe core design principle is: **implemented but not reported is not full compliance**. Code can rescue recoverability, but MIN-REPORT-RL is a reporting standard.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Scoring model\\n\\nEach checklist item receives **0–10 points**, for a raw total of **70**. The headline score is:\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{MIN-REPORT-RL Score} = \\\\mathrm{round}\\\\left(100 \\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\sum_{i=1}^7 s_i}{70}\\\\right)\\n\\\\]\\n\\nDefault bands:\\n\\n| Score | Label | Meaning |\\n|---:|---|---|\\n| 90–100 | **Compliant** | Complete, auditable, low ambiguity. |\\n| 75–89 | **Mostly compliant** | Minor gaps; a reader can usually reconstruct the treatment. |\\n| 55–74 | **Partial** | Several stack levers remain ambiguous. |\\n| 30–54 | **Weak** | Most claims are not reproducibility-auditable. |\\n| 0–29 | **Non-compliant** | “GRPO” label is doing most of the work. |\\n\\nHard flags override the label:\\n\\n| Flag | Effect |\\n|---|---|\\n| Paper/repo contradiction on a checklist field | Item capped at 4/10; overall marked **needs human audit**. |\\n| “Default settings,” “standard GRPO,” or “as usual” with no values | Relevant subfield capped at 1/10 or 2/10. |\\n| Closed backend hides loss/KL internals | Items 1–2 cannot receive full credit; result must be scoped as “platform implementation,” not generic GRPO, matching the paper’s own objection/response logic. fileciteturn1file9 |\\n| No held-out split distinct from reward environment | Item 6 capped at 2/10. |\\n| No usable-signal trajectory, ZVF/GU, reward variance, or gradient-variance substitute | Item 4 capped at 1/10. |\\n| Broken artifact links | Affected item capped at 6/10. |\\n| Only code inference, no paper/README/checklist report | Affected item capped at 7/10. |\\n\\nEvidence quality is reported separately:\\n\\n| Evidence grade | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| A | Explicitly reported and verified in repo/logs. |\\n| B | Explicitly reported, not machine-verified. |\\n| C | Recoverable from repo only. |\\n| D | Vague mention only. |\\n| F | Missing or contradicted. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Per-item rubric and heuristics\\n\\n## 1. Loss form — 10 points\\n\\nThe paper says authors should report whether a PPO-style importance ratio is used, whether clipping exists and with what bounds, whether the loss mask is completion-only or whole-sequence, and how advantages are normalized. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| PPO / importance ratio status: yes/no, formula, old-policy source | 2 |\\n| Clip rule: none, symmetric ε, asymmetric lower/upper, DAPO-style clip-higher | 2 |\\n| Token mask: completion-only, whole-sequence, prompt included/excluded | 2 |\\n| Advantage normalization: per-group, per-batch, running estimate, formula | 2 |\\n| Paper, config, and code agree | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “PPO ratio,” “importance ratio,” `πθ / πold`, “old logprobs”\\n- “clip ε=0.2,” “clip range,” “clip higher,” “asymmetric clip”\\n- “completion-only loss,” “response mask,” “prompt tokens masked”\\n- “per-group advantage normalization,” “subtract group mean,” “divide by group std”\\n- “length normalization,” “Dr.GRPO normalization”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- “standard GRPO loss”\\n- “we use GRPO as implemented in TRL”\\n- “loss follows prior work”\\n- “details are in code” with no field values\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nStackCheck-RL searches Python AST, configs, and trainer arguments for:\\n\\n```python\\nratio = torch.exp(new_logprobs - old_logprobs)\\ntorch.clamp(ratio, 1 - eps, 1 + eps)\\ncompletion_mask\\nresponse_mask\\nattention_mask[:, prompt_len:]\\nadvantages = (rewards - rewards.mean(dim=1)) / rewards.std(dim=1)\\nnum_generations\\nloss_type\\nclip_range\\nepsilon\\nepsilon_high\\n```\\n\\nIt also detects likely whole-sequence leakage by checking whether prompt-token positions have nonzero loss weight.\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- “Standard GRPO loss” alone: max 2/10.\\n- Loss recoverable only from code: max 7/10.\\n- Paper says completion-only but code applies loss to prompt tokens: cap 4/10 and raise contradiction.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Reference policy and KL handling — 10 points\\n\\nThe checklist requires the frozen reference status, KL location, coefficient/schedule, and estimator granularity. The draft emphasizes that KL in the loss, KL in the reward, and no frozen reference are different objectives. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Frozen reference retained? checkpoint/revision given? | 2 |\\n| KL location: loss, reward, absent, dual anchor | 2 |\\n| KL coefficient and schedule | 2 |\\n| KL estimator: forward/reverse, per-token/per-sequence | 2 |\\n| Paper, config, and code agree | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “frozen reference model”\\n- “reference = SFT checkpoint”\\n- “KL in reward,” “non-score reward,” “KL penalty in loss”\\n- `β=0.04`, `kl_coef`, “linear schedule,” “adaptive KL”\\n- “reverse KL,” “forward KL,” “per-token KL,” “sequence KL”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- “KL regularization as usual”\\n- “we follow PPO/GRPO defaults”\\n- no mention of reference model\\n- no β value\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nSearches for:\\n\\n```python\\nref_model\\nreference_model\\nkl_coef\\nkl_beta\\nkl_controller\\nnon_score_reward\\ncompute_kl\\nold_logprobs\\nref_logprobs\\nper_token_kl\\nsequence_kl\\n```\\n\\nIt checks whether `ref_model` is actually loaded, frozen, and used in the loss/reward path.\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- KL coefficient reported but no frozen-reference status: max 6/10.\\n- Closed backend with hidden KL/loss: max 5/10, or 6/10 if the paper explicitly scopes the result as a platform-specific implementation.\\n- Paper says “KL absent,” code computes KL penalty: cap 4/10.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Sampler, backend, and precision — 10 points\\n\\nThe paper treats rollout engine, decoding parameters, sampler precision, trainer precision, tokenizer, and chat template as mandatory because the sampler defines the rollout distribution consumed by group-relative updates. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Rollout engine and version: vLLM, SGLang, HF `generate`, managed API | 2 |\\n| Decoding parameters: temperature, top-p/top-k, max tokens, stop rules | 2 |\\n| Sampler precision and trainer precision | 2 |\\n| Tokenizer and chat template identity, revision, same/different across sampler/trainer | 2 |\\n| Backend/config evidence agrees with paper | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “vLLM 0.x,” “SGLang,” “HF generate,” “managed API”\\n- “temperature=0.8,” “top_p=1.0,” “max_tokens=512”\\n- `bf16`, `fp16`, `fp32`, “sampler logits”\\n- “same tokenizer and chat template in sampler and trainer”\\n- “tokenizer revision/hash”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- “generated with default settings”\\n- “we sample responses”\\n- “OpenAI-compatible endpoint” with no model/version/template details\\n- tokenizer omitted\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nSearches for imports and configs:\\n\\n```python\\nimport vllm\\nfrom vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\\nimport sglang\\nmodel.generate(...)\\nSamplingParams(temperature=..., top_p=..., max_tokens=...)\\ntorch_dtype=torch.bfloat16\\nbf16=True\\nfp16=True\\ntokenizer.apply_chat_template(...)\\nchat_template\\n```\\n\\nIt cross-checks tokenizer IDs and model revisions between rollout and trainer code.\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- Decoding defaults only: max 5/10.\\n- No tokenizer/chat-template evidence: max 6/10.\\n- Managed backend with unknown sampler internals: max 7/10 even if decoding parameters are reported.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Per-step ZVF and GU trajectory — 10 points\\n\\nThe paper defines ZVF as the fraction of prompts whose group completions all receive identical reward, and GU as `1 − ZVF`; it also allows substitutes such as per-step reward variance or gradient-norm variance when dense rewards make ZVF less meaningful. fileciteturn1file12 fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Metric defined: ZVF/GU or justified substitute | 2 |\\n| Per-step trajectory logged, not just final summary | 3 |\\n| Correct computation from per-prompt group rewards | 2 |\\n| Collapse/saturation summary, e.g. first-five-step rule | 1 |\\n| Artifact exists and matches paper | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “Zero-Variance Fraction”\\n- “ZVF”\\n- “Gradient Utilization”\\n- “GU = 1 − ZVF”\\n- “per-step reward variance”\\n- “gradient-norm variance”\\n- “mean ZVF@25”\\n- “collapse rule”\\n- “trajectory CSV”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- only final reward\\n- only loss curve\\n- only training accuracy\\n- “training was stable” with no usable-signal metric\\n\\n**Repo/log heuristics**\\n\\nSearches logs for columns:\\n\\n```text\\nstep,zvf,gu\\nzero_variance_fraction\\ngradient_utilization\\nreward_variance\\ngroup_reward_std\\nmean_zvf_at_25\\n```\\n\\nChecks computation shape:\\n\\n```python\\n# rewards shape: [num_prompts, group_size]\\nzvf = ((rewards.max(dim=1).values - rewards.min(dim=1).values) == 0).float().mean()\\ngu = 1 - zvf\\n```\\n\\nor equivalent.\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- Final ZVF only: max 4/10.\\n- No per-step usable-signal telemetry: max 1/10.\\n- Dense/process reward substitute can receive full credit only if the paper explains why ZVF degenerates and provides a per-step alternative.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Group-size schedule, fixed or adaptive — 10 points\\n\\nThe checklist requires `G` at every step and the rule for changing it if adaptive, because `G` directly changes the mixed-group probability and thus available gradient. fileciteturn1file10\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Fixed or adaptive schedule clearly stated | 3 |\\n| If adaptive: rule, thresholds, bounds, window | 2 |\\n| Per-step `G(t)` recoverable from logs/config | 2 |\\n| Compute implications reported for adaptive schedules | 1 |\\n| Paper, sampler config, and logs agree | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “fixed G=8 throughout”\\n- “num_generations=8”\\n- “adaptive G ∈ {4, 8, 16}”\\n- “double when rolling ZVF > 0.8”\\n- “halve when ZVF < 0.3”\\n- “window=10”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- “group size 8” but no statement about fixed/adaptive\\n- “dynamic sampling” with no schedule\\n- “we sample multiple completions” with no `G`\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nSearches for:\\n\\n```python\\nnum_generations\\ngroup_size\\nn_samples_per_prompt\\nrollout_n\\nsamples_per_prompt\\nadaptive_group_size\\nzvf_window\\nmax_group_size\\nmin_group_size\\n```\\n\\nIt also reads logs to confirm whether the group count changes over steps.\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- Initial `G` only, no fixed/adaptive statement: max 5/10.\\n- Adaptive code detected but paper says fixed: cap 4/10.\\n- Dynamic sampling with no rule: max 6/10.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment — 10 points\\n\\nThe paper requires a held-out slice disjoint from training prompts, scored by a harness, with sample size and confidence interval, reported separately from online training reward. fileciteturn1file10\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Held-out slice is disjoint from training prompts | 2 |\\n| Dataset/split, sample size, seed, and selection procedure | 2 |\\n| Evaluation harness/scoring described | 2 |\\n| CI/statistical test and seed aggregation | 2 |\\n| Reported separately from online training reward, with checkpoint-selection rule | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “held-out”\\n- “test split”\\n- “validation split”\\n- “disjoint from training prompts”\\n- `N=500`\\n- “Wilson CI,” “bootstrap CI,” “95% CI”\\n- “paired test,” “p-value”\\n- “lm-eval-harness,” “LightEval,” “EvalPlus”\\n- “checkpoint selected before held-out evaluation”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- “training reward”\\n- “online reward”\\n- “last-10 reward” used as capability\\n- “accuracy” with no split\\n- selecting best checkpoint by eval without declaring selection rule\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nSearches for dataset loading and split names:\\n\\n```python\\nload_dataset(\\\"gsm8k\\\", split=\\\"train\\\")\\nload_dataset(\\\"gsm8k\\\", split=\\\"test\\\")\\neval_dataset\\nheldout_dataset\\nvalidation_prompts\\nlm_eval\\nlighteval\\nbootstrap\\nwilson\\nconfidence_interval\\n```\\n\\nIt computes hashes of training prompts and eval prompts when files are present.\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- Only training reward reported: max 2/10.\\n- Held-out score reported but no N or CI: max 5/10.\\n- Held-out appears to overlap training prompts: max 4/10 and high-severity flag.\\n- No checkpoint-selection rule: max 8/10.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Decontamination probe results — 10 points\\n\\nThe paper requires both train/test contamination checking and parser behavior on adversarial format-only inputs, because reward hacking and train-prompt overlap can inflate apparent gains. fileciteturn1file10\\n\\n**Rubric**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Train/test overlap metric and threshold: n-gram, exact hash, embedding similarity | 3 |\\n| Reported overlap result and action taken on overlaps | 2 |\\n| Parser adversarial tests: format-only, no-answer, length/shortcut cases | 3 |\\n| Reproducible artifact or command | 1 |\\n| Parser source/tests agree with paper | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nPositive signals:\\n\\n- “decontamination”\\n- “8-gram overlap”\\n- “MinHash”\\n- “embedding overlap”\\n- “cosine similarity threshold”\\n- “train/test leakage”\\n- “format-only adversarial input”\\n- “parser rejects”\\n- “no-answer adversarial”\\n- “reward parser unit tests”\\n\\nNegative signals:\\n\\n- “dataset is standard, so no contamination”\\n- “we decontaminated” with no metric\\n- no parser tests\\n- no overlap threshold\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nSearches for:\\n\\n```python\\nngram_overlap\\ndeduplicate\\nminhash\\nembedding_similarity\\ncosine_similarity\\ncontamination\\ndecontam\\nparser_test\\nformat_only\\nno_answer\\nreject_rate\\nreward_parser\\npytest\\n```\\n\\nIt can optionally run parser tests against generated adversarial strings like:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"####\\\"\\n\\\"The answer is boxed.\\\"\\n\\\"<answer></answer>\\\"\\n\\\"Final answer: \\\"\\n\\\"Therefore, the answer is\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**Caps**\\n\\n- Generic “we decontaminated” with no metric/result: max 2/10.\\n- Overlap probe but no parser adversarial probe: max 5/10.\\n- Parser tests but no train/test overlap probe: max 5/10.\\n- Parser rewards format-only input: high-severity flag even if overlap probe passes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Checker architecture\\n\\n```text\\n                 ┌────────────────────┐\\n                 │ paper.pdf / .tex   │\\n                 │ README / model card│\\n                 └─────────┬──────────┘\\n                           │\\n                 ┌─────────▼──────────┐\\n                 │ Document extractor │\\n                 │ PDF/LaTeX/Markdown │\\n                 └─────────┬──────────┘\\n                           │\\nrepo/ ───────────┐         │\\nconfigs/logs ────┼─────────▼──────────┐\\nwandb export ────┘   Evidence indexer │\\n                     regex + AST +    │\\n                     semantic chunks  │\\n                     └──────┬─────────┘\\n                            │\\n                    ┌───────▼────────┐\\n                    │ Rule engine     │\\n                    │ 7 item scorers  │\\n                    │ caps + flags    │\\n                    └───────┬────────┘\\n                            │\\n                    ┌───────▼────────┐\\n                    │ Outputs         │\\n                    │ MD / JSON / CI  │\\n                    └────────────────┘\\n```\\n\\nThe checker has three internal passes:\\n\\n**Pass 1: GRPO-family detector**\\n\\nIt first decides whether the repo or paper is in scope. It looks for terms such as `GRPO`, `DAPO`, `GSPO`, `Dr.GRPO`, `MAD-GRPO`, `AERO`, `CPPO`, `NGRPO`, `Scaf-GRPO`, `group relative`, `num_generations`, `group_size`, `GRPOTrainer`, `verl`, `OpenRLHF`, and reward-group advantage normalization.\\n\\n**Pass 2: Evidence extraction**\\n\\nIt builds an evidence table:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"completion-only token mask\\\",\\n  \\\"source\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n  \\\"location\\\": \\\"appendix_a.tex:118\\\",\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Token mask: completion-only\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.96\\n}\\n```\\n\\nFor repos, it prefers exact config values over inferred defaults.\\n\\n**Pass 3: Consistency and scoring**\\n\\nIt scores each subfield, applies caps, and flags contradictions. For example:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"field\\\": \\\"loss_form.token_mask\\\",\\n  \\\"paper_value\\\": \\\"completion-only\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_value\\\": \\\"whole-sequence\\\",\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"contradiction\\\",\\n  \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n  \\\"cap_applied\\\": 4\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis is important because MIN-REPORT-RL is meant to make the stack auditable, not merely present somewhere in a codebase. The paper itself proposes a JSON-like block and argues that trainer defaults should emit the seven fields automatically. fileciteturn1file2\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Suggested manifest schema\\n\\nA repo can get near-automatic full credit by shipping `min_report_rl.json`:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"method_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"base_checkpoint\\\": {\\n    \\\"id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\\",\\n    \\\"revision\\\": \\\"abc123\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"tokenizer_chat_template\\\": {\\n    \\\"tokenizer_id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\\",\\n    \\\"tokenizer_revision\\\": \\\"abc123\\\",\\n    \\\"chat_template_hash\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n    \\\"same_sampler_and_trainer\\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\\"loss_form\\\": {\\n    \\\"ppo_ratio_used\\\": true,\\n    \\\"clip\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"symmetric\\\",\\n      \\\"epsilon\\\": 0.2\\n    },\\n    \\\"token_mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n    \\\"advantage_normalization\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": \\\"per_group\\\",\\n      \\\"formula\\\": \\\"(r - mean_group(r)) / (std_group(r) + 1e-8)\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"reference_kl\\\": {\\n    \\\"frozen_reference\\\": true,\\n    \\\"reference_checkpoint\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B-SFT@def456\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_location\\\": \\\"loss\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_coefficient\\\": 0.04,\\n    \\\"kl_schedule\\\": \\\"constant\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_estimator\\\": \\\"reverse_per_token\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n    \\\"rollout_engine\\\": \\\"vLLM\\\",\\n    \\\"engine_version\\\": \\\"0.8.5\\\",\\n    \\\"decoding\\\": {\\n      \\\"temperature\\\": 0.8,\\n      \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0,\\n      \\\"max_tokens\\\": 512\\n    },\\n    \\\"precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"sampler_logits\\\": \\\"bf16\\\",\\n      \\\"trainer_logits\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"usable_signal_trajectory\\\": {\\n    \\\"metric\\\": \\\"zvf_gu\\\",\\n    \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"artifacts/zvf_gu.csv\\\",\\n    \\\"mean_zvf_at_25\\\": 0.43,\\n    \\\"mean_gu_at_25\\\": 0.57,\\n    \\\"collapse_rule_trips\\\": \\\"0/5\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"fixed\\\",\\n    \\\"g\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"artifacts/group_size_by_step.csv\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"heldout_split\\\": {\\n    \\\"dataset\\\": \\\"GSM8K\\\",\\n    \\\"split\\\": \\\"test\\\",\\n    \\\"n\\\": 500,\\n    \\\"disjoint_from_training\\\": true,\\n    \\\"harness\\\": \\\"lm-eval-harness\\\",\\n    \\\"base_accuracy\\\": 0.82,\\n    \\\"post_accuracy\\\": 0.833,\\n    \\\"ci\\\": \\\"Wilson 95%\\\",\\n    \\\"reported_separately_from_training_reward\\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\\"decontamination\\\": {\\n    \\\"overlap_probe\\\": {\\n      \\\"metric\\\": \\\"8gram_overlap\\\",\\n      \\\"value\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"threshold\\\": 0.0\\n    },\\n    \\\"parser_adversarial\\\": {\\n      \\\"format_only_reject_rate\\\": 1.0,\\n      \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"tests/test_reward_parser_adversarial.py\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Example output A: highly compliant repo\\n\\nSynthetic example:\\n\\n```text\\n$ stackcheck-rl paper.pdf repo/ --mode paper+repo\\n\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Score: 94/100\\nRaw: 66/70\\nVerdict: COMPLIANT\\nEvidence confidence: 0.91\\nCritical flags: none\\n```\\n\\n| Item | Score | Evidence grade | Finding |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| 1. Loss form | 10/10 | A | PPO ratio present; symmetric clip ε=0.2; completion-only mask; per-group normalization; paper and `train_grpo.py` agree. |\\n| 2. Reference + KL | 10/10 | A | Frozen SFT reference; KL in loss; β=0.04 constant; reverse per-token KL. |\\n| 3. Sampler/backend/precision | 9/10 | A | vLLM rollouts; temp/top-p/max tokens reported; bf16 sampler/trainer; tokenizer/template shared. Missing exact vLLM commit, version present. |\\n| 4. ZVF/GU trajectory | 10/10 | A | Per-step CSV released; mean ZVF@25=0.43, GU@25=0.57; no collapse-rule trips. |\\n| 5. Group-size schedule | 9/10 | A | Fixed G=8 in paper/config/logs. Minor: no compute-normalization note. |\\n| 6. Held-out split | 9/10 | A | GSM8K held-out N=500, disjoint, harnessed, Wilson CI. Minor: seed list in artifact, not paper. |\\n| 7. Decontamination | 9/10 | A | 8-gram overlap 0.0%; parser rejects 100% format-only adversarial inputs. Minor: no embedding-overlap probe. |\\n\\nRecommended patch:\\n\\n```text\\nAdd vLLM commit hash and seed list to appendix.\\nOptional: add embedding-overlap probe to complement 8-gram overlap.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Example output B: typical under-specified GRPO paper\\n\\nSynthetic example:\\n\\n```text\\n$ stackcheck-rl paper.pdf --mode paper-only\\n\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Score: 21/100\\nRaw: 15/70\\nVerdict: NON-COMPLIANT\\nCritical flags:\\n  - No usable-signal trajectory.\\n  - Training reward is reported as capability.\\n  - No decontamination or parser adversarial probe.\\n```\\n\\n| Item | Score | Finding |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| 1. Loss form | 2/10 | Says “standard GRPO loss.” No ratio, clip, token mask, or advantage-normalization details. |\\n| 2. Reference + KL | 1/10 | Mentions KL regularization but no reference-policy status, β, location, or estimator. |\\n| 3. Sampler/backend/precision | 3/10 | Reports temperature and max tokens, but not backend, precision, tokenizer, or chat template. |\\n| 4. ZVF/GU trajectory | 0/10 | No ZVF, GU, reward variance, or gradient-variance trajectory. |\\n| 5. Group-size schedule | 4/10 | Reports G=8, but not whether fixed/adaptive or logged per step. |\\n| 6. Held-out split | 3/10 | Reports “accuracy” but does not state disjointness, N, CI, or whether separate from training reward. |\\n| 7. Decontamination | 2/10 | Says “we remove duplicates” but gives no overlap metric, threshold, or parser adversarial tests. |\\n\\nGenerated reviewer note:\\n\\n> The method comparison is not auditable under MIN-REPORT-RL. The paper reports the algorithm label and some visible hyperparameters, but omits the stack fields that the standard identifies as comparison-flipping levers. At minimum, add a MIN-REPORT-RL block with loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, per-step usable-signal telemetry, group-size schedule, disjoint held-out evaluation, and decontamination/parser probes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Example output C: closed managed-backend result\\n\\nSynthetic example:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"min_report_rl_score\\\": 57,\\n  \\\"raw_score\\\": \\\"40/70\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"PARTIAL_WITH_SCOPE_WARNING\\\",\\n  \\\"flags\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"item\\\": \\\"loss_form\\\",\\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"Closed backend does not expose loss implementation. Result should be scoped as ManagedRunner-GRPO, not generic GRPO.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n      \\\"item\\\": \\\"reference_kl\\\",\\n      \\\"message\\\": \\\"KL/reference handling hidden by platform.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"items\\\": {\\n    \\\"loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 4,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"D\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Paper says platform uses GRPO but cannot expose ratio/clip/mask/advantage normalization.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"reference_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 4,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"D\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"KL/reference status unavailable; paper honestly scopes the result as platform-specific.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 7,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"B\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Managed API named; decoding parameters reported; tokenizer/template and logit precision unavailable.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"B\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Per-step ZVF/GU logged from returned group rewards; computation script released.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"B\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Fixed G=8 reported and confirmed in request logs.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"heldout_split\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"A\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Disjoint held-out set, N, CI, and harness reported.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"decontamination\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 0,\\n      \\\"evidence_grade\\\": \\\"F\\\",\\n      \\\"summary\\\": \\\"No train/test overlap probe or parser adversarial probe.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nGenerated reviewer note:\\n\\n> This result is useful as a platform measurement but not as an algorithm-level GRPO claim. The paper is honest about hidden backend internals, which improves scoping, but MIN-REPORT-RL items 1 and 2 remain unauditable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Practical heuristics that make the checker robust\\n\\nStackCheck-RL should not merely keyword-match. It should use a layered evidence policy:\\n\\n**1. Prefer explicit values over method names.**  \\n“GRPO” gets no credit for loss details unless ratio, clipping, mask, and advantage normalization are actually stated.\\n\\n**2. Prefer reported evidence over inferred defaults.**  \\nIf TRL’s default config implies a value, but the paper never reports it, the item gets recoverability credit but not full reporting credit.\\n\\n**3. Treat “default settings” as an anti-pattern.**  \\nDefault settings are version-dependent and often invisible to readers.\\n\\n**4. Separate “absent” from “unknown.”**  \\n“KL absent, no frozen reference” is a reportable choice. Silence is not.\\n\\n**5. Cross-check paper, config, and logs.**  \\nA paper that says `G=8` but logs `G=4,8,16` under adaptive sampling should be flagged.\\n\\n**6. Require trajectories where the checklist says trajectories.**  \\nFor ZVF/GU and group size, a final scalar is not equivalent to per-step evidence.\\n\\n**7. Make closed-stack limitations visible, not fatal by default.**  \\nA managed backend can still score on sampler disclosure, held-out evaluation, and decontamination, but hidden loss/KL internals prevent full algorithm-level compliance.\\n\\n**8. Emit reviewer-ready language.**  \\nThe tool should produce copy-pasteable warnings: “Item 4 missing: no usable-signal trajectory,” not just “score low.”\\n\\n---\\n\\n## One-line badge format\\n\\nFor papers and repos, I would make the output badge intentionally blunt:\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL: 94/100 ✅ compliant\\nMIN-REPORT-RL: 63/100 ⚠ partial; missing ZVF/GU + decontamination\\nMIN-REPORT-RL: 21/100 ❌ non-compliant; stack under-specified\\nMIN-REPORT-RL: 57/100 ⚠ platform-scoped; closed loss/KL\\n```\\n\\nThat turns the paper’s proposed “cost is a JSON block” adoption path into a concrete automated gate: a repo either exposes the seven stack fields, or the checker tells reviewers exactly which unreported lever could be confounding the claimed GRPO gain.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379b16-0aec-83e8-8ce1-3bb69849d800\",\"title\":\"CLI Tool for GRPO Diff\",\"create\":1782029079.896159,\"update\":1782029485.273974,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a CLI tool that takes two GRPO run manifests and reports which stack levers differ and whether each difference is large enough to flip a comparison. Define the diff taxonomy and output format.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Tool: `grpo-stackdiff`\\n\\n`grpo-stackdiff` is a reviewer/auditor CLI for answering one question:\\n\\n> Are these two “GRPO” runs a clean method comparison, or are they two different stacks wearing the same label?\\n\\nIt is built around the paper’s premise that an algorithm label is not a complete treatment description: runs can differ in loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, usable-signal telemetry, group schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination/parser evidence, each of which the paper treats as a flip-capable lever. fileciteturn0file0 fileciteturn1file4 fileciteturn1file5\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. CLI shape\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpo-stackdiff diff RUN_A.yaml RUN_B.yaml \\\\\\n  --metric results.heldout.exact_match \\\\\\n  --claim \\\"A > B\\\" \\\\\\n  --allow-delta loss.clip.upper \\\\\\n  --allow-delta sampling.dynamic_rule \\\\\\n  --calibration zvf-stack-effects.yaml \\\\\\n  --format text,json \\\\\\n  --out report.json\\n```\\n\\nSubcommands:\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpo-stackdiff validate RUN.yaml\\ngrpo-stackdiff diff RUN_A.yaml RUN_B.yaml\\ngrpo-stackdiff explain MRR-3.rollout.backend\\ngrpo-stackdiff schema --format jsonschema\\ngrpo-stackdiff calibrate matched_ablation_runs/*.yaml --out effects.yaml\\n```\\n\\nThe key design choice: the tool distinguishes **intended method deltas** from **uncontrolled stack deltas**. For example, comparing DAPO to GRPO may intentionally allow `loss.clip.upper` or `sampling.dynamic_rule`, but a hidden change in tokenizer, reward parser, held-out split, KL placement, or sampler precision is reported as a stack confound. That mirrors the paper’s controlled-audit rule: hold everything identical except the variant’s defining hook. fileciteturn1file7\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. Required run manifest shape\\n\\nThe CLI consumes JSON or YAML. The manifest is essentially the paper’s “cost is a JSON block” made concrete.\\n\\n```yaml\\nschema_version: min-report-rl.manifest/v0.1\\n\\nrun:\\n  id: qwen3_grpo_trl_seed42\\n  algorithm_label: GRPO\\n  framework: trl\\n  framework_version: 0.XX\\n  trainer_commit: 7d9c...\\n  seed_set: [42, 43, 44]\\n\\nmodel:\\n  base_checkpoint: Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\n  base_checkpoint_sha: sha256:...\\n  tokenizer_id: Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\n  tokenizer_sha: sha256:...\\n  chat_template_sha: sha256:...\\n\\nloss:\\n  importance_ratio: ppo_token_ratio        # none | ppo_token_ratio | sequence_ratio\\n  clipping:\\n    enabled: true\\n    lower: 0.8\\n    upper: 1.2\\n    asymmetric_clip_higher: null\\n  token_mask: completion_only              # completion_only | whole_sequence\\n  advantage_norm: per_group                # per_group | per_batch | running\\n  length_normalization: token_mean\\n  variant_hooks: []\\n\\nreference_kl:\\n  frozen_reference: true\\n  reference_checkpoint_sha: sha256:...\\n  placement: loss                          # loss | reward | absent\\n  coefficient: 0.02\\n  schedule: constant\\n  estimator: forward_token                 # forward_token | reverse_token | seq_kl\\n\\nrollout:\\n  backend: vllm\\n  backend_version: 0.XX\\n  decoding:\\n    temperature: 0.7\\n    top_p: 0.95\\n    max_tokens: 512\\n  sampler_logit_precision: bf16\\n  trainer_logit_precision: bf16\\n  tokenizer_shared_with_trainer: true\\n  chat_template_shared_with_trainer: true\\n\\ngroup:\\n  schedule:\\n    type: fixed\\n    values: [{start_step: 0, G: 8}]\\n  adaptive_rule: null\\n\\nreward:\\n  environment_id: gsm8k_train_500\\n  parser_name: gsm8k_boxed_answer\\n  parser_sha: sha256:...\\n  parser_format_only_probe:\\n    adversarial_pass_rate: 0.00\\n    n: 200\\n\\nsignal_telemetry:\\n  zvf:\\n    path: traces/zvf.jsonl\\n    first_5_mean: 0.21\\n    step_25_mean: 0.34\\n  gu:\\n    path: traces/gu.jsonl\\n    first_5_mean: 0.79\\n    step_25_mean: 0.66\\n  collapse_rule:\\n    zvf_ge_80_reward_le_5_first_5: false\\n\\ntraining:\\n  optimizer: adamw\\n  optimizer_config_sha: sha256:...\\n  lora:\\n    enabled: true\\n    rank: 16\\n    alpha: 32\\n    target_modules: [q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj]\\n  precision: bf16\\n  steps: 30\\n  learning_rate: 1.0e-5\\n\\neval:\\n  heldout:\\n    dataset_id: gsm8k_test_500\\n    disjoint_from_reward_env: true\\n    overlap_probe_sha: sha256:...\\n    n: 500\\n    metric: exact_match\\n    value: 0.833\\n    ci95: [0.801, 0.862]\\n  harness_name: lm-eval-custom\\n  harness_sha: sha256:...\\n  checkpoint_selection: final_step          # final_step | best_train_reward | best_heldout\\n\\nresults:\\n  last10_train_reward: 0.844\\n  primary_metric_path: eval.heldout.value\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Diff taxonomy\\n\\nThe tool uses a three-layer taxonomy: **lever**, **diff kind**, and **comparison role**.\\n\\n## 3.1 Lever taxonomy\\n\\n| Code | Lever | Examples | Default risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `MRR-1.loss` | Loss form | ratio on/off, token vs sequence ratio, clip bounds, token mask, advantage normalization, length normalization | Critical |\\n| `MRR-2.ref_kl` | Reference policy and KL | frozen ref present/absent, KL in loss vs reward, coefficient, schedule, estimator | Critical |\\n| `MRR-3.rollout` | Sampler/backend/precision | vLLM vs managed API, decoding params, bf16/fp32 logits, tokenizer/template sharing | Critical |\\n| `MRR-4.signal` | Usable-signal telemetry | ZVF/Gu traces, collapse status, early mixed-group availability | Critical |\\n| `MRR-5.group` | Group-size schedule | fixed `G`, adaptive rule, per-step schedule, compute spent per prompt | High |\\n| `MRR-6.eval` | Held-out evaluation | disjointness, sample size, CI, harness, checkpoint selection | Critical |\\n| `MRR-7.decontam` | Decontamination and parser probes | train/test overlap, parser adversarial behavior, format-only shortcuts | High/Critical |\\n| `EXT-1.identity` | Model/tokenizer/template identity | checkpoint hash, tokenizer hash, chat template hash | Critical |\\n| `EXT-2.adapter_optimizer` | LoRA, optimizer, training precision | target modules, rank, optimizer defaults, bf16/fp16/fp32 trainer precision | High |\\n| `EXT-3.reward_env` | Reward environment and parser identity | reward dataset, parser code hash, reward service version | Critical |\\n| `EXT-4.selection` | Run/checkpoint selection | best training reward vs final step vs pre-registered checkpoint | Critical |\\n\\nThe first seven are the Min-Report-RL minimum stack. The `EXT-*` categories are included because the paper repeatedly names them as stack components that must be held fixed in a controlled audit: checkpoint/tokenizer, LoRA targets/rank, optimizer, reward parser, evaluator, and checkpoint selection. fileciteturn1file14 fileciteturn1file7\\n\\n## 3.2 Diff-kind taxonomy\\n\\n| Kind | Meaning | Example |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `VALUE_DIFF` | Categorical value differs | `token_mask: whole_sequence → completion_only` |\\n| `NUMERIC_SHIFT` | Numeric field differs | `temperature: 0.7 → 1.0` |\\n| `SCHEDULE_SHIFT` | Per-step schedule differs | `G=8 fixed → adaptive 4/8/16` |\\n| `ARTIFACT_HASH_DIFF` | Same human name, different artifact hash | same checkpoint label, different SHA |\\n| `CODE_PATH_DIFF` | Different implementation code | different reward parser commit |\\n| `TELEMETRY_DIST_DIFF` | Trace/distribution differs | ZVF first-5 mean `0.82 → 0.19` |\\n| `EVAL_INCOMPARABLE` | Metrics are not the same estimand | training reward vs held-out exact match |\\n| `EVIDENCE_MISSING` | Required field absent in one or both manifests | no KL placement reported |\\n| `SEMANTIC_EQUIV` | Surface diff canonicalizes to same meaning | `fp16` alias resolved to `float16` |\\n\\n## 3.3 Comparison-role taxonomy\\n\\n| Role | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| `INTENDED_METHOD_DELTA` | The difference is the claimed algorithmic hook, supplied by `--allow-delta` or the manifest. |\\n| `STACK_CONFOUND` | Difference is outside the intended method delta and can contaminate the comparison. |\\n| `MEASUREMENT_CONFOUND` | Difference changes how performance is measured, selected, or validated. |\\n| `REPORTING_GAP` | Field is missing, so the comparison cannot be audited. |\\n| `BENIGN_EQUIVALENT` | Difference is normalized away or demonstrably immaterial. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. “Large enough to flip” rule\\n\\n`grpo-stackdiff` does not pretend that two manifests alone identify causal effects. It reports flip materiality using explicit evidence.\\n\\nFor a comparison metric:\\n\\n```text\\ndelta_pp = 100 * (metric_A - metric_B)\\npoint_flip_threshold_pp = abs(delta_pp)\\n```\\n\\nA stack difference is marked **large enough to flip the point comparison** when:\\n\\n```text\\neffect_upper_bound_pp >= point_flip_threshold_pp\\n```\\n\\nThe effect bound can come from four sources, in order of preference:\\n\\n1. **Matched ablation calibration**: user-provided or corpus-derived effect sizes for a lever in a comparable model/task/backend setting.\\n2. **Analytic signal estimate**: used for ZVF/Gu and group-size changes, using the mixed-group probability logic from the paper.\\n3. **Paper-prior heuristic**: optional mode, disabled by default, that treats the paper’s documented flip mechanisms as broad priors.\\n4. **Unknown**: no numeric bound; the tool reports `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING` rather than inventing certainty.\\n\\nThe paper’s ZVF/Gu discussion is important here: usable group-relative signal depends on mixed-reward groups, and a run can appear to win simply because its stack produced more usable groups rather than because its algorithmic change is better. fileciteturn1file13\\n\\nVerdicts:\\n\\n| Verdict | `large_enough_to_flip` | Meaning |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| `FLIPS_POINT` | `true` | Calibrated or structural effect is at least as large as the observed point margin. |\\n| `ERASES_CLAIM` | `partial` | May not reverse the point estimate, but is large enough to erase a CI-based claim. |\\n| `NEAR_MARGIN` | `partial` | Effect upper bound is at least half the margin; needs controlled rerun. |\\n| `NO_EVIDENCE_OF_FLIP` | `false` | Calibrated upper bound is below the flip threshold. |\\n| `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING` | `null` | Required field missing or no calibrated bound for a mandatory flip lever. |\\n| `INCOMPARABLE` | `null` | Runs use different evaluation/reward environments or non-equivalent metrics. |\\n\\nFor structural changes such as different held-out splits, different reward parsers, absent KL disclosure, or training reward compared to held-out reward, the tool does not reduce the issue to a fake numeric estimate. It reports `INCOMPARABLE` or `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Text output format\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```text\\ngrpo-stackdiff 0.1.0\\ncomparison: dapo_run.yaml (A) vs grpo_baseline.yaml (B)\\nprimary metric: eval.heldout.exact_match\\nA: 84.6%   B: 82.1%   observed margin: +2.5 pp\\nclaim: A > B\\nallowed method deltas:\\n  - loss.clipping.upper\\n  - group.adaptive_rule\\n\\nOVERALL VERDICT: STACK-CONFOUNDED\\nReason: 3 uncontrolled stack differences are individually flip-capable or blocking.\\n\\n┌──────┬──────────────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────────┬─────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────┐\\n│ ID   │ Lever                    │ Role         │ Difference                 │ Effect bound│ Flip verdict │ Action             │\\n├──────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────┼─────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────┤\\n│ D001 │ MRR-1.loss.clip          │ INTENDED     │ upper: 1.20 → 1.28         │ unscored    │ method delta │ keep as claimed    │\\n│ D002 │ MRR-2.ref_kl.placement   │ CONFOUND     │ loss → reward              │ unknown     │ UNKNOWN_BLOCKING │ rerun same KL  │\\n│ D003 │ MRR-3.rollout.backend    │ CONFOUND     │ vllm → managed_api         │ 5–80 pp     │ FLIPS_POINT  │ same sampler       │\\n│ D004 │ MRR-4.signal.zvf_first5  │ CONFOUND     │ 0.82 → 0.21; collapse flip │ ≥10 pp      │ FLIPS_POINT  │ inspect signal     │\\n│ D005 │ MRR-6.eval.checkpoint    │ MEASUREMENT  │ final_step → best_train    │ structural  │ INCOMPARABLE │ pre-register ckpt  │\\n│ D006 │ MRR-7.decontam.probe     │ REPORT_GAP   │ missing in A               │ unknown     │ UNKNOWN_BLOCKING │ run probe      │\\n└──────┴──────────────────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────────────┴─────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────┘\\n\\nClean-method-comparison status:\\n  FAIL: uncontrolled differences remain outside the allowed method delta.\\n\\nMinimal rerun recipe:\\n  1. Use the same rollout backend, tokenizer, chat template, and logit precision.\\n  2. Put KL in the same location with the same reference checkpoint and beta schedule.\\n  3. Select checkpoints by the same pre-registered rule.\\n  4. Report ZVF/Gu traces for both arms.\\n  5. Run the same decontamination and parser probes.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. JSON output format\\n\\nMachine-readable output is the canonical format. The text report is a rendering of this.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"grpo-stackdiff.report/v0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"tool\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"grpo-stackdiff\\\",\\n    \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"inputs\\\": {\\n    \\\"left\\\": \\\"dapo_run.yaml\\\",\\n    \\\"right\\\": \\\"grpo_baseline.yaml\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"comparison\\\": {\\n    \\\"left_label\\\": \\\"A\\\",\\n    \\\"right_label\\\": \\\"B\\\",\\n    \\\"claim\\\": \\\"A > B\\\",\\n    \\\"primary_metric_path\\\": \\\"eval.heldout.exact_match\\\",\\n    \\\"left_value\\\": 0.846,\\n    \\\"right_value\\\": 0.821,\\n    \\\"delta_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n    \\\"point_flip_threshold_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n    \\\"metric_quality\\\": \\\"heldout_disjoint_with_ci\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"allowed_method_deltas\\\": [\\n    \\\"loss.clipping.upper\\\",\\n    \\\"group.adaptive_rule\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"STACK_CONFOUNDED\\\",\\n    \\\"clean_method_comparison\\\": false,\\n    \\\"diff_count\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"uncontrolled_diff_count\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"single_lever_flip_count\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"blocking_unknown_count\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"incomparable_count\\\": 1\\n  },\\n  \\\"diffs\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"D001\\\",\\n      \\\"lever\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"MRR-1.loss\\\",\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"Loss form\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"loss.clipping.upper\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"NUMERIC_SHIFT\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"INTENDED_METHOD_DELTA\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": 1.28,\\n      \\\"right\\\": 1.2,\\n      \\\"normalized_delta\\\": {\\n        \\\"unit\\\": \\\"ratio\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": 0.08\\n      },\\n      \\\"materiality\\\": {\\n        \\\"risk\\\": \\\"unscored_intended_delta\\\",\\n        \\\"effect_interval_pp\\\": null,\\n        \\\"effect_source\\\": \\\"not_applicable\\\",\\n        \\\"large_enough_to_flip\\\": null,\\n        \\\"flip_verdict\\\": \\\"METHOD_DELTA\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Allowed by comparison intent; this is part of the claimed DAPO-style hook.\\\",\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Keep, but label it as the intended algorithmic delta.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"D002\\\",\\n      \\\"lever\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"MRR-2.ref_kl\\\",\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"Reference policy and KL handling\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"reference_kl.placement\\\",\\n        \\\"reference_kl.coefficient\\\",\\n        \\\"reference_kl.estimator\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"VALUE_DIFF\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"STACK_CONFOUND\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": {\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"reward\\\",\\n        \\\"coefficient\\\": 0.02,\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_token\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"right\\\": {\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"loss\\\",\\n        \\\"coefficient\\\": 0.02,\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_token\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"materiality\\\": {\\n        \\\"risk\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n        \\\"effect_interval_pp\\\": null,\\n        \\\"effect_source\\\": \\\"unknown_structural\\\",\\n        \\\"point_flip_threshold_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n        \\\"large_enough_to_flip\\\": null,\\n        \\\"flip_verdict\\\": \\\"UNKNOWN_BLOCKING\\\",\\n        \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high_that_it_is_a_confound_low_that_magnitude_is_known\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"KL placement changes the effective objective; no calibrated bound was supplied.\\\",\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Rerun with identical KL placement, reference checkpoint, beta schedule, and estimator.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"D003\\\",\\n      \\\"lever\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"MRR-3.rollout\\\",\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"Sampler, backend, and precision\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"rollout.backend\\\",\\n        \\\"rollout.sampler_logit_precision\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"VALUE_DIFF\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"STACK_CONFOUND\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": {\\n        \\\"backend\\\": \\\"managed_api\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\": \\\"unknown\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"right\\\": {\\n        \\\"backend\\\": \\\"vllm\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"materiality\\\": {\\n        \\\"risk\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n        \\\"effect_interval_pp\\\": {\\n          \\\"lower\\\": 5.0,\\n          \\\"upper\\\": 80.0\\n        },\\n        \\\"effect_source\\\": \\\"calibration:backend_ablation_qwen3_gsm8k\\\",\\n        \\\"point_flip_threshold_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n        \\\"large_enough_to_flip\\\": true,\\n        \\\"flip_verdict\\\": \\\"FLIPS_POINT\\\",\\n        \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Rollout backend defines the distribution consumed by the group-relative update.\\\",\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Use the same rollout engine, decoding parameters, tokenizer, chat template, and sampler precision.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"rerun_recipe\\\": [\\n    \\\"Hold all non-allowed Min-Report-RL fields identical.\\\",\\n    \\\"Use one held-out split and one evaluation harness.\\\",\\n    \\\"Report per-step ZVF/Gu for both runs.\\\",\\n    \\\"Run parser and train/test contamination probes.\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Exit codes for CI and paper review\\n\\n```text\\n0  No material uncontrolled differences.\\n1  Differences exist, but calibrated bounds are below flip threshold.\\n2  At least one uncontrolled difference is large enough to flip the point comparison.\\n3  At least one mandatory field is missing or closed/unknown, blocking audit.\\n4  Evaluation or reward metric is incomparable.\\n5  Invalid manifest.\\n```\\n\\nA paper-review workflow could therefore run:\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpo-stackdiff diff claimed_variant.yaml baseline.yaml --strict\\n```\\n\\nand fail the check unless the only material differences are listed as intended method deltas.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Core philosophy\\n\\n`grpo-stackdiff` should be conservative in one specific way: it must never convert missing stack telemetry into “safe.” If a mandatory flip lever is absent, the output is not “no diff”; it is `REPORTING_GAP` or `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING`.\\n\\nThat matches the paper’s central claim: the field cannot audit what it does not record, and clean GRPO-family comparisons require the stack to be reported and held fixed except for the named variant delta. fileciteturn1file10\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379a9c-0c40-83ee-b032-422b1df0c4fe\",\"title\":\"TRL plugin design\",\"create\":1782028959.305392,\"update\":1782029386.572807,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached MIN-REPORT-RL position paper. Invent a TRL plugin that automatically emits the 7-item minimum-reportable-stack block as JSON at the start of every GRPO run and logs per-step ZVF/GU. Specify the hooks, config flags, and output schema.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Plugin: `trl-min-report-rl`\\n\\nI’d implement this as a small TRL extension named **`MinReportRLPlugin`**: a `TrainerCallback` for start/end/logging events plus one GRPO-specific reward hook that sees the per-prompt reward matrix before advantages are normalized. That split matters because standard trainer callbacks can emit a manifest, but they do not reliably receive the group reward tensor needed to compute ZVF/GU.\\n\\nThe attached paper’s natural contract is: **write the seven-item minimum-reportable-stack block once at run start, then write item-4 telemetry every step**. The page-6 checklist defines the seven mandatory stack fields, and the page-7 telemetry table makes ZVF/GU the per-arm/per-step signal to log. fileciteturn0file0 Current TRL is already close to this: `GRPOTrainer` supports callbacks and custom rollout plumbing, and `GRPOConfig` exposes the relevant knobs such as `num_generations`, vLLM settings, loss type, reward scaling, KL coefficient, and generation parameters. citeturn295757view1turn965512view1turn965512view0\\n\\n---\\n\\n## User-facing API\\n\\n```python\\nfrom trl import GRPOConfig\\nfrom trl_min_report_rl import MinReportGRPOTrainer, MinReportRLConfig\\n\\ntraining_args = GRPOConfig(\\n    output_dir=\\\"runs/qwen3_gsm8k_grpo\\\",\\n    num_generations=8,\\n    loss_type=\\\"dapo\\\",\\n    beta=0.0,\\n    use_vllm=True,\\n    vllm_mode=\\\"colocate\\\",\\n    temperature=1.0,\\n    top_p=1.0,\\n    report_to=[\\\"wandb\\\", \\\"tensorboard\\\"],\\n)\\n\\nmin_report = MinReportRLConfig(\\n    enabled=True,\\n    emit_manifest_on_train_begin=True,\\n    log_zvf_gu_every_step=True,\\n    reward_equal_atol=0.0,\\n    reward_equal_rtol=0.0,\\n    fail_on_missing_required=False,\\n    heldout=MinReportRLConfig.Heldout(\\n        name=\\\"gsm8k_test_500\\\",\\n        split=\\\"test\\\",\\n        reward_environment_disjoint=True,\\n        evaluator=\\\"lm-eval-harness:gsm8k_exact_match\\\",\\n        confidence_interval=\\\"bootstrap_95\\\",\\n    ),\\n    decontamination=MinReportRLConfig.Decontamination(\\n        ngram_overlap_path=\\\"artifacts/decontam/ngram_overlap.json\\\",\\n        parser_probe_path=\\\"artifacts/decontam/parser_adversarial.json\\\",\\n    ),\\n)\\n\\ntrainer = MinReportGRPOTrainer(\\n    model=\\\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\\",\\n    args=training_args,\\n    train_dataset=train_ds,\\n    eval_dataset=heldout_ds,\\n    reward_funcs=[gsm8k_reward],\\n    min_report_rl=min_report,\\n)\\n\\ntrainer.train()\\n```\\n\\nIt writes:\\n\\n```text\\noutput_dir/\\n  min_report_rl/\\n    min_report_rl.json\\n    min_report_rl.sha256\\n    zvf_gu.jsonl\\n    final_summary.json\\n```\\n\\nIt also logs scalar metrics through TRL’s existing logging path. TRL already supports experiment trackers through `report_to`, including Trackio, W&B, and TensorBoard, so the plugin should reuse that instead of inventing a separate tracker interface. citeturn295757view2\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Hooks\\n\\n| Hook | Location | Purpose |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `GRPOTrainer.__init__` wrapper | Plugin subclass | Capture `GRPOConfig`, model/tokenizer identity, `peft_config`, reward funcs, rollout backend, dataset fingerprints, and attach the emitter. |\\n| `TrainerCallback.on_train_begin` | Standard HF callback | Emit `min_report_rl.json` **before the first rollout**; log `min_report_rl/manifest_sha256`. Transformers callbacks expose `on_train_begin`, `on_step_end`, `on_log`, `on_save`, and `on_train_end` lifecycle hooks. citeturn447619search1 |\\n| `on_grpo_rewards_ready` | New GRPO-specific hook | Called immediately after reward aggregation and before advantage normalization/loss. Receives `prompt_ids`, `completion_ids`, `reward_matrix`, `reward_components`, `group_size`, `split`, and `global_step`. |\\n| `TrainerCallback.on_log` | Standard callback | Adds latest ZVF/GU scalars to normal TRL logs when tracker logging fires. |\\n| `TrainerCallback.on_save` | Standard callback | Copies the manifest into each checkpoint folder and snapshots telemetry summary. |\\n| `TrainerCallback.on_train_end` | Standard callback | Writes `final_summary.json`, including mean ZVF/GU, first-five collapse triage, and missing-field warnings. |\\n\\nThe one new hook should be upstreamed into `GRPOTrainer` as:\\n\\n```python\\ndef on_grpo_rewards_ready(\\n    self,\\n    *,\\n    split: str,\\n    prompt_ids: list[str],\\n    completion_ids: list[str],\\n    rewards: \\\"torch.Tensor\\\",        # shape [num_prompts, G]\\n    reward_components: dict | None, # optional raw per reward function\\n    group_size: int,\\n) -> None:\\n    if self.min_report_rl_emitter is not None:\\n        self.min_report_rl_emitter.log_step(\\n            trainer=self,\\n            split=split,\\n            prompt_ids=prompt_ids,\\n            completion_ids=completion_ids,\\n            rewards=rewards,\\n            reward_components=reward_components,\\n            group_size=group_size,\\n        )\\n```\\n\\nFor a no-fork package, `MinReportGRPOTrainer` subclasses `GRPOTrainer` and inserts that call at the reward-scoring site. For an upstream TRL PR, this becomes a native `GRPOConfig` feature flag.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Config flags\\n\\n```python\\n@dataclass\\nclass MinReportRLConfig:\\n    enabled: bool = True\\n    emit_manifest_on_train_begin: bool = True\\n    manifest_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.json\\\"\\n    telemetry_filename: str = \\\"zvf_gu.jsonl\\\"\\n    final_summary_filename: str = \\\"final_summary.json\\\"\\n\\n    emit_to_file: bool = True\\n    emit_to_trainer_log: bool = True\\n    emit_to_trackers: bool = True\\n    log_zvf_gu_every_step: bool = True\\n    tracker_metric_prefix: str = \\\"min_report_rl/\\\"\\n\\n    reward_source: Literal[\\\"post_aggregation\\\", \\\"per_reward_func\\\", \\\"both\\\"] = \\\"post_aggregation\\\"\\n    reward_equal_atol: float = 0.0\\n    reward_equal_rtol: float = 0.0\\n    nan_policy: Literal[\\\"exclude_group\\\", \\\"zero_variance\\\", \\\"error\\\"] = \\\"exclude_group\\\"\\n    group_key: Literal[\\\"prompt_hash\\\", \\\"dataset_index\\\", \\\"batch_index\\\"] = \\\"prompt_hash\\\"\\n\\n    store_prompt_text: bool = False\\n    hash_prompt_text: bool = True\\n    hash_completion_text: bool = False\\n\\n    collapse_zvf_threshold: float = 0.80\\n    collapse_reward_threshold: float = 0.05\\n    collapse_window_steps: int = 5\\n\\n    fail_on_missing_required: bool = False\\n    missing_policy: Literal[\\\"emit_unknown\\\", \\\"warn\\\", \\\"error\\\"] = \\\"warn\\\"\\n\\n    heldout: Heldout | None = None\\n    decontamination: Decontamination | None = None\\n```\\n\\nSuggested `GRPOConfig` flags after upstreaming:\\n\\n```python\\nreport_min_report_rl: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_log_zvf_gu_every_step: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_fail_on_missing_required: bool = False\\nmin_report_rl_reward_equal_atol: float = 0.0\\nmin_report_rl_reward_equal_rtol: float = 0.0\\nmin_report_rl_store_prompt_text: bool = False\\nmin_report_rl_decontamination_config: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_heldout_config: str | None = None\\n```\\n\\nCLI equivalents:\\n\\n```bash\\n--report_min_report_rl true\\n--min_report_rl_log_zvf_gu_every_step true\\n--min_report_rl_fail_on_missing_required false\\n--min_report_rl_reward_equal_atol 0.0\\n--min_report_rl_store_prompt_text false\\n--min_report_rl_heldout_config configs/heldout.json\\n--min_report_rl_decontamination_config artifacts/decontam/probes.json\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ZVF/GU computation\\n\\nFor each prompt \\\\(x\\\\), TRL samples \\\\(G\\\\) completions and scores them with reward functions before forming the group-relative advantage; that is exactly the matrix the plugin needs. citeturn295757view0\\n\\n```python\\ndef compute_zvf_gu(reward_matrix, *, atol=0.0, rtol=0.0, nan_policy=\\\"exclude_group\\\"):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    reward_matrix: shape [num_prompts, G]\\n    ZVF = fraction of prompt groups whose G rewards are all identical.\\n    GU  = 1 - ZVF.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    valid_groups = []\\n    zero_variance = []\\n\\n    for rewards in reward_matrix:\\n        finite = rewards[torch.isfinite(rewards)]\\n\\n        if len(finite) != len(rewards):\\n            if nan_policy == \\\"error\\\":\\n                raise ValueError(\\\"NaN/Inf reward in GRPO group\\\")\\n            if nan_policy == \\\"exclude_group\\\":\\n                continue\\n\\n        if len(finite) < 2:\\n            continue\\n\\n        same = torch.isclose(finite, finite[0], atol=atol, rtol=rtol).all()\\n        valid_groups.append(True)\\n        zero_variance.append(bool(same))\\n\\n    n = len(valid_groups)\\n    z = sum(zero_variance)\\n    zvf = z / n if n else None\\n    gu = 1.0 - zvf if zvf is not None else None\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"zvf\\\": zvf,\\n        \\\"gu\\\": gu,\\n        \\\"zero_variance_groups\\\": z,\\n        \\\"usable_groups\\\": None if n == 0 else n - z,\\n        \\\"valid_groups\\\": n,\\n    }\\n```\\n\\nFor binary verifiable rewards, `atol=0` is correct. For dense process rewards, set a tolerance or use `reward_std`/gradient-variance fallback; the manifest records that choice rather than pretending ZVF means the same thing in every reward regime.\\n\\nIn distributed training, the hook must compute ZVF/GU after gathering the full reward groups across processes, not on local shards, because a group can be split across devices. TRL’s GRPO source already has custom batching/sampling logic for generation batches, so the plugin should attach at the post-gather reward point, not inside the reward function itself. citeturn470275view2\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Run-start manifest schema: `min_report_rl.json`\\n\\nThe manifest uses explicit `unknown`, `not_applicable`, or `not_configured` statuses. Missing fields are never silently omitted.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"min-report-rl.run.v0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"run\\\": {\\n    \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"created_at_utc\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"output_dir\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"seed\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n    \\\"global_step_at_emit\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"emitter\\\": {\\n    \\\"package\\\": \\\"trl-min-report-rl\\\",\\n    \\\"version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"mode\\\": \\\"subclass|upstream|monkeypatch\\\",\\n    \\\"missing_policy\\\": \\\"emit_unknown|warn|error\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"framework\\\": {\\n    \\\"trainer\\\": \\\"trl.GRPOTrainer\\\",\\n    \\\"trl_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"transformers_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"accelerate_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"torch_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"python_version\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n    \\\"report_to\\\": [\\\"string\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"model_stack\\\": {\\n    \\\"policy_model\\\": {\\n      \\\"id_or_path\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"revision\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"model_class\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"config_sha256\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"tokenizer_or_processor\\\": {\\n      \\\"id_or_path\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"revision\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"class\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"padding_side\\\": \\\"left|right|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"chat_template_sha256\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"peft\\\": {\\n      \\\"enabled\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"lora|qlora|none|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"rank\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n      \\\"alpha\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n      \\\"dropout\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n      \\\"target_modules\\\": [\\\"string\\\"],\\n      \\\"target_parameters\\\": [\\\"string\\\"],\\n      \\\"bias\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"hardware_precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"num_processes\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n      \\\"distributed_type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"device_names\\\": [\\\"string\\\"],\\n      \\\"bf16\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n      \\\"fp16\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n      \\\"tf32\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"trainer_compute_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16|fp16|fp32|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"sampler_logit_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16|fp16|fp32|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"cast_lm_head_to_fp32\\\": \\\"boolean\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"minimum_reportable_stack\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"algorithm_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n      \\\"loss_type\\\": \\\"grpo|dapo|dr_grpo|bnpo|cispo|sapo|luspo|vespo|custom|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"policy_ratio\\\": {\\n        \\\"uses_importance_ratio\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"importance_sampling_level\\\": \\\"token|sequence|none|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"num_iterations\\\": \\\"integer\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clip\\\": {\\n        \\\"enabled\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"epsilon_low\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"epsilon_high\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"delta\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_importance_sampling_correction\\\": \\\"boolean|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_importance_sampling_mode\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_importance_sampling_clip_min\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_importance_sampling_clip_max\\\": \\\"number|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"token_mask\\\": {\\n        \\\"scope\\\": \\\"completion_only|whole_sequence|custom|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"mask_truncated_completions\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n        \\\"top_entropy_quantile\\\": \\\"number|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"advantage_normalization\\\": {\\n        \\\"scale_rewards\\\": \\\"group|batch|none|true|false|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"multi_objective_aggregation\\\": \\\"sum_then_normalize|normalize_then_sum|custom|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"reward_weights\\\": [\\\"number\\\"]\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"2_reference_policy_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"reference_policy\\\": {\\n        \\\"present\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n        \\\"frozen\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"id_or_path\\\": \\\"string|none|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"sync_ref_model\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n        \\\"ref_model_sync_steps\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n        \\\"ref_model_mixup_alpha\\\": \\\"number|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"kl\\\": {\\n        \\\"enabled\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"loss|reward|absent|custom|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"beta\\\": \\\"number\\\",\\n        \\\"schedule\\\": \\\"constant|custom|none|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_approx|reverse|custom|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"granularity\\\": \\\"per_token|per_sequence|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"use_bias_correction_kl\\\": \\\"boolean\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"3_sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"rollout_engine\\\": \\\"vllm|transformers.generate|custom_rollout_func|managed_api|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"backend\\\": {\\n        \\\"use_vllm\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_mode\\\": \\\"server|colocate|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_model_impl\\\": \\\"vllm|transformers|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_server_base_url\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_tensor_parallel_size\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_gpu_memory_utilization\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"custom_rollout_func\\\": \\\"string|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"decoding\\\": {\\n        \\\"temperature\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"top_p\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"top_k\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n        \\\"min_p\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"max_completion_length\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n        \\\"repetition_penalty\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"generation_kwargs\\\": {}\\n      },\\n      \\\"tokenizer_template_shared\\\": {\\n        \\\"sampler_trainer_same_tokenizer\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_trainer_same_chat_template\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"4_zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n      \\\"enabled\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n      \\\"formula\\\": \\\"zvf = zero_variance_groups / valid_groups; gu = 1 - zvf\\\",\\n      \\\"reward_source\\\": \\\"post_aggregation|per_reward_func|both\\\",\\n      \\\"group_key\\\": \\\"prompt_hash|dataset_index|batch_index\\\",\\n      \\\"reward_equal_atol\\\": \\\"number\\\",\\n      \\\"reward_equal_rtol\\\": \\\"number\\\",\\n      \\\"nan_policy\\\": \\\"exclude_group|zero_variance|error\\\",\\n      \\\"telemetry_path\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"tracker_metric_prefix\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"collapse_rule\\\": {\\n        \\\"window_steps\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n        \\\"zvf_threshold\\\": \\\"number\\\",\\n        \\\"reward_threshold\\\": \\\"number\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"5_group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n      \\\"initial_group_size\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n      \\\"num_generations\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n      \\\"num_generations_eval\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n      \\\"schedule_type\\\": \\\"fixed|adaptive|custom|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"adaptive_rule\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n      \\\"per_step_group_size_logged\\\": \\\"boolean\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"6_heldout_split\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"configured_verified|configured_unverified|not_configured|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"train_dataset\\\": {\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"split\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"fingerprint\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"num_rows\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"heldout_dataset\\\": {\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"split\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"fingerprint\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"num_rows\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reward_environment_disjoint\\\": \\\"boolean|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"evaluator\\\": \\\"string|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence_interval\\\": \\\"bootstrap_95|wald_95|none|unknown\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"7_decontamination_probe\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"passed|failed|not_run|not_configured|unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"ngram_overlap\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"max_overlap\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"threshold\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"passed|failed|not_run|unknown\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"embedding_overlap\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"max_similarity\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"threshold\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"passed|failed|not_run|unknown\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"parser_adversarial_probe\\\": {\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n        \\\"format_only_reward_rate\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"passed|failed|not_run|unknown\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Per-step telemetry schema: `zvf_gu.jsonl`\\n\\nEach line is one JSON object. It is written every optimizer step, even when normal `logging_steps` is larger.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"min-report-rl.telemetry.v0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"split\\\": \\\"train|eval\\\",\\n  \\\"global_step\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n  \\\"optimizer_step\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n  \\\"generation_step\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n  \\\"time_utc\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n  \\\"group_size\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n  \\\"prompt_groups\\\": {\\n    \\\"valid_groups\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"invalid_groups\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"zero_variance_groups\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"usable_groups\\\": \\\"integer\\\",\\n    \\\"all_correct_groups\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\",\\n    \\\"all_incorrect_groups\\\": \\\"integer|null\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n    \\\"zvf\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n    \\\"gu\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n    \\\"reward_mean\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n    \\\"reward_std\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n    \\\"reward_min\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n    \\\"reward_max\\\": \\\"number|null\\\",\\n    \\\"nonfinite_reward_count\\\": \\\"integer\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"reward_source\\\": {\\n    \\\"aggregation\\\": \\\"post_aggregation|per_reward_func|both\\\",\\n    \\\"reward_func_names\\\": [\\\"string\\\"],\\n    \\\"reward_weights\\\": [\\\"number\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"collapse_triage\\\": {\\n    \\\"within_first_window\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n    \\\"collapse_flag\\\": \\\"boolean\\\",\\n    \\\"rule\\\": \\\"zvf >= threshold and reward_mean <= threshold\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"privacy\\\": {\\n    \\\"prompt_text_stored\\\": false,\\n    \\\"prompt_hashes_sha256\\\": [\\\"string\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nTracker scalar keys:\\n\\n```text\\nmin_report_rl/zvf\\nmin_report_rl/gu\\nmin_report_rl/group_size\\nmin_report_rl/zero_variance_groups\\nmin_report_rl/usable_groups\\nmin_report_rl/reward_mean\\nmin_report_rl/reward_std\\nmin_report_rl/collapse_flag\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Minimal upstream patch shape\\n\\n```python\\n# trl/trainer/grpo_config.py\\n@dataclass\\nclass GRPOConfig(...):\\n    report_min_report_rl: bool = True\\n    min_report_rl_log_zvf_gu_every_step: bool = True\\n    min_report_rl_fail_on_missing_required: bool = False\\n    min_report_rl_reward_equal_atol: float = 0.0\\n    min_report_rl_reward_equal_rtol: float = 0.0\\n    min_report_rl_store_prompt_text: bool = False\\n    min_report_rl_heldout_config: str | None = None\\n    min_report_rl_decontamination_config: str | None = None\\n```\\n\\n```python\\n# trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py\\nclass GRPOTrainer(...):\\n    def __init__(..., min_report_rl: MinReportRLConfig | None = None, ...):\\n        ...\\n        if args.report_min_report_rl:\\n            self.min_report_rl_emitter = MinReportRLEmitter.from_trainer(\\n                trainer=self,\\n                config=min_report_rl,\\n            )\\n            self.add_callback(MinReportRLCallback(self.min_report_rl_emitter))\\n        else:\\n            self.min_report_rl_emitter = None\\n\\n    def _after_rewards_aggregated(...):\\n        ...\\n        if self.min_report_rl_emitter is not None:\\n            self.min_report_rl_emitter.log_step(\\n                trainer=self,\\n                split=\\\"train\\\" if self.model.training else \\\"eval\\\",\\n                prompt_ids=prompt_ids,\\n                completion_ids=completion_ids,\\n                rewards=aggregated_rewards.reshape(num_prompts, self.num_generations),\\n                reward_components=raw_rewards_by_func,\\n                group_size=self.num_generations,\\n            )\\n```\\n\\nThat PR is small because TRL already owns the necessary stack fields: `GRPOConfig` records generation settings, vLLM mode, KL coefficient, reward scaling, clipping/loss knobs, and group size; the trainer receives callbacks and reward/rollout functions; and existing `report_to` handles external logging. citeturn965512view1turn351594view0turn295757view2\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Important honesty behavior\\n\\nThe plugin should **not** fake compliance. If a closed sampler does not expose logit precision, the field is emitted as:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"sampler_logit_dtype\\\": \\\"unknown\\\"\\n```\\n\\nIf no held-out split is configured:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"6_heldout_split\\\": {\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\",\\n  \\\"reward_environment_disjoint\\\": \\\"unknown\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf no decontamination probe is provided:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"7_decontamination_probe\\\": {\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWith `min_report_rl_fail_on_missing_required=true`, those become hard errors before the first rollout. That makes the paper’s adoption claim concrete: the default path costs one JSON block and one JSONL telemetry stream; strict mode turns the standard into an auditable gate.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379a63-893c-83ee-87f5-7416e96701c0\",\"title\":\"Threat-Modeling RL Reproducibility\",\"create\":1782028902.585452,\"update\":1782029433.650078,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a threat-modeling framework for reproducibility failures in RL-for-LLM papers. List threat categories, mitigations, and how they map to the 7 checklist items.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"Below is an appendix-ready framework you can add after the Min-Report-RL checklist.\\n\\n## RIFT-RL: a threat model for RL-for-LLM reproducibility\\n\\n**RIFT-RL** stands for **Reproducibility-Integrity Failure Threat model for RL-for-LLM papers**. It treats a paper’s main asset as the **attribution claim**: “method A improves over method B because of the named algorithmic delta.” A reproducibility failure occurs when the observed gain could instead be caused by an unreported or uncontrolled stack difference.\\n\\nThis directly extends the paper’s stack-conditioning thesis: the manuscript argues that an algorithm label like “GRPO” does not define the treatment, because loss form, KL handling, sampler/backend, group schedule, reward parser, evaluation harness, and other stack choices can determine the outcome. The motivating example is a matched-visible-config GRPO comparison with a large backend-driven reward gap, while the draft also notes that these numbers are inherited from a v1 audit and are not independently re-derived in this manuscript. fileciteturn0file0 fileciteturn1file13\\n\\n### Checklist shorthand\\n\\nI’ll use the paper’s seven Min-Report-RL items as controls:\\n\\n| Code | Min-Report-RL item |\\n|---|---|\\n| **C1** | Loss form: ratio, clip, token mask, advantage normalization |\\n| **C2** | Reference policy and KL handling |\\n| **C3** | Sampler, backend, tokenizer/template, decoding, precision |\\n| **C4** | Per-step usable-signal telemetry: ZVF / GU or substitute |\\n| **C5** | Group-size schedule, fixed or adaptive |\\n| **C6** | Held-out split distinct from reward environment |\\n| **C7** | Decontamination and parser/adversarial probes |\\n\\nThe paper’s Table 1 presents these as mandatory because each is a documented lever that can flip a comparison. fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Threat categories, mitigations, and checklist mapping\\n\\n| ID | Threat category | Reproducibility failure mode | Mitigations / controls | Checklist mapping |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **R0** | **Label–stack identity spoofing** | The paper says “GRPO,” “DAPO,” or “GSPO,” but the actual treatment is a full stack: checkpoint, tokenizer, sampler, loss, KL, reward parser, evaluator, and defaults. A method comparison is really a stack comparison. | Report the result as **method + stack**, not method alone. Require a Min-Report-RL JSON block. For head-to-head claims, run a **single-stack survival audit** where only the variant’s defining hook changes. | **C1–C7** |\\n| **R1** | **Objective drift** | Two runs share an algorithm label but optimize different objectives: PPO ratio vs no ratio, different clipping, whole-sequence vs completion-only mask, different advantage normalization, length normalization, or hidden prompt-token loss. | Publish the exact objective equation, masks, normalization scope, clip bounds, and code path. Add token-level loss-attribution tests. Compare variants by patching only one hook on a shared trainer. | **C1**, diagnostic support from **C4** |\\n| **R2** | **Anchor / KL drift** | A result changes because the reference policy, KL placement, KL estimator, or KL schedule differs, not because the claimed RL variant is better. A no-reference runner and a KL-regularized runner are treated as the same algorithm. | Log frozen-reference checkpoint hash, KL location, coefficient, schedule, estimator direction, and per-token/per-sequence aggregation. Report per-step KL statistics. Re-run baselines under the same KL regime. | **C2**, support from **C4** |\\n| **R3** | **Rollout-distribution drift** | Sampler/backend/tokenizer/template/precision differences change the distribution of completions consumed by group-relative RL. The trainer’s apparent learning behavior is caused by rollout plumbing. | Log rollout engine, version, decoding params, tokenizer hash, chat-template hash, max tokens, precision, and whether sampler/trainer share tokenization. Save rendered prompts and sample completions. Run cross-sampler smoke tests. | **C3**, secondary **C4**, **C5** |\\n| **R4** | **Reward-interface exploit** | The reward parser rewards format artifacts, length, delimiter placement, or answer-extraction quirks. Online reward rises without capability improving. | Maintain parser unit tests, adversarial format-only inputs, malformed-answer probes, independent rescoring, and parser-version hashes. Report parser failures separately from model failures. | **C7**, secondary **C6** |\\n| **R5** | **Signal starvation or saturation** | Group-relative learning has little usable signal because groups are all-correct or all-wrong. A run may collapse, saturate, or “win” only because its stack produces more mixed-reward groups. | Log per-step ZVF/GU, reward variance, gradient-norm variance, collapse rate, and time-to-collapse. Apply an early collapse triage rule. Treat final reward without signal telemetry as insufficient. | **C4**, secondary **C5**, **C3** |\\n| **R6** | **Group-schedule / compute-allocation confound** | Adaptive group size, dynamic sampling, retries, or rejection rules spend more samples on hard prompts. The measured gain comes from compute allocation rather than the named algorithmic idea. | Log group size per step and preferably per prompt. Report token budget, rollout budget, rejected samples, retry rules, and adaptive trigger. Compare equal-token and equal-rollout budgets. | **C5**, secondary **C4** |\\n| **R7** | **Evaluation substitution** | Online training reward is reported as capability. Checkpoints are selected by training reward. Held-out improvement vanishes or is not estimated with uncertainty. | Use a pre-specified held-out slice disjoint from training prompts and reward environment. Fix checkpoint-selection rules before scoring. Report sample size, confidence interval, all seeds, and training reward separately from held-out capability. | **C6**, secondary **C4** |\\n| **R8** | **Contamination and memorization** | The benchmark overlaps with training prompts, reward prompts, synthetic data, or evaluation examples. A gain may be memorization or benchmark leakage. | Run n-gram and embedding overlap probes. Deduplicate train/eval prompts. Publish contamination thresholds, exclusions, and train/test hashes. Use fresh held-out slices when possible. | **C7**, secondary **C6** |\\n| **R9** | **Opaque implementation / default drift** | Closed backends, undocumented optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules, precision settings, package versions, or hidden trainer changes make the run non-reconstructable. | Publish an immutable run manifest: commits, package lock, container, hardware, optimizer, adapter config, precision, launcher args. For closed systems, mark unknown fields as opaque and scope the claim as platform-specific. | Primarily **C1–C3**; telemetry via **C4–C5** |\\n| **R10** | **Statistical survivorship** | The paper upgrades a lucky seed, an autocorrelated trace, or a selected checkpoint into an inferential method claim. | Pre-register seed set, survival thresholds, and primary metric. Infer across seeds, not per-step samples. Report bootstrap CIs, standard errors, and multiple-comparison correction. Treat single-seed arms as descriptive. | **C6**, secondary **C4**, audit protocol |\\n\\nThe controlled-audit design proposed in the paper is exactly the high-assurance mitigation for R0–R7: re-implement GRPO-family variants as minimal configuration overrides on one shared trainer, holding the Min-Report-RL block fixed except for the named variant delta. fileciteturn1file11 The paper also recommends seed-level inference, bootstrap confidence intervals, and pre-registered survival tests rather than per-step trace inference. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reverse map: what each checklist item defends against\\n\\n| Checklist item | Threats primarily covered | What the reviewer should require |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **C1. Loss form** | R1 objective drift; R0 label spoofing; R9 hidden defaults | Exact loss equation, token mask, ratio, clip bounds, advantage normalization, length handling, and implementation hook. |\\n| **C2. Reference/KL** | R2 anchor drift; R9 opacity | Frozen reference identity, KL placement, coefficient, schedule, estimator, aggregation level, and per-step KL logs. |\\n| **C3. Sampler/backend/precision** | R3 rollout drift; R9 closed/default drift | Backend, version, precision, tokenizer/template identity, decoding params, sample prompt renderings, and completion/logprob traces. |\\n| **C4. ZVF/GU trajectory** | R5 signal starvation; diagnostic evidence for R1–R3 and R10 | Per-step usable-signal trajectory, collapse/saturation flags, reward variance, and gradient-variance substitute where ZVF is inappropriate. |\\n| **C5. Group-size schedule** | R6 compute allocation; R5 signal availability | Group size at every step, adaptive rule, token/rollout budget, dynamic sampling/rejection rules, and equal-budget comparisons. |\\n| **C6. Held-out split** | R7 evaluation substitution; R4 reward overfitting; R8 contamination; R10 statistical survivorship | Disjoint held-out set, fixed checkpoint rule, sample size, CI, seed-level stats, and separate reporting of online reward vs capability. |\\n| **C7. Decontamination probes** | R8 leakage/memorization; R4 parser exploit | Train/test overlap scan, benchmark contamination report, parser adversarial probes, format-only controls, and answer-extraction tests. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## How to use RIFT-RL in a paper review\\n\\nFor every central claim, create a short **threat card**:\\n\\n> **Claim:** DAPO improves over GRPO on benchmark X.  \\n> **Protected attribution:** improvement is due to clip-higher/dynamic-sampling delta, not stack.  \\n> **Primary threats:** R1, R3, R5, R6, R7.  \\n> **Required controls:** C1, C3, C4, C5, C6.  \\n> **Residual risk:** any unreported closed-backend or parser field downgrades the claim to platform-specific.\\n\\nThen grade each checklist item as:\\n\\n| Status | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Green** | Reported and controlled across arms |\\n| **Yellow** | Reported but not controlled |\\n| **Red** | Missing or ambiguous |\\n| **Black** | Opaque / closed / impossible to inspect |\\n\\nA strong algorithmic claim should have all primary controls **Green**. A claim with Red or Black controls can still be useful, but RIFT-RL forces it to be scoped honestly: “this stack produced this result,” not “this algorithm is better.”\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379a57-a814-83e8-8768-476bfcc2407c\",\"title\":\"GRPO Registry Format Proposal\",\"create\":1782028889.276114,\"update\":1782029571.339611,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a public registry format for GRPO-family variants where each entry records the defining delta plus the full MIN-REPORT-RL block. Provide the JSON schema, an example entry for DAPO, and a query API.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## GRPO Variant Registry, v0.1\\n\\nI’d make the registry’s atomic object **not** “DAPO” or “GSPO” alone, but **a variant identity plus one or more stack reports**. That matches the paper’s central claim: an algorithm label is not a full experimental treatment, because loss form, KL handling, sampler/backend, precision, reward parsing, group schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination can all change the result. fileciteturn0file0 The attached paper also says the controlled audit should hold the full Min-Report-RL block identical across arms except the variant’s defining delta; Table 2 lists DAPO’s defining delta as “clip-higher + dyn. sampling.” fileciteturn1file4\\n\\nThe key registry rule is:\\n\\n> **Every registry entry must contain a `defining_delta` and at least one `report` whose `min_report_rl` object has all seven required fields. Unknown values are allowed, but only as explicit `unknown` / `not_reported` fields, never by omission.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. JSON Schema\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"$schema\\\": \\\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\\\",\\n  \\\"$id\\\": \\\"https://grpo-registry.org/schemas/gvr-entry-v0.1.0.schema.json\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"GRPO-family Variant Registry Entry\\\",\\n  \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n  \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n  \\\"required\\\": [\\n    \\\"schema_version\\\",\\n    \\\"registry_id\\\",\\n    \\\"variant\\\",\\n    \\\"defining_delta\\\",\\n    \\\"reports\\\",\\n    \\\"governance\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"properties\\\": {\\n    \\\"schema_version\\\": {\\n      \\\"const\\\": \\\"gvr.entry.v0.1.0\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"registry_id\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n      \\\"pattern\\\": \\\"^gvr:[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_.-]*$\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"variant\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n      \\\"required\\\": [\\n        \\\"canonical_name\\\",\\n        \\\"family\\\",\\n        \\\"base_algorithm\\\",\\n        \\\"aliases\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"properties\\\": {\\n        \\\"canonical_name\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"family\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n          \\\"examples\\\": [\\\"GRPO-family\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"base_algorithm\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n          \\\"examples\\\": [\\\"GRPO\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"aliases\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n          \\\"items\\\": {\\n            \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n          },\\n          \\\"uniqueItems\\\": true\\n        },\\n        \\\"parents\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n          \\\"items\\\": {\\n            \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \\\"tags\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n          \\\"items\\\": {\\n            \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n          },\\n          \\\"uniqueItems\\\": true\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"defining_delta\\\": {\\n      \\\"$ref\\\": \\\"#/$defs/DefiningDelta\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"reports\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n      \\\"minItems\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"items\\\": {\\n        \\\"$ref\\\": \\\"#/$defs/Report\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"audit_summary\\\": {\\n      \\\"$ref\\\": \\\"#/$defs/AuditSummary\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"governance\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n      \\\"required\\\": [\\n        \\\"entry_status\\\",\\n        \\\"created_at\\\",\\n        \\\"updated_at\\\",\\n        \\\"maintainers\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"properties\\\": {\\n        \\\"entry_status\\\": {\\n          \\\"enum\\\": [\\n            \\\"draft_stub\\\",\\n            \\\"submitted\\\",\\n            \\\"verified\\\",\\n            \\\"disputed\\\",\\n            \\\"superseded\\\",\\n            \\\"retracted\\\"\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"created_at\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n          \\\"format\\\": \\\"date-time\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"updated_at\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\",\\n          \\\"format\\\": \\\"date-time\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"maintainers\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n          \\\"items\\\": {\\n            \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \\\"change_log_uri\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"format\\\": \\\"uri\\\"\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"extensions\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": true\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"$defs\\\": {\\n    \\\"Status\\\": {\\n      \\\"enum\\\": [\\n        \\\"reported\\\",\\n        \\\"partial\\\",\\n        \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n        \\\"unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"not_applicable\\\",\\n        \\\"planned\\\",\\n        \\\"redacted\\\",\\n        \\\"inferred\\\",\\n        \\\"verified\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"Evidence\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n      \\\"required\\\": [\\n        \\\"kind\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"properties\\\": {\\n        \\\"kind\\\": {\\n          \\\"enum\\\": [\\n            \\\"paper\\\",\\n            \\\"code\\\",\\n            \\\"config\\\",\\n            \\\"run_log\\\",\\n            \\\"telemetry\\\",\\n            \\\"artifact\\\",\\n            \\\"maintainer_attestation\\\",\\n            \\\"audit_note\\\"\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"status\\\": {\\n          \\\"$ref\\\": \\\"#/$defs/Status\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"citation_key\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"uri\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"format\\\": \\\"uri\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"locator\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Page, section, table, commit, config path, or artifact path.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"sha256\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"pattern\\\": \\\"^[a-fA-F0-9]{64}$\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"note\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"SourceRef\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n      \\\"required\\\": [\\n        \\\"source_type\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"properties\\\": {\\n        \\\"source_type\\\": {\\n          \\\"enum\\\": [\\n            \\\"paper\\\",\\n            \\\"preprint\\\",\\n            \\\"official_repo\\\",\\n            \\\"third_party_repo\\\",\\n            \\\"controlled_audit\\\",\\n            \\\"library_default\\\",\\n            \\\"registry_stub\\\"\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"status\\\": {\\n          \\\"$ref\\\": \\\"#/$defs/Status\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"title\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"citation_key\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"uri\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"format\\\": \\\"uri\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"version\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"accessed_at\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"format\\\": \\\"date-time\\\"\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"DefiningDelta\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n      \\\"required\\\": [\\n        \\\"base_algorithm\\\",\\n        \\\"summary\\\",\\n        \\\"delta_types\\\",\\n        \\\"hooks\\\",\\n        \\\"target_invariance\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"properties\\\": {\\n        \\\"base_algorithm\\\": {\\n        